(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m2-v20"]={"id":"v20","label":"Version 20 · natural teaching transcript","revision":"ling406-modern-m2-v20-2","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"**Speech perception** is the process of recovering a linguistic message from the sound of speech. Listeners take an acoustic signal that seems like a mess and somehow pull out a message as clear as “I can see you.”","children":["In the acoustic signal for “I can see you,” the energy of each sound runs into the next, so nothing in the signal cleanly separates letters or even words.","Real-time MRI of the **vocal tract** from USC SPAN explains why this happens. The tongue, lips and other articulators move without pause, and the movement for one sound overlaps the movement for the next, so their acoustic effects overlap as well."]},{"text":"Listeners also extract **the same message from very different physical stimuli**. Voices and vocal tract sizes differ, so one word has different acoustic properties from each speaker, yet listeners still hear the same word.","children":["Across **76 speakers**, each of **ten vowels** covers a broad range of first and second formant values instead of a single point.","Many of those ranges **overlap**, so one pair of formant values can be one vowel for one speaker and a different vowel for another speaker."]},{"text":"Recognizing speech means finding a **stable linguistic identity** inside enormous physical variation. Matching a recording against one fixed sound pattern for each word would fail for many ordinary productions, because the message depends on how the sounds function in the language.","children":[]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"The starting premise is that listeners somehow have to **turn a highly variable, analog signal into something more abstract**, so that the language system can operate on it. An **analog signal** is one that varies continuously, so its frequency, loudness and timing can each shift by tiny amounts.","children":["A continuous signal has no built-in markers of where one speech sound stops and the next one starts.","Many small changes leave the speech sound the same, so two physically different productions can still count as the same sound in the language."]},{"text":"The more abstract form is a **symbolic representation**, which identifies a language unit such as a particular consonant or vowel and sets its fine physical detail aside. The unit here is the **phoneme**, which is a sound category that distinguishes words in a language.","children":["/b/ in “bat” and /p/ in “pat” are different phonemes, because swapping them changes the word.","Many slightly different acoustic versions of /b/ all count as one phoneme, so the language system can work with a category that stays constant."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is thought to reflect this kind of **“translation” from physical to symbolic**. In categorical perception, listeners are less sensitive to differences inside a category and more sensitive to equally large differences that cross a category boundary.","children":["A change stands out when it alters the identity the listener gives the sound, and a change of the same physical size can pass almost unnoticed when both sounds remain the same phoneme.","Categorical perception is one account of the translation, and alternative ideas about it exist."]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"The **vocal tract** is the passage that sound travels through in speech, from the larynx through the throat and mouth to the lips. Its shape changes as the tongue, jaw and lips move. Following **Ladefoged (1996)**, the shape of the vocal tract is related in systematic ways to **which resonant frequencies get amplified**.","children":["A **resonant frequency** is a frequency that a space naturally strengthens, so a tube of a given shape boosts some frequencies and weakens others.","Changing the shape of the vocal tract changes which frequencies get the biggest boost, so the sound a listener receives depends partly on that shape."]},{"text":"**Formants** are the resonant frequencies that the vocal tract emphasizes, and every vowel has its own combination of them. Each vowel pairs a vocal tract shape with its own pattern of amplified frequencies.","children":["Across **heed, hid, head and had**, the tongue moves from high and front toward low. Heed has its second formant far above its first, and the two formants draw closer together through hid, head and had.","Across **hod, hawed, hood and who’d**, the tongue moves back and the lips round more. The amplified frequencies gather at the low end, so the first and second formants sit close together.","A vowel is defined by the **relationship among its resonances**, which stays similar across speakers even when the exact frequencies differ."]},{"text":"A **formant transition** is a change in a formant’s frequency over time. It happens because the vocal tract is moving into or out of a sound, so as the mouth moves from a consonant into a vowel, the resonances glide from one set of values to another.","children":["The steady part of a vowel carries information about the vowel, and the moving part carries information about how the neighboring sound is made."]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"A **spectrogram** maps acoustic energy over time and frequency, with time running left to right, frequency running bottom to top, and darker shading marking stronger energy. Each vowel has several dark bands of strong energy, and these bands are its **formants**.","children":["The vowel recordings come from Rob Hagiwara’s samples of English vowels."]},{"text":"The words **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood” and booed** all start with /b/ and end with /d/. The consonants are the same in every word, so the differences between the spectrograms come mainly from the vowels.","children":["**Bead** has a very low first formant and a very high second formant, above 2,000 Hz, so the two bands are far apart.","Through bid, bade, bed and bad, the first formant rises and the second formant falls, so the two move closer together.","In the back rounded vowels such as **bode, “bood” and booed**, the second formant drops to about 1,000 Hz or below and sits near the first formant."]},{"text":"**F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up, and both run through the whole vowel at the same time. The pattern of these two lowest formants carries most of what tells one vowel from another.","children":["Bead pairs a **low F1 with a high F2**, while booed pairs a **low F1 with a low F2**."]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** builds speech sounds by treating them as two parts. The **source** is the sound energy that the voice produces, and the **filter** is the way the vocal tract shapes that energy. Pulling the two apart makes it possible to change one while the other stays fixed.","children":["The work is done in **Praat**, which is free software for speech analysis available at praat.org. Praat can display recordings, measure formants and resynthesize sounds."]},{"text":"The recording is a speaker saying **/u/**, the vowel in “booed.” A **waveform** records changes in air pressure over time, and a **spectrogram** of the same sound breaks it into its frequency components.","children":["The waveform captures timing and loudness, while the spectrogram captures the resonance pattern."]},{"text":"This /u/ has its **first formant at about 300 Hz**, and its **second formant is very low, around 900 Hz**. Both resonances run together through the whole vowel.","children":["Low F1 and low F2 together fit the back rounded quality of /u/.","If changing only the filter changes the vowel, then the vowel’s identity must lie in the filter, which is its pattern of formants. These measured formants become the starting point for building new filters."]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"**Praat can find formants automatically**. It follows each resonance through the recording and marks its frequency moment by moment. In this recording of /u/ it has identified **five formants**.","children":["Several resonances run at the same time, and each one is a frequency component of the same spoken sound.","Automatic tracking measures the resonance pattern without anyone estimating each frequency by eye."]},{"text":"**Only the first three formants are usually important for phonemic processing**. F1, F2 and F3 carry most of the information that separates one phoneme from another, while the higher formants depend more on the individual voice.","children":["The numbers give the order of the resonances from low frequency to high frequency, so F1 is the lowest, F2 is the next and F3 is the third."]},{"text":"This example deals with only **the first two formants**, which sit near 300 Hz and 900 Hz in this /u/. Changing only these two is enough to turn one vowel into another, because they carry most of the vowel information.","children":["F3 and the higher formants stay fixed, so any change that listeners hear comes from F1 or F2 alone."]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"The **source** can be **extracted** from the recorded /u/. Praat builds a model of the filter from the measured formant frequencies, and that model is used to remove the filter’s effect from the recording. What is left is a **more-or-less “unfiltered” version of the voice**.","children":["What remains is the sound energy that the vocal folds supplied.","It is only more or less unfiltered, because the separation is an estimate."]},{"text":"The extracted source **sounds like a buzz**. It keeps its energy and its pitch, but it loses the resonance pattern that made it sound like /u/.","children":["Its energy is spread across many frequencies, without the strong low formants of /u/.","Pitch survives because it comes from the vibration of the vocal folds, and vowel quality disappears because it comes from the filter."]},{"text":"The separation is **a little bit like doing a multiple regression analysis**. In multiple regression, the contribution of one predictor is estimated and removed so that the rest can be examined. Here the contribution of the filter is estimated and removed, and what is left is treated as the source.","children":["With the source isolated, new filters can be applied to it. Keeping one source while swapping filters makes the effect of the resonance pattern testable."]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"Three different **filters** can be applied to the same source. A filter specifies which frequencies get more emphasis and which get less, so every filter is a resonance pattern.","children":["The **first filter** is just the first four formants from the original recording of /u/, so applying it should bring back the original vowel.","The **second filter** raises **the first formant a little** and leaves the other formants roughly where they were.","The **third filter** raises **the second formant a lot** and leaves F1 and the higher formants roughly in place."]},{"text":"Each filter changes one part of the resonance pattern instead of shifting every frequency together. In the original, F1 and F2 are both low, which fits /u/. Raising F1 moves only the lowest resonance up, and raising F2 moves only the second resonance up, by a much larger amount.","children":["Each filter spans 0 to 2.5 seconds in time and 0 to 5,000 Hz in frequency."]},{"text":"The **sound source stays the same** for all three filters, so any change in vowel quality comes from the filter. No new speaker and no new recording are needed.","children":["Comparing the outputs isolates what a change in the lowest resonance does compared with a change in the second resonance."]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"**Multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels**. In source-filter terms, the output sound is the source shaped by the filter. Each filter boosts a different set of frequencies, so each combination comes out as a different vowel.","children":["The source provides the raw sound energy, and the filter decides how much of that energy stays strong in each frequency region."]},{"text":"One source and three filters give three results. The source is identical each time, so the three results differ only in their filters.","children":["With the **original filter**, F1 and F2 stay low and the result keeps the quality of /u/.","With **F1 raised a little**, the lowest resonance rises and the vowel quality changes.","With **F2 raised a lot**, the second resonance rises far and the vowel changes in a different way, because raising F2 moves the back rounded /u/ toward a more front vowel."]},{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** pulls apart two contributions that ordinary speech combines. The voice provides the sound, and the vocal tract resonances shape its quality. All three outputs share the same buzz, the same pitch and the same timing, yet they sound like different vowels.","children":["A researcher can change vowel quality by swapping only the filter, without asking a speaker to record each version."]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"Formant values are only **partly** set by the vowel a speaker intends. They are also shaped by **anatomical features** that differ between speakers of the same language, by **different rates of speech**, and by **context**, such as the phones produced before and after a vowel.","children":["**Anatomy** matters because vocal tracts vary in length and shape, and a longer vocal tract gives lower resonances overall.","**Speaking rate** matters because fast speech gives the articulators less time to reach their targets.","**Context** matters because the tongue and lips are already heading toward the next sound or leaving the previous one. A **phone** is one particular spoken realization of a speech sound."]},{"text":"Across 76 speakers, each of ten vowels covers a wide range of formant values, and those ranges overlap. The same F1 and F2 values can be different vowels for different speakers, so no single pair of frequencies defines a vowel for everyone.","children":[]},{"text":"Formant values can **nonetheless** give a pretty good description of **vowel space**, especially for **a specific talker speaking carefully**. Vowel space is the layout of a language’s vowels according to their formant frequencies. Within one careful talker, the vowels separate far more cleanly than they do across many talkers.","children":["All else being equal, **reasonable approximations of vowels** can be produced **by manipulating steady-state formants**. A **steady-state formant** is the resonance in the stable middle of a vowel, as opposed to the transitions at its edges."]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"The **pattern playback synthesizer** turns a painted spectrogram pattern back into sound. Researchers paint bands on a transparent sheet wherever they want acoustic energy in time and frequency, and the machine plays the pattern aloud. Changing the painting changes the sound.","children":["Researchers could build controlled, speech-like sounds this way instead of relying on natural recordings that vary in many uncontrolled ways."]},{"text":"The machine works by shining light through the painted pattern. The light is first given many frequencies, then filtered by the painting, and then converted to sound.","children":["Light from a **light source** passes through a **cylindrical lens** onto a spinning **tone wheel**, whose rings modulate the light at many frequencies at once.","A lens and a **mirror** send that light onto the painted **spectrogram**, and the painted areas pass along the matching frequencies.","A **light collector** and a **photocell** turn the light into an electrical signal, and an **amplifier** and a **loudspeaker** turn that signal into sound."]},{"text":"A researcher can alter a single formant band or a single transition and hear the result right away. Every other part of the pattern stays exactly the same, so any change in what listeners hear can be traced to the **one painted change**.","children":["Series of patterns that differed in tiny steps revealed how formant transitions signal consonants."]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"**Stop consonants are distinguished by formant transitions**. The stops **/b/, /d/ and /g/** are made by briefly closing the vocal tract and then opening it. This is sensible given how they are produced, because the audible part of a /b/, /d/ or /g/ happens **while the jaw is opening**.","children":["During that opening the vocal tract changes shape quickly. The changing shape makes the formants glide from the consonant’s position toward the vowel’s position, so the consonant’s identity lies in how the formants move."]},{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the **pattern playback synthesizer** to create speech-like sounds that replicated these formant transitions. Each painted pattern had two formants.","children":["The **lower formant** was nearly the same in every stimulus, rising briefly and then staying steady near 600 cycles per second or below.","The **upper formant** started at a different frequency in each stimulus and then settled at the same steady vowel level near 1,800 cycles per second.","In some stimuli the upper formant started low and **rose** into the vowel, in others it was nearly **flat**, and in others it started high and **fell** into the vowel."]},{"text":"The **fourteen patterns** kept the vowel the same and changed only where the upper transition began. A rising transition was heard as **/b/**, a roughly flat one as **/d/** and a falling one as **/g/**.","children":["Only the transition differed, so the responses of listeners could be tied directly to it."]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property. These stimuli are constructed by **keeping F1 constant** and **varying the F2 onset frequency linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13. The **F2 onset frequency** is the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition.","children":["Holding F1 constant removes one possible difference between the stimuli.","Varying the F2 onset **linearly** means that it changes by the same amount every time, so **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series. Early in the series, F2 starts well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises. As the onset climbs, the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat, and later F2 starts above the vowel frequency and falls.","children":[]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum is a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories. Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and others straddle a boundary.","children":["A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size of change.","If perception is categorical, steps within a category will sound alike and steps across a boundary will sound different, so any advantage for boundary pairs comes from the boundary itself."]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond if perception worked according to a particular account. This simple version assumes that perception is **non-categorical**, which means that it tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries.","children":["The model works as a baseline, because real data can be compared against it to see whether perception is more categorical than a gradual account allows."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization would get “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category. The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","children":["Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**. The farther a stimulus is from the /b/ prototype, the less /b/-like and the more /d/-like it sounds.","**Stimulus 2** would be heard as **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. It sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype, so the responses split in that same proportion."]},{"text":"Under this account, **labeling** changes smoothly across stimuli 1 to 11. The /b/ responses fall in a straight slope to 0 at stimulus 6, where /d/ responses peak at 1, and /d/ then falls to 0 at stimulus 10, where /g/ reaches 1.","children":["Two neighboring labels are equally likely, at 0.5, halfway between their prototypes. Every change is gradual, with no sudden jump from one label to the next."]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"The same simple ideal observer can assume that perception is **linear**, which means that equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large. The other important prediction is that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are **“same” or “different.”** These are separate tasks, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a sort of “peaked” function**. There are three categories and listeners are choosing between labels, so the responses for each category rise toward its prototype and fall away from it. These peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination works differently under the linear account. The pairs compared are two steps apart, from stimuli 1 and 3 through stimuli 9 and 11, and every pair has the same physical separation. If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, **discrimination accuracy would also be constant**, at about 0.8 for every pair.","children":["A flat pattern would mean that category boundaries play no role in hearing differences. A peaked pattern would mean that the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","A listener can be unsure which name to choose and still hear that two sounds differ."]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** measured actual labeling and discrimination along the continuum from /b/ through /d/ to /g/. Listeners labeled each stimulus, and they also judged pairs of stimuli that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart. The results departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions, and this pattern is **categorical perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling had **sharp changes** in identification. Stimuli 1 through about 3 were heard as /b/ close to 100% of the time. Around stimuli 4 and 5 the responses switched almost completely to /d/, and around stimuli 9 and 10 they switched just as quickly to /g/.","children":["These switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes that the ideal observer predicted.","The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**. Accuracy peaked at the boundary between /b/ and /d/ and again at the boundary between /d/ and /g/. Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, which is the level expected from guessing.","children":["Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data, on the assumption that listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels. The predictions captured much of the real pattern, and between the peaks the predicted accuracy falls to about 50%, which is chance.","children":["Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity to acoustic differences beyond the category labels alone."]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"Data from students in prior years tested categorical perception along a continuum from **beach** to **peach**. The two words differ in their first consonant, which is /b/ in one and /p/ in the other. Each listener completed an **identification** task, labeling each of stimuli 1 to 9, and a **discrimination** task, judging pairs two steps apart from stimuli 1 and 3 through stimuli 7 and 9.","children":[]},{"text":"For the first listener, identification stays on one word for the early stimuli and then **switches to the other word** around the middle of the continuum. Responses are mixed only in a small region near the switch. The acoustic change is gradual, but the label changes within a narrow region.","children":["That listener discriminates best for the pairs that straddle the identification boundary, which are stimuli 4 and 6 and stimuli 5 and 7.","Pairs well inside one category are hard to tell apart, even though their physical separation is the same.","The pair of stimuli 6 and 8 falls in between, because stimulus 6 itself received mixed labels."]},{"text":"The second listener **switches word labels later** along the continuum and discriminates best near that later boundary. Different people can place their category boundaries at different points.","children":["For each listener, the **best discrimination falls close to that person’s own identification boundary**."]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"The data from prior years support a few general thoughts. **Not everyone has exactly the same category boundary**, so a sound near the boundary between beach and peach for one listener can fall squarely inside a category for another listener.","children":["A pair that straddles one person’s boundary may stay inside one category for someone else.","When individual boundaries differ, averaging across the group can flatten the boundary peak."]},{"text":"**In general, discrimination is best for stimuli perceived as being in different categories**. A listener may miss a physical difference between two versions of the same phoneme. The same size of difference becomes far easier to hear when the two sounds are heard as different words.","children":[]},{"text":"To make the lab doable in a short amount of time, the discrimination experiment included **only “different” trials**. Every pair held two physically different stimuli, so the correct answer on every trial was **“different,”** provided the listener was able to discriminate them.","children":["Answering “same” meant missing a real difference.","Accuracy is the share of pairs heard as different. Someone who always said “different” would score perfectly, so low accuracy on within-category pairs means that listeners often failed to hear differences that were really there."]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"More results from prior years compare a continuum from **bear** to **pear** with the continuum from **beach** to **peach**. For bear and pear, identification switches fairly clearly from one word to the other. Discrimination, however, jumps up and down from one pair to the next.","children":["Accuracy is 0 for the first two pairs and jumps to 1 for stimuli 3 and 5. It then drops back to 0 for stimuli 4 and 6, returns to 1 for stimuli 5 and 7 and falls to 0 for stimuli 6 and 8.","Another listener’s data for beach and peach fluctuate even more. Identification zigzags instead of switching once, and discrimination bounces between high and low values."]},{"text":"The one iron-clad law of experimental research is that **stuff happens**. Responses to a carefully built continuum can still bounce around.","children":["Identification records which word a listener chooses, while discrimination records whether a difference is detected, and these two behaviors can vary in different ways across trials and people.","An isolated rise or drop in discrimination reflects noise in a few responses."]},{"text":"**Can you think of some ways this experiment was maybe not the best, and how would you do it differently?** Only “different” trials were used, so there was no measure of how often listeners said “different” when the sounds were identical. A short session also gave each listener few trials per pair, so a single response could move accuracy a great deal.","children":["The real test is whether better discrimination lines up with the place where identification changes, across the whole continuum."]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"**How would categorical perception help, or what is the advantage of being worse at hearing some differences between sounds?** The advantage is **stable word recognition**. Categorical perception makes listeners worse at hearing differences within a category, which at first seems like throwing away useful detail.","children":["Every production of a phoneme differs physically from every other one, because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds.","If each small acoustic change were heard as a new identity, everyday versions of one word would sound like unrelated sounds."]},{"text":"Reduced sensitivity within a category lets many acoustic versions count as **one phoneme**. Variation inside a category is tolerated because it leaves the word unchanged. A listener who noticed every variation would struggle to recognize that two productions were the same word.","children":[]},{"text":"Differences **between categories** stay easy to hear, so the distinctions that change words are kept. Ignoring small differences within a category frees the listener to focus on the differences that change meaning.","children":["Being worse at detecting some physical changes is compatible with being better at recognizing a stable message."]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be tested even though infants cannot name sounds. Infants react to novelty, so researchers watch for a behavior that changes when an infant notices something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, from Janet Werker’s infant studies lab, an infant learns to turn the head toward a sound when the sound changes. A correct head turn earns an interesting visual reward, such as a moving toy.","children":["After the infant learns this link, a head turn following a change means that the infant detected the change."]},{"text":"The infant results on categorical perception come from **high-amplitude sucking**. The infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a recording device, and strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["As the same sound repeats, the infant gets used to it, which is called **habituation**, and the sucking slows.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change."]},{"text":"Both methods make perception testable before an infant can talk. The experimenter controls exactly which sound difference is presented. A head turn or renewed sucking then reveals sensitivity to that difference without any labeling, even in infants who are months from saying words.","children":[]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, which is the delay between the release of a consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["Adults hear a short VOT as a voiced consonant such as /b/ and a longer VOT as a voiceless consonant such as /p/.","In a syllable with a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing begins much later after the release than in a syllable with a VOT of +10 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Infants heard one synthetic syllable over and over until their sucking slowed, and then the sound either changed or stayed the same. The study had three conditions.","children":["In condition **20D**, the VOT changed from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**, a 20-millisecond change that **crossed** the adult boundary between two phonemic categories.","In condition **20S**, the VOT also changed by **20 milliseconds**, but both sounds came from the **same** adult category, either minus 20 and 0 or +60 and +80 milliseconds.","In condition **0**, the control group kept hearing the same sound."]},{"text":"In every group, sucking rose at first and then dropped to around 30 to 40 responses per minute as the sound became familiar. In 20D, sucking **recovered** sharply after the change, back up near 50. In 20S it kept falling with little recovery, and in the control group it kept declining toward about 15.","children":[]},{"text":"The two changed conditions were matched at 20 milliseconds, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary. Infants noticed a change that adults would hear as a new consonant, but they did not notice an equally large change inside one category.","children":["Even at four months, before they can produce or label speech, infants **perceive voicing categorically**."]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** is the process in which experience tunes perception to the sounds of the surrounding language, while sensitivity to some distinctions from other languages declines. **Werker and Tees (1984)** tested how age and experience affect the ability of infants to discriminate two **non-English contrasts**.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two “ta”-like sounds that English does not use.","The **Salish contrast** is a distinction between “ki” and “qi” in Nthlakampx, a Salish language, which English also does not use."]},{"text":"The measure was the percentage of infants who reached a **discrimination criterion** at each age. English-learning infants were tested at three ages and compared with infants learning Hindi or Salish.","children":["At **6 to 8 months**, most English-learning infants discriminated both contrasts, about 95% for Hindi and about 80% for Salish.","At **8 to 10 months**, fewer succeeded, about 67% for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","At **10 to 12 months**, very few succeeded, near 20% for Hindi and near 10% for Salish.","**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** met the criterion close to 100% of the time for the contrast from their own language."]},{"text":"These results are described as **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**. Young infants can detect these nonnative contrasts, but with experience of English they become much less likely to discriminate them by about their first birthday.","children":["Infants who hear the contrast in their own language keep it, so the decline depends on language experience. Contrasts that the language uses are kept, and contrasts that it lacks become hard to hear."]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"These findings feed **a very compelling narrative** about speech perception, which is built from three linked claims. The first claim is that speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**. On this claim, the mechanism that sorts sounds into categories is specialized for language.","children":[]},{"text":"The second claim is that these abilities are **present very early in development**. The infant experiments with sucking and head turns are taken as evidence that the mechanism is in place long before children talk.","children":[]},{"text":"The third claim is that children **start off as “citizens of the world,”** able to hear **all** contrasts, and then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems to their specific language**. On this claim, development is mostly a matter of losing contrasts the language does not use.","children":["As “citizens of the world,” infants could learn any human language and are ready to hear the distinctions of every one.","Tuning means that the contrasts of the surrounding language stay available while contrasts from other languages fade, as in the Werker and Tees (1984) results."]},{"text":"The narrative covers both the **starting state** and **developmental change**. It proposes broad, language-specific abilities at birth, and it treats development mostly as narrowing from that broad start.","children":["Each early result seems to fit, because infants discriminate categorically before talking, hear contrasts from many languages and lose nonnative contrasts with experience."]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"The compelling narrative meets **some issues**, and three of them concern **categorical perception**. Each one weakens a different part of the narrative.","children":["Categorical perception is **not limited to language**. It also appears with nonspeech sounds, which undercuts the claim that it is special to language.","Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**. Nonhuman animals also perceive speech sounds categorically, which undercuts the claim that it is special to humans.","Categorical perception is also **task-dependent** in its results. Results look more or less categorical depending on what the listener is asked to do, and choosing between labels makes responses more categorical than a task that measures fine acoustic differences."]},{"text":"Another issue concerns **infant speech perception**, which is **not actually “universal.”** Some contrasts are harder for infants to detect than others from early on. Development includes initial differences in sensitivity as well as changes driven by experience, so not all contrasts are equally available at birth.","children":[]},{"text":"Together, these issues weaken the strong claim that every speech distinction starts out equally available in a mechanism devoted only to language. Task dependence also suggests that some categorical patterns come from the way listeners are asked to respond.","children":[]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds**. The sounds varied in **rise time**, which is how quickly a sound reaches its full loudness after it begins.","children":["A sound with a **rapid rise time** sounds **plucked**, like a plucked string.","A sound with a **slow rise time** sounds **bowed**, like a bowed string."]},{"text":"The stimuli included **sawtooth waves**, **sine waves** and **speech syllables**, each made with rise times such as **10 milliseconds** and **70 milliseconds**. With a 10-millisecond rise the sound reaches full strength almost at once, and with a 70-millisecond rise it builds up gradually.","children":["Pluck and bow are labels for two kinds of sound onset, and they are not phonemes."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, listeners labeled short rise times as **pluck** and long rise times as **bow**. Pluck responses started near 100% and fell steeply around 30 to 50 milliseconds, where bow responses rose toward 100%.","children":["Discrimination of pairs that differed by 10 milliseconds stayed around 55 to 60% for most pairs, only a little above chance.","Discrimination **peaked near 75%** for the pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, right where the labels switched."]},{"text":"The link between a label boundary and better discrimination appears for a sound distinction that has nothing to do with language. The physical continuum is gradual, yet the preferred label changes over a narrow region, and discrimination improves there.","children":[]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested whether **infants** have categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**. They used high-amplitude sucking to measure whether a change in sound was detected.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until their sucking declined.","The sound was then shifted, and the change was compared across three conditions."]},{"text":"The three conditions were **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**. In every condition, sucking started near 20 per minute and declined to about 12 before the shift as the sound became familiar.","children":["After **no change**, sucking stayed low, near 12.","After a **within-category** change, sucking also stayed low.","After a **between-category** change, sucking jumped back up to about 18 and then declined again as the new sound became familiar."]},{"text":"Infants responded more strongly to a non-speech change that crossed a category boundary than to a change within a category. This mirrors the pattern that Eimas and colleagues (1971) found for speech.","children":["Infants cannot be drawing on knowledge of words or phonemes when they respond to plucked and bowed sounds. Their categorical response must come from **more general auditory processing**, which could also underlie their categorical response to speech."]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"Categorical perception of speech sounds has also been found in **chinchillas**, by **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and in the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**. The animals were trained with **operant conditioning**, which is a form of learning in which consequences change how often a behavior occurs.","children":["Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-lag** stimulus or the **80-millisecond lag** stimulus was an **SD** for food. **SD** stands for **discriminative stimulus**, which is a signal that a response will be rewarded.","The lag is **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, the chinchillas were tested with intermediate VOTs along the continuum from /ga/ to /ka/, and human listeners were tested on the same sounds. Short VOTs were labeled /ga/ almost every time, and long VOTs almost never. The switch happened over a fairly narrow range, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds, and the chinchilla curves had the **same steep, categorical shape** as the human curves.","children":[]},{"text":"The study also varied the **burst** and **aspiration noise**. The **burst** is the brief noise at the release of a stop consonant, and **aspiration** is the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release.","children":["Making the burst and aspiration louder shifted the boundary toward shorter VOTs, made the switch steeper, or both.","This happened for one chinchilla and for one human listener, so both species responded to these sound properties in a similar way.","Animals without human language can sort a speech continuum into categories, which argues against the view that categorical perception is special to human language."]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"Some speech contrasts are **less salient than others** for infants. A **salient** contrast is one that is easy to notice.","children":["The contrasts tested here differ in **place of articulation**, which is where in the mouth a sound is made.","In **[ma]**, the nasal is made with the lips. In **[na]**, it is made with the tongue near the ridge behind the teeth. In **[ŋa]**, which begins with the sound at the end of “sing,” it is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate."]},{"text":"The study compared the **looking time** of infants on **same** trials, where the syllable stayed the same, and on **change** trials, where it switched. Longer looking on change trials means that the infant noticed the change.","children":["At **4 to 5 months**, English-hearing infants looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials for the native contrast between **[ma] and [na]**.","For the nonnative contrast between **[na] and [ŋa]**, the same infants looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials. They did not detect this change, and English never uses [ŋ] at the start of a syllable."]},{"text":"**Filipino-learning infants** hear the contrast between [na] and [ŋa] in their own language. At **6 to 8 months**, they looked about 3.8 seconds on both kinds of trials, so they did not yet detect the change. By **10 to 12 months**, looking rose from about 3.3 seconds on same trials to about 4.8 seconds on change trials.","children":["Some contrasts start out hard for infants to hear, even when they belong to the native language. Experience with the language **strengthens** sensitivity to such contrasts over the first year, so development includes gains as well as losses."]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"**Klatt (1979)** described a very old and very general model of speech perception, in which recognition runs through a **series of stages**. Each stage transforms the output of the stage before it. The **speech waveform** enters the **peripheral auditory system**, where the **cochlea** in the inner ear carries out a **spectral analysis** that splits the sound into its frequency components.","children":["**Acoustic property detectors** respond to particular properties of the sound, and **phonetic feature detectors** use their outputs to estimate the probability of features such as voicing at each moment.","**Segmental analysis** combines the features into a sequence of speech segments called a **segmental feature matrix**. **Lexical search** then compares that sequence with stored words and produces **lexical hypotheses**, which are candidate words that might match."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** fits this model. Somewhere between the **analog signal** that comes in through the cochlea and the **activation of phonemes** in segmental analysis, **information is lost**, so many physically different sounds become the same phoneme. This **loss of information is helpful**, because a shared phoneme sequence can make contact with higher levels of representation and be matched against stored words.","children":["The assumption that follows is that **in order to identify words, we need to first identify their constituent phonemes**."]},{"text":"There are **two problems** for this simple view. The first is **cascaded processing**, in which partial information moves on to the next level before the earlier decision is complete. The second is **feedback**, in which a higher level such as words sends influence back down to a lower level such as phonemes.","children":[]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"The **beach-peach continuum** is a familiar **voicing continuum**. The two words differ in their first consonant, which is /b/ in beach and /p/ in peach. These consonants differ mainly in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, which is the delay between the release of the lips and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["The continuum runs from “peach” to “beach” as VOT gets shorter in equal steps. A **long delay** before voicing sounds like **peach**, and a **short delay** sounds like **beach**."]},{"text":"One listener's **identification** results have a **sharp, step-like shape**. Stimuli 1 through 5 are labeled as one word every time, and stimuli 7 through 9 are labeled as the other word every time. Only stimulus 6 gets mixed labels, with a proportion near 0.33.","children":["The **category boundary** is the narrow region where the preferred label switches."]},{"text":"The same listener's **discrimination** results follow the boundary. Pairs inside one category are almost never heard as different, so accuracy is near 0 for them. Accuracy jumps to 1 for the pair 4 and 6 and for the pair 5 and 7, because those pairs **straddle the boundary**.","children":["The pair 6 and 8 is in between, near 0.33, and accuracy falls back to 0 for the pair 7 and 9.","Equal changes in VOT are much **easier to detect across the boundary**, where they change the word, than inside one category, where the word stays the same."]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"**How does categorical perception happen?** One answer is a **threshold model**, in which **activity is only passed forward from one level to the next when an activity threshold is crossed**. A **threshold** is a set level of activity that must be reached before anything is sent onward. Activity below the threshold can rise and fall without any effect on the next level.","children":["In this kind of model, **categorical perception arises at phoneme identification**."]},{"text":"Consider the voicing evidence for a sound between /b/ and /p/. At the **feature level**, the evidence swings up and down over time between a **+voiced** threshold and a **-voiced** threshold. At the **phoneme level**, nothing happens while the evidence wavers below threshold, so both /b/ and /p/ stay silent. Only when the evidence finally crosses the +voiced threshold does **/b/** switch on, **all at once**.","children":[]},{"text":"An ambiguous sound that gets mixed responses, such as the one near the middle of the beach-peach continuum, either crosses a threshold or does not. The word level receives a finished /b/ or a finished /p/, and the history of uncertainty stays behind at the feature level.","children":["Two acoustically different sounds that both end up as /b/ become **identical** by the time they reach word recognition, because the differences that existed before the threshold are discarded.","This **all-or-none switch** would produce categorical perception directly at the phoneme level."]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"A **cascaded model** gives a different answer to how categorical perception happens. In a cascaded model, **activity is passed forward continuously over time from one level to the next**. Each level responds to whatever activity exists at the moment, while the decision is still forming.","children":["Feature activity and phoneme activity rise and fall together. As evidence for +voiced grows, **/b/** activation grows, and as the evidence shifts toward -voiced, activation moves toward **/p/**."]},{"text":"In this kind of model, **categorical perception might happen later on, or might even be an artifact of the task structure**. An **artifact** is a result created by the way something is measured and not by the process under study. An identification task forces a listener to click beach or peach, even if both words were partly active. The answer looks categorical because the **response choices are categorical**.","children":["Two sounds that both end up labeled /b/ can still differ in how much they supported /p/ along the way."]},{"text":"The cascaded model lets **uncertainty flow forward**. When the voicing evidence is mixed, /b/ and /p/ each get activation in proportion to their evidence. Graded phoneme activation passes graded activation to words, so a sound near the boundary **partly activates both beach and peach**.","children":["The processing before the final choice keeps fine-grained information about how ambiguous the sound was."]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"**Making furniture** is a metaphor for **thresholded activity**. Each operation takes one specific input and turns it into one specific output. A **lumber mill** takes **trees** as input and produces **lumber** as output.","children":["A **furniture factory** takes **lumber** as input and produces **unassembled furniture** as output.","An **IKEA customer** takes **unassembled furniture** as input and produces finished **furniture** as output."]},{"text":"Each step needs a **finished product** in a form it can use. Trees must become lumber before any furniture is made, and lumber must become furniture parts before any assembly begins. No part of the work at one step passes forward until the whole job is done.","children":[]},{"text":"**Thresholded processing** depends on its inputs in the same way. A higher level **waits** until the lower level delivers the required representation, so information moves forward only as a completed product.","children":["The property the furniture chain captures is that each stage waits for a finished product from the stage before it."]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"The furniture metaphor says that each **“layer”** of the system **can only take a specific input that has been transformed completely by the previous layer**. Manufacturing has to wait for the shipment of lumber before it can start. **Many computer programs** function this way, because each step finishes before the next one starts.","children":["Each stage does its own transformation and hands on only its finished output. A lower stage can keep working while the higher stage sits idle and waits."]},{"text":"In speech recognition, a strict threshold account makes **word processing depend on an identified phoneme**. A partly resolved acoustic signal stays at the lower level until it is identified as a phoneme. The word level receives only the **finished segment**.","children":[]},{"text":"This design also decides **what information is lost**. When different sounds are converted into the same phoneme, their earlier differences never reach word recognition. That is useful for treating varied sounds alike, but it has a cost.","children":["The cost is that later stages never learn **how uncertain** the earlier stages were, because that uncertainty was never passed forward."]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"A **food web** is a metaphor for **cascaded processing**, because its **“levels” continuously interact with one another**. A desert food web links **desert plants**, **insects**, **kangaroo rats**, **small lizards**, **large lizards**, **scorpions**, **snakes**, **desert foxes** and **hawks** by who eats whom. A drought that reduces the number of plants **immediately** affects the health of kangaroo rats. That change then affects the snakes that eat the kangaroo rats, and the effect continues up the web.","children":["No population waits for the level below it to reach a finished state before it is affected."]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** has the same continuous dependence. Shifting feature evidence changes phoneme activity as it happens, and shifting phoneme activity changes the support for words. Each level reacts to **partial information** as it arrives from the level below.","children":[]},{"text":"The food web also has clear **feedback loops**. **What impact does adding more hawks have on populations of snakes, lizards and rats?** More hawks reduce the populations they eat, and those changes in turn affect the hawks' own food supply.","children":["In a recognition model, **feedback** is influence that travels from a higher level back down to a lower level.","Cascading and feedback are different ideas, because cascading sends developing evidence **forward** and feedback lets higher-level activity change an **earlier** representation."]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"**Is phoneme identification for word recognition more like thresholded processing or cascaded processing?** Thresholded processing is the lumber mill, and cascaded processing is the food web. The eye-tracking evidence from McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003) favors **cascaded processing**, but first the two accounts have to be told apart. In a **thresholded account**, word recognition starts only once a phoneme decision is complete. In a **cascaded account**, a word can gather support while the phoneme is still uncertain.","children":[]},{"text":"The two accounts differ in how much developing acoustic evidence reaches candidate words. Two sounds can both be labeled **/b/** and still differ in how much they resemble **/p/**. A thresholded system turns both into the same finished /b/, so **pear should not compete with bear**. A cascaded system passes along more /p/ support for the more ambiguous sound, so **pear should compete with bear for a while**.","children":["Both accounts can predict the same final word, because they differ only in what happens before the final choice.","The deciding test therefore measures **temporary support for a competing word** during recognition."]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** asked whether phoneme identification for word recognition is thresholded or cascaded. Their study looked for **gradient effects of within-category VOT on lexical access**. **Lexical access** is retrieving a word from memory during recognition.","children":["**Gradient** means changing by degrees. **Within-category VOT** means differences in voice onset time among sounds that are all heard as the same phoneme."]},{"text":"Listeners identified sounds from a **/b/-/p/ continuum** in a **“visual world” display** while **eye-tracking** recorded where they looked among the pictures. Each display had a **target** such as **pear**, a **competitor** such as **bear**, and two unrelated **fillers**, which were a **lamp** and a **ship**. The target and the competitor differ only in their first consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"Eye movements suit this question because they are **fast and continuous** during word recognition. Listeners look at objects as they think about them, often before they know they have decided. A listener may end up choosing bear after a quick look at the pear.","children":["A look at the pear while hearing a /b/-like sound means that pear was being considered.","How often and when such looks happen measures **how active the competitor was** before the final click."]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"In the study by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)**, participants used the **mouse to select** whichever of the pictures was most consistent with what they heard, such as a **“bear”** or a **“pear.”** Each trial presented one sound from the VOT continuum along with the four pictures. The click records which word the listener finally settled on.","children":[]},{"text":"The clicks produced a **typical, step-like identification function**. For VOTs from 0 to about 15 milliseconds, /p/ responses stayed near 0, so listeners almost always clicked bear. From about 20 milliseconds onward, /p/ responses stayed near 1, so listeners almost always clicked pear.","children":["The switch happened abruptly at a **category boundary of 17.5 milliseconds**, plus or minus 0.83 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Judged by the clicks alone, perception looks **fully categorical**. A sound well inside the /b/ category and a sound just short of the boundary both produce bear nearly every time. On its own, this sharp shift would seem to support the **threshold model**.","children":["A click records only the word chosen at the end of recognition, while eye movements record which other words received support before that choice.","A sharp identification function can therefore exist alongside **gradual differences in competition** among sounds with the same final label."]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"**Eye movements reveal that ambiguity is gradient**, because there are **more looks to the competitor close to the boundary**. Gradient ambiguity means that uncertainty varies by degree and does not switch all at once. The analysis holds the final response constant, so it compares only trials where the listener clicked bear, or only trials where the listener clicked pear.","children":["Every trial in a comparison ended with the same click, so differences in competitor looks cannot come from listeners changing their answer."]},{"text":"On trials where listeners clicked **bear**, they still looked at the **pear** for a while. The VOTs on these trials were 0, 5, 10 and 15 milliseconds. Looks to the pear rose after the word began, peaked around 400 to 600 milliseconds, and then declined as bear won.","children":["Sounds closer to the boundary, such as 15 milliseconds, drew **more looks** to the pear, and the looks **lasted longer**, than the sound at 0 milliseconds."]},{"text":"On trials where listeners clicked **pear**, the pattern was the mirror image. The VOTs on these trials were 20, 25, 30, 35 and 40 milliseconds. The sound at 20 milliseconds, just past the boundary, drew the most looks to the bear, and the clearest /p/ at 40 milliseconds drew the fewest.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect of VOT is mainly on the **duration of activation**, meaning how long the competitor stays active. Two sounds that both end up as bear can still produce different amounts of looking at the pear.","children":["The recognition system keeps track of how ambiguous each sound was, which is information a **threshold model would throw away**."]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"The gradient effect is clearer when looks to the competitor are compared across the **whole VOT range**, from 0 to 40 milliseconds. On one side of the **category boundary**, listeners clicked bear, and the measure is how often they **looked at the pear**. On the other side, listeners clicked pear, and the measure is how often they **looked at the bear**.","children":["Looks were measured in two time windows. The **early** window covers **300 to 1100 milliseconds** after the word begins, and the **late** window covers **1100 to 1900 milliseconds**."]},{"text":"In both windows, competitor looks **increase as VOT approaches the boundary from either side**. On the bear side, late looks to the pear rise from about 0.02 at 0 milliseconds to about 0.045 near 15 milliseconds. On the pear side, late looks to the bear are highest near 20 milliseconds, at around 0.04, and fall to about 0.01 by 40 milliseconds.","children":["Early looks are higher overall, but they follow the same pattern of peaking near the boundary.","The rise is **gradual** and not sudden on both sides."]},{"text":"The effect appears in both windows, so uncertainty about the first sound shapes looking **throughout recognition** and not only in a brief first moment. A sound can support the eventual winner while still supporting its rival. The closer the sound is to the boundary, the stronger that rival support is.","children":[]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"The eye-movement finding is consistent with the idea that **activation flows in a cascaded way from features to phonemes**. Changing features affect phonemes, and changing phonemes affect words, while recognition is still under way. **Even stimuli that are identified as /b/ 100% of the time can vary in how strongly and unambiguously the “voiced” feature is activated**.","children":["A clear /b/ activates voicing strongly, while a /b/ near the boundary activates it only weakly."]},{"text":"**/b/ and /p/** share most of their features. Both are **bilabial**, which means the two lips make the closure, and both are **stops**, which means the airflow is fully blocked and then released. A stimulus that is obviously **+bilabial** and **+stop** supports /b/ and /p/ equally. Only voicing tells them apart, so weak voicing evidence creates **momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/**.","children":["That ambiguity is reflected in the **eye movements** while the participant decides where to click."]},{"text":"The **TRACE** model captures this cascade with three layers. The **feature layer** takes a **pseudo-spectral input representation**, which is a simplified version of the frequency content of the sound over time. The **phoneme layer** has units for phonemes such as /b/, /p/, /r/ and /t/, and the **word layer** has units for words.","children":["The feature detectors are POW, VOC, DIFF, ACU, GRD, VOI and BUR, which stand for power, vocalic, diffuseness, acuteness, gradualness of onset, voicing and burst.","The phoneme units and word units are repeated at every point in time. Activity passes up from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words, and units within a layer compete through **inhibition**."]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"There is evidence that **partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level**, which is the level of stored words and their meanings. **Andruski and colleagues (1994)** found that **semantic priming** can happen even with **nonwords**. Semantic priming is the speed-up in recognizing a word after hearing a word related in meaning.","children":["**“Beace”** primes **“war,”** because beace is peace with a voiced first consonant and peace is related in meaning to war. **“Ging”** primes **“queen,”** because ging is king with a voiced first consonant.","In the **word-to-word** items, changing the voicing of the first consonant of one real word produces another real word, as in **“pet”** becoming **“bet.”** One such item is **“bill”** priming **“drug,”** because bill is an altered form of pill."]},{"text":"Participants did a **lexical decision task**, in which they decide as quickly as possible whether a target is a real word. The primes were **unaltered**, had their VOT reduced by **one third**, had it reduced by **two thirds**, or were **unrelated** to the target. Faster responses than after an unrelated prime indicate priming.","children":[]},{"text":"Every related prime **sped up responses** compared with unrelated primes. Unrelated primes gave the slowest responses, near 950 to 970 milliseconds, and unaltered primes gave responses around 810 to 860 milliseconds. With one third of the VOT removed, responses stayed fast, around 825 to 855 milliseconds.","children":["With two thirds removed, word-to-nonword items still stayed fast, near 820 milliseconds, while word-to-word items slowed to about 890 milliseconds.","The word-to-word items slowed because the altered prime also partly activates a competing real word, such as bill competing with pill, which reduces the benefit."]},{"text":"This result means that **lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete or imperfect input**. Beace never becomes a real word, but it matches peace closely enough to partly activate it. That activation spreads to meaning and speeds recognition of war, which fits **cascaded processing**.","children":[]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing** in speech perception. A thresholded model would have a hard time explaining why **the number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**. A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in a single sound, such as bear and pear.","children":["Looks to the competitor climb steadily as VOT nears the boundary. This happens even when the final answer is the same, so it reflects **graded activation within one category**."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity **never reaches the phoneme level**. Feature evidence can swing between +voiced and -voiced, but the phoneme level stays silent until /b/ is chosen all at once. The phoneme level therefore cannot know how close a sound came to the boundary.","children":["Every token placed in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level. Word-level competition would then be identical for every /b/, so nothing could produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"A **cascaded account** explains the effect directly. More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word, so sounds nearer the boundary draw more looks to the competitor.","children":[]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. Activity runs up from phonemes to words and also back down from words to phonemes. **Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","children":["An uncertain sound can partly activate a word, and that word can then strengthen the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"Influence therefore travels **in both directions**. Acoustic evidence changes which words are active, and active words shift the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming. This helps listeners cope with noisy or ambiguous input.","children":[]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**. Cascading sends incomplete evidence **forward**, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words. Feedback sends influence from words **back down** to phonemes.","children":["An interactive model can have both cascading and feedback.","The reason to add feedback comes from experiments in which **word knowledge changes how sounds are heard**, such as the **Ganong effect**."]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**The Ganong effect** comes from **Ganong (1980)**, who looked at how **phoneme identification is affected by word knowledge**. He tested whether knowing which sound sequences are real words changes how an ambiguous sound is heard. The phrase “Gisses and kifts for Valentine’s day” plays on the effect, because it swaps the voicing of the first consonants in “kisses and gifts.”","children":[]},{"text":"Ganong came up with **word/nonword pairs** such as **“corpse/gorpse,”** with a real word at one end of the continuum and a nonword at the other. Hearing /k/ gives the familiar word corpse, and hearing /g/ gives gorpse, which is not a word. He produced continua that spanned the relevant phoneme boundary in **5 millisecond steps** of VOT.","children":["The rest of the word, “orpse,” is the same at every step, and it tells the listener which beginning would complete a real word."]},{"text":"The design pairs **uncertain phonetic evidence** with a **difference in lexical status**. If word knowledge affects identification, an ambiguous first consonant should more often be heard as the **sound that makes a word**.","children":["The crucial test is in the **ambiguous middle** of the continuum, because that is where word knowledge has the most room to act."]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"One prediction for the experiment of **Ganong (1980)** comes from a **late “correction” process**. In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual, but there is a bias to report having heard a word**. The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge. At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a real word.","children":[]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias**. Three kinds of continua are compared, measured by the proportion of voiced responses from short VOT to long VOT. In **word-nonword** continua the voiced end forms a word, in **nonword-word** continua the voiceless end forms a word, and in **neutral** continua neither end has a lexical advantage.","children":["A bias added after perception should push responses toward the word by the **same amount everywhere**.","The word-nonword responses would be shifted up even at the long VOT end, staying near 0.25 and not falling to 0. The nonword-word responses would be shifted down at the short VOT end, starting near 0.75 and not at 1."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions at each step. Under late correction, the lexical effect is a **flat line near 0.25** across the whole continuum.","children":["The bias favors the word equally at clear steps and at ambiguous steps, because perception and word knowledge stay separate and word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"A second possibility is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**. In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other. Near an ambiguous onset, the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support.","children":["The effect changes **categorization itself**, and not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ **only in the middle** of the continuum. The word-nonword, neutral and nonword-word responses all start near 1 at short VOT and end near 0 at long VOT, because the sound is clear at both ends. Near the boundary, the word-nonword condition crosses later and the nonword-word condition crosses earlier.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** should therefore form a **peak**. It is near 0 at the endpoints, rises to about 0.35 for the items closest to the boundary, and falls again. The effect is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**, because those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve some ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","children":["At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant, so there is little room for word knowledge to help.","A **flat** lexical effect points to a reporting bias, while a **peaked** lexical effect points to a shift in the boundary itself."]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"The results of **Ganong (1980)** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, which is the **boundary-shift** account. The proportion of voiced responses in the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions starts near 1 at short VOT and falls toward 0 at long VOT. The two conditions separate most in the middle, near the boundary.","children":["The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary and then falls back."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary **rules out a simple reporting bias**, because word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest. At the boundary, the word that fits the context tips the balance. At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence decides the answer alone.","children":[]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen, and he focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**. A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when they were unsure of the sound. Later models would emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","The whole-word representation can then shape how an ambiguous part of the word is interpreted during recognition."]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme-level and word-level activations** at four moments as the input unfolds. The input begins with a sound that is **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**. It is followed by **/l/**, the vowel **/ʌ/** and **/g/**, which together make the word **plug**.","children":["In the **first frame**, only the ambiguous first sound has arrived. **/b/** and **/p/** are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2, and word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"In the **second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive. Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl, and partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl. Several candidates gain activation together, including **plug**, **blood** and **blush**, because all of them fit the beginning heard so far.","children":["Phoneme activity for /p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"In the **fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives. The /g/ fits plug but does not fit the endings of blood or blush. **Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, while the alternatives fall away.","children":["**/p/ ends up more active than /b/** at the phoneme level."]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"As the input unfolds over time, **activity accumulates for “plug,”** especially when the **final /g/** arrives. The /g/ is highly inconsistent with **“blood”** and **“blush,”** which were also activated in the third frame. Blood and blush matched the early part of the input, so they were plausible candidates for a while.","children":["Once /g/ arrives, blood and blush lose support because their endings do not fit."]},{"text":"The TRACE model therefore explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**. Top-down processing means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis. **Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**, and the feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","children":["Plug is the winning word, so /p/ receives extra support from it."]},{"text":"The ambiguous beginning becomes clearer because /p/ belongs to the word that the whole sequence supports. Information that arrives at the **end of a word** can reach back and shape how an **earlier sound** is heard.","children":["This happens through interactions during recognition, and the ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/."]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model is a version of the **Interactive Activation model** for **written word recognition**, from **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**. Many of the principles are the same in both models. In the Interactive Activation network, **feature** units connect to **letter** units, and letter units connect to **word** units such as **WEAR, WORK, WORD** and **WEAK**.","children":["Compatible units at different levels support each other, and competing units within a level inhibit each other.","Active words send support back down to their own letters."]},{"text":"Suppose a reader sees **W, O** and **R** clearly, while ink blots hide part of the final letter. The visible parts of that letter fit both **R** and **K**. W, O and R activate words such as WORK and WORD, but only WORK fits the visible features of the final letter, so WORK becomes the strongest word.","children":["WORK sends support back to **K**, so the ambiguous letter is perceived as K. This is the same way plug supports /p/ in speech."]},{"text":"A key issue in speech perception is that **the stimuli unfold over time**. A written word is all there at once, while a spoken word arrives one sound after another. TRACE handles this by repeating feature, phoneme and word units at **every point in time**.","children":["A candidate word can match the beginning, gain activation and then fade when the ending conflicts."]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** means that perception is shaped by **category boundaries**. **Given the same physical difference, it is more difficult to distinguish stimuli from the same category than from different categories**. Nothing about cascading or feedback undermines that basic finding.","children":["Equal acoustic steps have unequal perceptual effects, depending on whether they cross a boundary."]},{"text":"Two findings add to it at the **lexical-phonetic interface**, where words and sounds meet. First, **ambiguity about phoneme identity does have an impact on word recognition**. Sounds with the same label differ in how strongly they activate a competing word, so labels can be categorical while the processing behind them is graded. Second, **word-level information seems to have an impact on phoneme categorization**.","children":["In the Ganong effect, word knowledge moves the category boundary, and in TRACE that happens through lexical feedback to the phoneme level."]},{"text":"It therefore **cannot be the case that the word recognition system just sits there waiting for the phoneme recognition system to finish doing its job**, like a lumber mill. **Spoken word recognition is more interactive** than that, and it works more like the food web.","children":["Partial information moves forward and active words send support back down, all while uncertainty is still being resolved."]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"**Spoken word recognition is also very fast**. **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** used a visual world eye-tracking task to measure how quickly candidate words become active. Participants saw a grid of pictures and geometric shapes and followed spoken instructions to move objects.","children":["The display included a **beaker**, which was the **referent** or target, along with a **beetle**, a **speaker** and a **carriage**."]},{"text":"The pictures were chosen for their sound overlap with the word **beaker**. **Beetle** is a **cohort competitor** because it shares the beginning of the word. **Speaker** is a **rhyme competitor** because it shares the ending of the word. **Carriage** is **unrelated** and shares no sounds with beaker.","children":[]},{"text":"The measure was the probability of fixating each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the target word began. The spoken word ended a little before 400 milliseconds on average. Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, starting around 200 milliseconds, because both fit the first sounds.","children":["Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds and then fell once the sounds that separate the two words arrived.","Looks to the beaker kept climbing, passing 0.8 by about 800 milliseconds and approaching 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, peaking near 0.1, and looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"Looks to the target started rising **before the word had finished**, so recognition begins while the word is still unfolding. Words that match the input at a given moment become active, and they drop out as soon as they stop matching.","children":[]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"The eye-tracking data from the beaker task can be compared with a **TRACE simulation** of the same task. The word activations of the model over time were converted into predicted fixation probabilities and compared with the real fixations over 1,000 milliseconds after the target began. The TRACE predictions **closely match** the eye movements.","children":["For the **referent**, the predicted and real looks both rise steadily from about 300 milliseconds and approach 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","For the **cohort** competitor, both rise early, peak near 0.17 to 0.2 around 500 milliseconds and then fall.","For the **rhyme** competitor, both rise later and stay small, peaking near 0.1."]},{"text":"The **granularity** of the simulation is fine, which means it predicts activity moment by moment and not only the final choice. **Eye movements give us an idea about the changes in “activity” for different competitors over time**. That makes it possible to **see cognitive processing before a final response is issued**.","children":[]},{"text":"This parallels the **graded effects of category goodness** in the categorical perception task. Sounds that are better or worse examples of a category produce different amounts of competitor activity, even when the final label is the same.","children":["The developing pattern of looks reveals which words became active along the way and when each one lost out."]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** is a grammatical English sentence, even though it usually feels broken the first time you read it. The trouble comes from the opening words, because they push the reader toward the wrong structure.","children":["Most readers take **raced** as the **main verb**, so “the horse raced past the barn” seems to be a complete event in which the horse did the racing.","When **fell** arrives, that structure has no room left for it, so the sentence seems to fall apart."]},{"text":"**What events are described in this sentence?** The sentence describes **two events**. Someone raced the horse past the barn, and then the horse fell.","children":["In this reading, “raced past the barn” tells which horse is meant, and **fell** is the main verb that says what happened to that horse."]},{"text":"**How could you disambiguate it?** You can **disambiguate** it, which means making only one reading possible, by putting back the missing words, as in **“The horse that was raced past the barn fell.”** The words “that was” make the modifying relationship explicit, so raced can no longer be read as the main verb.","children":["Understanding a sentence depends on working out the relationships among its words, and knowing what each word means is not enough."]},{"text":"**Could this be the sentence that launched a thousand experiments?** It fairly could be, because researchers have used it again and again to study how readers build structure as the words come in.","children":[]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** is almost as famous as **“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously.”** The two sentences are famous for different reasons. The colorless green ideas sentence is **grammatical but meaningless**. The horse sentence is grammatical and meaningful, but it is **very hard to process**.","children":["A comic strip jokes that nobody would ever say the horse sentence, and the reply is that farmers might say it if their horses kept falling over. The joke only works because the sentence really is grammatical, even though it sounds wrong."]},{"text":"The horse sentence is the classic case of the **“garden path” effect**. A **garden path sentence** steers the reader into one reading early on, and later words reveal that this reading was wrong. The name comes from the saying “to lead someone down the garden path,” which means to mislead someone.","children":["Raced looks like the main verb, and fell then clashes with the structure already built, so the reader has to go back and treat “raced past the barn” as “that was raced past the barn.”","Garden paths happen because readers build structure word by word and commit to one analysis before the sentence is over. A reader who waited until the end of the sentence would never be led astray."]},{"text":"**Grammaticality and processing difficulty are different things**. A sentence can have a perfectly acceptable structure and still be very hard to follow as it unfolds, because the difficulty depends on which analysis the reader picks first and how hard that analysis is to replace.","children":[]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start with a **parser**, which is a mental process whose job is to build a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read. A syntactic tree records how the words group into phrases and how those phrases relate to each other.","children":["The trees for the horse sentence and the dinosaur comic both come from a Language Log piece about this sentence."]},{"text":"In the **correct tree**, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**, that holds “the horse” plus a reduced clause. The reduced clause means “that was raced past the barn,” and the words “that” and “was” are silent in the actual sentence. The clause also has an empty subject position. Inside the clause, raced takes the prepositional phrase, or **PP**, “past the barn,” so the main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**.","children":["In the **failed parse**, “the horse” is an ordinary NP and “raced past the barn” is the main VP, with raced as the main verb. **Fell** is then left with nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"Any parser has to work inside a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**. Words arrive quickly, one after another, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence before it starts building structure. It can also keep only a limited number of structures in play at once.","children":["An account of processing has to explain how the parser copes with these two limits when a sentence is ambiguous."]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is **much more complex syntactically** than the failed parse, because it has **many more nodes**. A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":["The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was.”","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"One strategy that saves space is to **initially prefer smaller trees**, and this strategy is called **“minimal attachment.”** Under minimal attachment, the parser fits each new word into the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes. Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment.","children":[]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**, because that analysis needs the fewest nodes. The reading in which “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause, so the parser passes it over at first.","children":["The smaller structure lightens the immediate load, but it leads the reader down the garden path. When fell arrives, it requires the larger structure that was passed over, so the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework. It is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**, so new words get attached to the phrase that is being built right now.","children":["Keeping a clause open means that the parser keeps adding incoming words to it, and does not close it off and start a new structure."]},{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** the phrase **“with the dynamite”** wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** that starts with **“blew open.”** **Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for this sentence.","children":["In the **preferred** structure, the PP attaches to the **VP**. This **verb-phrase attachment** makes the dynamite the **instrument** that the burglar used to blow open the safe.","In the **unpreferred** structure, the PP attaches to the **NP** “the safe.” This **noun-phrase attachment** makes the phrase say which safe was blown open, namely the safe that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they say different things. Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the new phrase joins it. Minimal attachment prefers the same attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than building an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite.”","children":["For this sentence the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning without any difficulty."]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"Late closure can be seen at work in a sentence that **requires the “unpreferred” parse to be sensible**, such as **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels.”** Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe. The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**, so “with the jewels” has to attach to the noun phrase “the safe,” which is the unpreferred NP attachment.","children":[]},{"text":"In **careful tests of reading time and eye movements**, people are **slower to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse**. **Reading time** measures how long readers spend on each part of the sentence. **Eye movement** records track where readers look and when they jump back to reread earlier words.","children":["The extra time appears right at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea that the jewels were used to blow open the safe**, and then they have to **retrace their steps**. The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, because that is its usual preference and it is what it did for the dynamite. The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading, so the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase.","children":["The final meaning is easy to understand, but getting there carries a measurable **processing cost**, because the repair takes time."]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"This syntactic explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence. It parallels the **“lumber mill”** model of thresholded processing from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without checking meaning or context, and meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**. It wants to make **the simplest tree possible**, which is **minimal attachment**. It does this partly by **leaving clauses open as long as possible**, which is **late closure**.","children":["Both preferences limit how much structure has to be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"This is one way of dealing with ambiguity **very efficiently**, because the parser can **ignore ambiguity until it becomes a problem**. It commits to one likely structure and builds only that one, and most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","children":["When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be revised, and that revision is the source of garden path difficulty. Garden paths are the price of an efficient strategy that works well on average."]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"A **purely syntactic explanation** runs into **problems**. One problem is that **syntactically “difficult” sentences are not always difficult to parse**. Many sentences in published research articles contain a **reduced relative clause**, which is a relative clause with “that was” or “that were” left out, as in “the horse raced past the barn fell.”","children":[]},{"text":"In each of the following sentences, the verbs in capital letters introduce a reduced relative. **Trueswell, Tanenhaus and Garnsey (1994)** wrote “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a MORPHOLOGICALLY AMBIGUOUS VERB … will be temporarily ambiguous.” **Britt, Perfetti, Garrod and Rayner (1992)** wrote “Referential information PROVIDED by the discourse is of no help.”","children":["**Frazier and Rayner (1987)** wrote “Recent research REPORTED by FERREIRA AND CLIFTON (1986) has demonstrated that syntactic processing is quite independent and that the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence is little influenced by the semantic information ALREADY ANALYZED.”","**Altmann and Steedman (1988)** wrote “The pattern of results OBTAINED with global reading times is reflected by differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase.”"]},{"text":"Every capitalized verb begins a reduced relative with the same structure as the horse sentence, **but these are not impossibly difficult sentences**. Readers follow them easily, with no feeling of being led down a garden path.","children":["In “recent research reported by Ferreira and Clifton,” reported describes research that someone reported, so research is not the one doing the reporting."]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** advanced an alternative argument, which is that **semantic factors and statistical regularities interact with parsing from very early on**. **Semantic factors** concern meaning, and **statistical regularities** are patterns in how often words occur in particular structures.","children":["On this view, particular words and their familiar uses can support a reduced relative reading from the start, so the sentence is easy to read."]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"The collection of reduced relatives is a **wonderfully passive-aggressive** table. **Many of the examples are drawn from papers that use the difficulty of parsing reduced relatives as evidence for an autonomous parser**. An **autonomous parser** is a parser that builds syntactic structure without help from meaning or context.","children":["The authors of those papers argued that reduced relatives are hard to process. Yet they wrote reduced relatives in their own articles, and readers understood them without trouble."]},{"text":"More examples from the same collection make the same point. In each one the reader attaches the capitalized verb to the right noun with no effort.","children":["In “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a morphologically ambiguous verb,” followed modifies noun phrase. In “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence,” assigned modifies analysis. In “differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase,” located modifies differences.","**Ferreira and Clifton (1986)** wrote “A person PRESENTED with the evidence EXAMINED by the lawyer may interpret the evidence as a theme, not an agent, because examined requires an animate agent.” Here presented modifies person, and examined modifies evidence."]},{"text":"The examples cited were **not the only reduced relatives** in those articles. Reduced relatives turned up at a rate of about **one per journal page** across this sample of articles. So reduced relatives are routine in scientific writing, and in a supportive context a formally complex structure is easy to understand.","children":[]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"The argument is that several factors besides syntax are at work when we read **“Recent research reported by…”** One factor is **semantics**. Research is an **inanimate** object, which means it is not a living thing, so it is unlikely to be the **subject** of a verb like **report**. Taking research as the thing that was reported fits the meanings of the words.","children":["A second factor is **lexical statistics**, which are learned patterns of how often particular words appear in particular structures. **“Reported” occurs frequently in reduced relatives**.","A third factor combines lexical statistics with **“world knowledge.”** “Reported” occurs very frequently in reduced relatives **in scientific papers, especially when following the word “research,”** and world knowledge here means knowing how people actually write in that setting."]},{"text":"These and **many other factors interact with the syntax of the sentence very early on**. They act from the first moments of reading, so they help decide which structure is chosen in the first place.","children":[]},{"text":"Consider **“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today”** and **“The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today.”** **Why is the policeman sentence harder to read?** Burglars are likely to be arrested, so arrested is easily read as the start of a reduced relative that describes the burglar. Policemen are likely to be the ones doing the arresting, so arrested is first taken as the main verb, and “was charged” then causes a garden path.","children":["The grammar of the two sentences is identical, so the difference in difficulty comes from what we know about burglars and policemen."]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**, because it can come from the visual scene right in front of us as well as from general knowledge. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a **visual world** task. They gave people a display of real objects, which were **a pencil, a box, an empty towel, and an apple sitting on a towel**. People then heard the instruction **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","children":[]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like it should be the end of the clause **“put the apple on the towel.”** In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**, the place where the apple should go.","children":["In the intended reading, “on the towel” says **which apple** to move, the one already on a towel, and **“in the box”** is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do seem to look at the empty towel while planning their movements**, as though they are considering it as a target, before they plan a movement to the box. With one apple in view and the ambiguous instruction, a typical sequence of looks goes to the apple at about 1,000 milliseconds, then to the empty towel shortly after, and then to the box at about 2,000 milliseconds.","children":["The comparison was the unambiguous instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box.”** With that instruction, the eyes go from the apple straight to the box, with no look to the empty towel.","The listener recognizes every word correctly. The error comes from taking the location phrase as the destination, which briefly changes the planned action."]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"In a **two-referent context**, the display holds **two apples**. One apple sits on a towel and the other sits somewhere else, and the empty towel and the box are still there. “The apple” alone no longer says which apple to move, so the phrase **“on the towel”** now does useful work, because it picks out one apple from the two.","children":[]},{"text":"In this context, participants usually look first at the apple on the towel and then at the box. Looks to the empty towel become much less common.","children":["**The sequence and the timing of eye movements**, measured relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and the unambiguous instructions.","So the ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit instruction with “that’s on the towel.”"]},{"text":"The two-apple display gives “on the towel” a clear job before the listener even reaches “in the box.” With two apples in view, the listener needs to know which apple is meant, so a phrase that singles one out is expected. Listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start, so the destination reading never takes hold.","children":["The same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context is different."]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"Now consider what happens when there are **two apples** in the display. It suddenly becomes **felicitous**, which means natural and appropriate in the situation, to give more information about **which apple** you are talking about.","children":["With only one apple, there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination.","With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"So **people do not look at the empty towel as much**. The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the **incorrect destination**, which is the empty towel. In the **one-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials, and unambiguous instructions led to almost none. In the **two-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials, which is close to the roughly **12%** for unambiguous instructions.","children":["The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts, so the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled."]},{"text":"This is an effect of **referential context**, which is the set of objects that a phrase could refer to. Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while they are still hearing the sentence.","children":["The large cost of ambiguity appears only when the display contains one apple."]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"The results from **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** are **a big problem for purely syntactic accounts**. **Different parses of the same sentence are favored under different semantic and pragmatic conditions, and this effect happens very early**.","children":["**Semantic** conditions have to do with meaning.","**Pragmatic** conditions have to do with how language is used in a particular situation, such as whether a speaker needs to specify which object is meant."]},{"text":"The same ambiguous sentence leads to looks at the empty towel on about **55%** of trials with one apple, but on only about **17%** of trials with two apples. The words are identical in both conditions. Only the visual situation changes, and that change alters the parse the listener adopts first.","children":[]},{"text":"The difference appears in **eye movements during the sentence**, before the action is carried out, so it captures the parse while it is being built. If context only repaired the parse afterward, listeners in both contexts should first look at the empty towel. Those looks drop with two apples, so context must have shaped the **first parse**.","children":["A parser that always starts with the same structure cannot predict that the garden path nearly disappears with two apples."]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"More recently, researchers have begun using data from **mouse movements** to look at these questions. **Farmer and colleagues (2007)** tracked the path of the computer mouse while participants carried out spoken instructions in a version of the apple and towel display. Mouse tracking records the whole path of the hand, so a competing interpretation can pull the movement toward another location before the hand reaches its target.","children":["It is not just eye movements but **the actual execution of arm movements** that can be influenced by **syntactic ambiguity** and its **interaction with the visually defined context**."]},{"text":"In the **one-referent context**, the hand moved from the apple toward the box, but with ambiguous instructions its path **curved toward the empty towel**, compared with the path for unambiguous instructions. The two paths separated most in the middle of the movement, and the hand still ended at the correct place.","children":["In the **two-referent context**, the ambiguous and unambiguous paths were nearly identical. The second apple makes the location phrase useful for picking out an apple, so it is no longer taken as a destination."]},{"text":"The researchers also measured the **Euclidean distance between the ambiguous and unambiguous trajectories** at each moment, which is the straight-line distance between the two paths. In the one-referent context, the distance rose to a peak of about 200 pixels around 40% of the way through the movement. In the two-referent context, it peaked lower, at about 140 pixels, and earlier, around 30%, and then dropped off.","children":["Comprehension and action planning interact while both are underway. A competing interpretation bends the path of the hand even when the movement ends at the correct place."]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"**The** is a **determiner**, which is a word that opens a **noun phrase**. Once the parser meets “the,” it expects a noun to follow soon, possibly with an adjective or another modifier in between.","children":["“The” does not name anything by itself.","It limits which kinds of words can come next, so the parser can anticipate the shape of the phrase."]},{"text":"This expectation is about a **grammatical category**, and it is not about one particular word. Many different nouns could follow, such as pizza, dog or book. An adjective could also come first, as in “the hot pizza.”","children":["The parser predicts the form of the phrase while its specific meaning is still open."]},{"text":"Taking a sentence one word at a time reveals how each word sets up expectations for the next word. Words that fit the expectation are processed smoothly, while words that break it cause difficulty.","children":[]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"**Pizza** is a **noun**, and it completes the noun phrase that “the” opened. It is the **head of the noun phrase**, which is the central word that the rest of the phrase is built around.","children":["“The” combines with “pizza” to form “the pizza,” which can now serve as the subject of the sentence.","With a complete noun phrase in place, the parser expects a verb or verb phrase that says what the pizza did or what happened to it."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word makes two kinds of information available. Its **category**, noun, tells the parser how it can combine with other words. Its **meaning**, a kind of food, sets up expectations about plausible events, such as being eaten or baked.","children":["Word recognition supplies more to sentence processing than a sound or a spelling.","Both **grammatical expectations** and **meaning-based expectations** guide how the next words are processed."]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"**Was** is a past-tense form of the verb **be**. It can act as an **auxiliary**, which is a helping verb that combines with another verb to mark grammatical information such as tense or voice.","children":["In “the pizza was eaten,” was helps form a **passive** with the participle eaten.","In “the pizza was in the oven,” was links the subject to a phrase that gives a location."]},{"text":"When “was” arrives, the sentence could continue in several ways, so several possibilities stay open. The sentence could describe where the pizza was, what it was like or what was done to it. The parser has to keep the developing structure available while more words arrive, because the grammatical role of was depends partly on what comes next.","children":["A location phrase would come next with a preposition, an adjective would say what the pizza was like, and a passive would come next with a participle such as eaten."]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"**In** is a **preposition**, which is a word that expresses a relationship such as location or containment. It begins a **prepositional phrase**.","children":["“In” does not name the location by itself.","It sets up the expectation of a noun phrase that will supply the location, as in “in the oven” or “in the box.”"]},{"text":"After “the pizza was,” the arrival of “in” narrows the possible structures. The sentence is now heading toward a statement about where the pizza was. The parser expects a noun phrase that names a place or container, because that noun phrase is what completes the prepositional phrase.","children":["This expectation is grammatical, so the next word must be able to start a noun phrase. Words like “the,” “an” or a noun would fit the expectation.","Understanding the phrase requires combining the preposition with that noun phrase."]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"**The** begins a new **noun phrase** inside the prepositional phrase, which reads “in the …” so far. It is the start of the phrase that will name the location.","children":["“The” does not supply the location by itself.","A noun is still needed, possibly with modifiers before it, as in “in the hot oven.”"]},{"text":"The second “the” confirms that a noun phrase has started, so the parser now strongly expects a **noun**. This expectation concerns a **word category**, and it is more than an expectation about a sensible topic. Whatever comes next must work grammatically as part of a noun phrase.","children":["Knowing that the sentence is about pizza makes words like oven or box likely, but the grammatical requirement holds whatever the meaning is.","The grammatical frame is now very specific, so any word that cannot be part of a noun phrase will clash with it."]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"**Eaten** is the **past participle** of the verb **eat**. It is a verb form and not a noun, so it cannot complete the noun phrase that “in the” has set up. The phrase **“in the eaten”** puts a verb form exactly where a noun is required.","children":["This is a **word-category violation**, which means a word of the wrong grammatical category appears in a position that demands a different category."]},{"text":"A category error is different from a strange but grammatical meaning. “The pizza was in the ocean” is odd in meaning but grammatically fine, because ocean is a noun. No unusual situation can rescue “the pizza was in the eaten,” because the problem lies in the grammatical type of the word.","children":["A strange but grammatical word such as ocean only makes the meaning odd. A word of the wrong category such as eaten breaks the structure itself."]},{"text":"This contrast between **category errors** and **meaning errors** is what makes such sentences useful for studying the brain. Brain responses to the two kinds of error can be compared, so researchers can see whether grammar and meaning are processed differently.","children":[]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"The **early left anterior negativity**, or **ELAN**, is an **ERP** phenomenon elicited by **word class violations**. An **ERP**, or **event-related potential**, is electrical brain activity recorded at the scalp, averaged over many trials and time-locked to an event such as the onset of a word. A **word class** is a grammatical category such as noun, verb or preposition.","children":[]},{"text":"Classic ELAN stimuli are sentences with word-category violations. One is **“The pizza was in the eaten,”** where a participle appears where a noun is expected. Another is **“The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem,”** where the preposition **of** appears where a noun is expected after “Max’s.”","children":[]},{"text":"The ELAN has three main features. It is **early**, appearing about **100 to 200 milliseconds** after the critical word. It is **left and anterior**, which means it is strongest over the left front of the scalp, and this location has raised the question of whether it is specific to language. It occurs **specifically for word category violations**, such as a noun when a verb is expected, or a preposition in place of a noun.","children":["Incorrect sentences produce an early negative response over left frontal sites soon after the critical word, and correct sentences do not.","The response comes so fast that it was taken as evidence for a very quick syntactic analysis that checks word categories before meaning is processed."]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"With visually presented words, **the ELAN came from visual cortex**, which is the region at the back of the brain that processes what we see. This finding comes from **Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)**, whose paper is titled “Early occipital sensitivity to syntactic category is based on form typicality.” The **occipital** lobe is where visual cortex is located.","children":["For **bimorphemic nouns**, which are nouns that end in an identifiable noun suffix, the response to **“The beautifully princess …”** was larger than the response to **“The beautiful princess …”** at about **120 to 130 milliseconds** after the noun appeared.","Beautiful is an adjective, which correctly predicts a noun. Beautifully is an adverb, which predicts a verb or an adjective, so princess is unexpected."]},{"text":"The finding suggests that when we are expecting a verb, for example, **that prediction can feed back down to the earliest parts of the visual system**. There it predicts visual patterns that are consistent with **the way verbs are written**, because a predicted grammatical category implies likely visual forms, such as typical word endings.","children":["The very early response to the error might reflect **a mismatch between that predictive process and what finally appears on the screen**."]},{"text":"**Does this only work for items that are morphologically marked as nouns or verbs, or for open-class versus closed-class words?** A word is **morphologically marked** when part of it, such as a suffix, signals its category. **Open-class** words are content words such as nouns and verbs, which keep gaining new members, and **closed-class** words are function words such as prepositions and determiners, which form a small fixed set.","children":["The later comparison of noun types answers the first part, because the early effect appears for nouns with a noun suffix and for nouns with a typical noun form, but not for neutral nouns like “infant.”"]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"Many English words can function as either a **noun** or a **verb**, and the rest of the sentence decides which. In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install,” **perches** is a **noun** that means the rods birds sit on, and seem is the verb. In “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches comfortably in the cage,” perches is a **verb** that says what the bird does.","children":["In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met,” **needs** is a **noun** that means the student’s requirements.","In “The teacher told the principal that the student needs to be more focused,” needs is a **verb**."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word form alone does not settle its grammatical role. The **continuation** after the ambiguous word resolves it, so the reader has to wait for words such as “seem” or “comfortably.”","children":[]},{"text":"Words also differ in **phonological form typicality**, which is how much a word’s sound pattern resembles the sound patterns of typical nouns or typical verbs. A corpus analysis of **3,158 words** measured each word’s mean distance in phonological feature space from all nouns and from all verbs, where a small distance means the word sounds similar to that category.","children":["Nouns tend to sound closer to other nouns, and verbs tend to sound closer to other verbs.","Some words go against the trend. Besides **noun-like nouns** and **verb-like verbs**, there are **verb-like nouns** and **noun-like verbs**, which sound like one category while belonging to the other."]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"In terms of **form typicality**, **“perches” is noun-like** and **“needs” is verb-like**. These are learned statistical tendencies in how words sound, and both words can still appear in either role.","children":["Among the 3,158 words in the analysis, perches falls among the noun-like forms and needs falls among the verb-like forms."]},{"text":"For **perches**, the noun-like form matches one sentence and clashes with the other. In **“the bird perches seem easy to install,”** perches is used as a noun, which matches its form. In **“the bird perches comfortably in the cage,”** perches must be a verb, which goes against its form.","children":[]},{"text":"For **needs**, the pattern is reversed. In **“the student needs to be more focused,”** needs is used as a verb, which matches its form. In **“the student needs were not being met,”** needs must be a noun, which goes against its form.","children":[]},{"text":"Each word appears once as a noun and once as a verb, so the same word form can be compared in a **matching** use and a **mismatching** use. This comparison separates what a word’s form suggests from what the grammar of the sentence requires.","children":["If form typicality guides parsing, the mismatching uses should be harder to process, even though all four sentences are grammatical."]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"Reading is **slower when a word’s required category conflicts with its form typicality**. The measure is reading time at **disambiguation** minus reading time at the **point of ambiguity**, adjusted for how long the words are.","children":["The **point of ambiguity** is the ambiguous word itself, such as perches or needs.","**Disambiguation** is the point where the continuation makes the intended category clear, such as “seem” or “comfortably.” A larger increase from one point to the other signals more difficulty."]},{"text":"For **noun-like homonyms** such as perches, sentences that resolved the word as a noun added only about **50 milliseconds**. Sentences that resolved it as a verb added much more, about **120 milliseconds**. So a noun-like form is **harder to resolve as a verb**.","children":[]},{"text":"For **verb-like homonyms** such as needs, the pattern reversed. Sentences that resolved the word as a noun added about **110 milliseconds**, while sentences that resolved it as a verb added almost nothing, under 10 milliseconds. So a verb-like form is **harder to resolve as a noun**.","children":[]},{"text":"The ambiguous word stays the same in each pair, and only the continuation changes. The extra cost appears specifically when the continuation demands the category that does not match the form.","children":["Parsing uses learned statistics about how word forms sound, along with the structure the sentence finally requires."]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"The **ELAN effect for nouns where a verb is expected** is limited to cases where there is either **syntactic information** or **statistical form-based information** that is **physically available** in the word. Syntactic information here means **morphemes** that indicate noun-ness, and a morpheme is the smallest meaningful part of a word, such as a suffix that marks a noun. Statistical form-based information means **“nouniness,”** which is how much a word’s form resembles typical nouns even without a noun suffix.","children":[]},{"text":"The early brain response studied here is the **M100**, which peaks about 100 milliseconds after a word appears. Three kinds of nouns were compared, each in an expected and an unexpected version.","children":["For **bimorphemic nouns**, the comparison was “The beautiful princess …” and “The beautifully princess …” Princess carries a noun-marking suffix, and the unexpected version produced a significantly larger response.","For **typical nouns**, the comparison was “The tasteless soda …” and “The tastelessly soda …” Soda has a statistically noun-like form, and the unexpected version again produced a significantly larger response.","For **neutral nouns**, the comparison was “The cute infant …” and “The cutely infant …” Infant has neither a suffix nor a typical noun form, and the two versions did not differ."]},{"text":"So **no effect was observed for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues**. An abstract category mismatch alone is not enough to produce this earliest visual response. An expected category generates predictions about what the next word will look like, so the early response appears only when the arriving word carries **physical evidence** that conflicts with that prediction.","children":[]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"**Sentence processing can be summed up in four sentences**, and each one carries one main idea. The first is **“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously.”** **Syntax is independent of semantics to some extent**. In the limit you can have sentences that are grammatically correct but meaningless, because words can fill a grammatical structure without describing a sensible event.","children":[]},{"text":"The second is **“The horse raced past the barn fell.”** The syntactic structure of a sentence is often highly **ambiguous**, or more exactly **vague**, but we do not notice except where things fail.","children":["**Reading times** for preferred and unpreferred structures suggest that syntactic processes **take shortcuts to save memory**, and do not activate every possible parse of a sentence.","When a shortcut picks the wrong structure, as in the horse sentence, the reader is led down the garden path."]},{"text":"The third is **“Put the apple on the towel in the box.”** **Context, pragmatics and other factors seem to interact with syntactic processing from very early on**.","children":["With two apples in view, listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple and not as a destination, so the visual situation shapes the parse while the sentence is still unfolding."]},{"text":"The fourth is **“The bird perches were comfortable.”** **The very earliest syntactic effects observable in the brain are driven in part by form typicality**, not necessarily by **morphosyntactic** structures, which are grammatical structures signaled by word parts and word order. These effects seem to be related to **predictions about the physical forms of words**.","children":["Perches has a noun-like form, so its use as a noun here matches the expectation that its form sets up."]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"**Aphasia** is a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage. In the **1870s**, a number of German neurologists, including **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke**, began making **more systematic observations of aphasia**. They ascribed specific losses of function to **“disconnections” between “centers,”** and this was reasoning about aphasia before Chomsky.","children":["A **center** is a brain region that stores one kind of language representation. A disconnection is damage to the pathway that links two centers, so information can no longer travel between them."]},{"text":"**Lichtheim's “House” model** arranges the centers and pathways in the shape of a house. **A** is the center for **sound images**, which are the stored sound patterns of words, and **M** is the center for **movement images**, which are the stored motor plans for saying words. **B** is the center for **concepts**, and it sits at the peak of the roof.","children":["The **acoustic pathway a** brings heard speech into A, and the **motor pathway m** carries plans from M out to the speech muscles.","Seven numbered sites mark different forms of aphasia, and each one is tied to damage at a particular center or pathway."]},{"text":"Each damage site predicts its own pattern of loss. A loss of the ability to **understand spoken words** might be due to a disconnection between **A and B**, because the sound of the word arrives but cannot reach its concept. A loss of the ability to do **verbatim repetition** might be due to a disconnection between **A and M**, because the heard word cannot pass directly to the plan for saying it.","children":["This kind of **“box and arrow” model** is foundational in **aphasiology**, the study of aphasia, and in modeling typical language function. The **interactive activation model** of word recognition has the same basic form, with levels of representation joined by pathways.","This history comes from Eling and Whitaker (2009)."]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"The **“Geschwind-Wernicke model”** of brain and language ties language functions to particular brain regions and the pathways that join them. **Norman Geschwind revived** the box-and-arrow approach of Wernicke and Lichtheim in the **1970s**.","children":["**Broca's area** sits toward the front of the left hemisphere, in the frontal lobe, and **Wernicke's area** sits farther back, in the temporal lobe. The **arcuate fasciculus** is a bundle of nerve fibers that connects Wernicke's area with Broca's area.","Two major grooves serve as landmarks. The **central sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Rolando**, separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe behind it. The **lateral sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Sylvius**, separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes above it."]},{"text":"In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, **the field of linguistics evolved dramatically**. Linguists developed a theoretical model in which **syntax was independent of other aspects of language** and had **its own computational principles**. That model opened the door to theorizing about **dissociations** and **double dissociations** of language functions in more sophisticated ways.","children":["In a **dissociation**, one ability is impaired while another is spared. In a **double dissociation**, one patient has the first ability impaired and the second spared, while another patient has the reverse pattern.","A double dissociation is strong evidence that the two abilities depend on separate systems. Once syntax, word knowledge and meaning are treated as separate parts of language, a patient can be described as impaired in one part while the others work well."]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"**Fromkin (1997)** describes the classic profiles of the two main aphasias. The speech of **Broca's aphasia** patients has **word-finding pauses**, **loss of grammatical morphemes**, and quite often **disturbed word order**. Their understanding of everyday conversation **gives the impression of being generally good**, but **controlled testing reveals considerable impairments**.","children":["A **grammatical morpheme** is a small word or word part that marks a grammatical relationship, such as “the,” “is” or the past-tense ending “-ed.”"]},{"text":"**Agrammatism** means difficulty producing or understanding grammatical structure, and the term is almost a synonym for Broca's aphasia. Even so, **some patients with lesions in Broca's area are not agrammatic**, and **some agrammatic patients would not be classified neurologically as Broca's aphasics**. So the behavior and the damaged region overlap heavily but not perfectly.","children":["Broca's aphasia names a pattern of behavior, and that pattern does not always follow damage to the area with the same name."]},{"text":"Patients with **Wernicke's aphasia** produce **fluent speech** with **good intonation and pronunciation**. Their speech contains **many word substitutions**, some related in meaning to the intended word and some unrelated. They also produce **neologisms**, which are invented words, and **phonological errors**, which are errors in the sounds of words, and they have **comprehension difficulties**.","children":["Fluency describes how smoothly speech is delivered, so it is separate from whether the words carry a coherent message.","Broca's patients keep meaningful words but lose the small grammatical pieces that connect them, while Wernicke's patients keep the grammatical frame but fill it with wrong or invented words."]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, meaning a two-way division, sets the two aphasias against each other. **Wernicke's aphasia** is described as **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax is roughly intact**. “Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll's poem of grammatical sentences filled with nonsense words.","children":["One Wernicke's patient, asked about his health, replied “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves,” which is an example from Kriendler and colleagues (1971). The sentence flows smoothly but never forms a clear message.","Another patient said “My wires don’t hire right” when he was asked about his poor vision.","An aphasic physician who was asked if he was a doctor said “I’m a male demaploze on my own” and “I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go,” and **demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient described a fork as “a need for a schedule” and a spoon as “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** is described as **“telegraphic” speech**, with **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles** and **relative sparing of important content words**. One Broca's patient from **Badecker and Caramazza (1985)** was asked what brought him to the hospital. His answer included “Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital” and “Two doctors … and ah … teeth,” with long pauses throughout.","children":["Content words such as Dad, hospital, doctors and teeth carry pieces of the story. The grammatical words and endings that would connect them into sentences are largely missing, and the speech is full of pauses."]},{"text":"The two patterns look like mirror images. Wernicke's patients have fluent grammar with empty or wrong words, while Broca's patients have meaningful words with little grammar.","children":[]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"The dichotomy can also be stated in terms of what is damaged. On this account, **Wernicke's aphasia** reflects **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** reflects **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words. **Activation** makes a candidate word available, and **inhibition** suppresses competing words that should not be chosen, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"This description is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Paul Broca's presentation of **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan,”** says that Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, which was “tan.” He **seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses**, and he in fact **seemed to understand what was being said**.","children":["**Why is it critical that Leborgne had his other senses intact?** Intact senses mean that the loss was specific to speech, so it counts as a dissociation.","A “capacity for articulate speech” also seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness,” which is a phrenology term for love of one's children.","If Tan could be examined today, his deficits would be specified at the **levels of description used in modern linguistics**, which are phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic."]},{"text":"The result is confusing, because **Broca's actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca's aphasics** today. They were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control issues**. They would be classified as suffering from **apraxia of speech**, which is a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak.","children":["Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"The dichotomy treats Wernicke's aphasia as a problem in the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a problem with grammar. It is interesting partly because it **aligns well with a sharp distinction that late 20th-century formal linguistics drew between the grammar and the lexicon**.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences. The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky's formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. A person recognizes a new item as a sentence **not because it matches some familiar item in any simple way**, but because **it is generated by the grammar** that each person has internalized.","children":["A sentence never heard before can still be understood, because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"Chomsky also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**. In **Chomsky (1955)**, neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker's past experience, and yet **the first is grammatical while the second is not**.","children":["The first sentence has no sensible meaning, but its word order follows English grammar. The second uses the same words in an order the grammar does not allow.","The two sentences are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference between them must come from grammar as its own system.","The aphasia dichotomy maps onto this split, with Wernicke's aphasia as a disorder of the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a disorder of the grammar."]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"The dichotomy also fits the **speech production model**, and in particular the difference between the **functional level** and the **positional level**. At the functional level, which is the first linguistic level, the speaker is **arousing word meanings and semantic roles**. At the positional level, which is the second linguistic level, the speaker is **getting word forms in grammatically appropriate order**.","children":["A **semantic role** is the part a participant plays in an event, such as the doer or the receiver.","Before these two levels comes the conceptual **message level**, where the speaker is choosing a story angle and arousing concepts of referents and event structure. After them, the **phonological encoding level** supplies the sounds of the words, and the **speech gesture level** handles **articulation**, the physical movements of speaking."]},{"text":"At the functional level, **lemmas have an arbitrary relationship to their referents**. A **lemma** is a word's entry in the lexicon, which links its meaning to its grammatical properties, and a referent is the person, object or event being talked about. Arbitrary means that nothing about the concept determines the word, so there is no reason a dog must be called “dog.” At the positional level, in contrast, **syntactic trees and affixes are the product of abstract syntactic processes**.","children":["An **affix** is a word part added to a stem, such as “-ed” or “-ly.” The positional level is the grammatical side of language, which is built by rules."]},{"text":"One objection is that words like **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** have meanings that are clearly not arbitrary, because they are built from parts. The reply is that **speech error evidence** places **derivational morphology** at the positional level.","children":["**Derivational morphology** builds new words from existing parts, such as adding “-ly” to make an adverb.","In the error **“easy enoughly,”** the speaker meant “easily enough,” but the “-ly” suffix attached to the wrong word. The suffix moved as a separate piece, so a grammatical operation at the positional level must have added it."]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"Popular theories of **sentence processing** hold that **the syntactic structure of a sentence is computed independently of meaning** as people listen or read. Under these theories, an autonomous parser builds the structure first, and the meaning is worked out afterward. **Isolating these “parsing” mechanisms** requires carefully constructing stimulus materials.","children":[]},{"text":"The sentence “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite” has two possible structures. The **preferred** structure attaches “with the dynamite” to the **verb phrase**, so the dynamite is the instrument. The **unpreferred** structure attaches it to the **noun phrase** “the safe,” so the phrase tells which safe.","children":["The words are the same in both structures and only the attachment changes, so a test built on such sentences can target parsing without changing word meanings."]},{"text":"**One possibility** is that **Broca's patients generally understand what is being said even though they have a core grammatical deficit**. In ordinary conversation, word meanings, familiar events and context all point toward the same message. A listener can use these cues to understand without fully computing the grammar.","children":["Testing a specific parsing deficit requires sentences where the grammatical relationships cannot be recovered by guessing the most likely event.","Reversible sentences, where either person could plausibly do the action, force the listener to use grammar."]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"**None of this theory was available to Broca back in the 1860s**. For him, it was interesting enough to **dissociate “articulate speech” from comprehension**. Leborgne could produce only one syllable, yet he appeared to understand what was said to him.","children":["Showing that speaking and understanding can come apart was the key finding at the time."]},{"text":"The modern question is different from the one Broca asked. Suppose the goal is to find out whether **some patients have a difficulty that is more generally with syntax**. **How would we do it?** The way to do it is to **test comprehension**.","children":["A **syntactic impairment** concerns the organization of grammatical relationships, so it should affect both producing and understanding sentences.","A comprehension test lets the patient respond without speaking, so motor problems cannot explain a poor score. If the patient still struggles to understand grammatical relationships, the deficit goes beyond articulation."]},{"text":"Broca's original question was simpler than the one modern researchers ask. He wanted to separate producing speech from understanding speech, and Leborgne, who could understand but could not speak, separated the two. Modern researchers ask whether a patient's difficulty lies in grammar itself, and that question requires testing comprehension of specific structures.","children":[]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"**Why was it interesting to show that Broca's patients have comprehension deficits?** The first reason is that **Broca's aphasia is almost always accompanied by some level of motor dysfunction**. Agrammatic speech might **merely reflect an attempt to communicate despite motor limitations**. If speaking takes great effort, a patient might drop the small grammatical words and keep only the most informative content words.","children":["**Hughlings Jackson** made a related point, which is that **we are observing what the individual can do despite the impairment**. A brain injury that leads to agrammatic speech does not literally mean that **the grammar itself is injured**.","Caramazza and Zurif (1976) cite a version of this idea from Lenneberg (1973). If anterior damage spares the patient's tacit knowledge of language, the telegraphic output can be attributed to a neuromuscular problem, because **the strain of speaking is so great that the patient speaks asyntactically in order to economize effort**."]},{"text":"**Chomsky's distinction between competence and performance** also applies. **Competence** is a person's underlying knowledge of language. **Performance** is the actual use of that knowledge in speaking or understanding, and it can be disrupted by memory limits, distraction or motor problems. So **when motor deficits obviously interfere with performance, comprehension may provide a clearer window into competence**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** tested comprehension in Broca's patients for this reason. Speech production cannot tell the motor account and the grammar account apart, because both predict telegraphic speech.","children":["If these patients understand grammar normally, their agrammatic speech is a motor performance problem. If they also fail to understand grammatical relationships, the deficit reaches the grammar itself."]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"**“Man bites dog”** is the newspaper example of an unexpected event. **Why do we need psycholinguistic tests to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension?** The reason is that **in conversation, there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**.","children":["**Redundant** cues are several different sources of information that all point to the same meaning."]},{"text":"In “The dog bit the man,” there are **many ways to get the right meaning**. A listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN into an event representation**, **with or without syntax**, because dogs bite people much more often than people bite dogs. The sentence also follows **canonical word order**, which is **agent, then verb, then undergoer**, or **subject, then verb, then object**.","children":["The **agent** is the doer of the action, and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English, the first noun is usually the agent.","A lot of comprehension also takes place in a **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, which clarifies a great deal."]},{"text":"Broca's patients' comprehension might depend on these **compensatory heuristics**. A **heuristic** is a mental shortcut, and a compensatory one makes up for a weakness elsewhere. A patient might assume the most plausible event, or assume that the first noun is the doer, and either shortcut gives the right answer for “The dog bit the man” without any grammatical analysis.","children":["To test grammar directly, the sentences have to defeat the shortcuts, as “The dog that the man is biting is angry” does. Here the **man** is doing the biting, even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters, so only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** devised **a list of just this kind of sentence, along with a set of control sentences**. The materials come in four groups of eight sentences each. **Control sentences** are simple sentences in canonical order, such as “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle,” where the doer comes first and the event is ordinary.","children":[]},{"text":"**Center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints** contain one clause inside another, but meaning makes the roles obvious. A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause placed in the middle of the main clause. Examples are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round,” and apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone reveals who does what.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reversible center-embedded sentences** use two participants who could each plausibly do the action. Examples are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy,” where meaning gives no help and only grammar reveals the roles. **Improbable center-embedded sentences** describe events that go against expectations. Examples are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny,” where common sense points to the wrong answer.","children":["Worms do not usually eat birds, but in the improbable sentence the worm is the eater."]},{"text":"In every embedded sentence, the first noun is the one acted upon, and the doer is inside the relative clause. The grammatical structure stays the same across the three embedded groups, while the help or interference from meaning changes.","children":["A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on the control and semantically constrained sentences and fail on the reversible and improbable ones. A listener who parses normally should succeed on all four."]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**, in which participants heard a sentence and chose which of the pictures matched its meaning. Accuracy reveals whether the listener understood who did what to whom, and no spoken answer is required. They compared healthy listeners with patients who had Broca's, conduction and Wernicke's aphasia on the four sentence types.","children":["The four types were control sentences, semantically constrained sentences, reversible sentences and improbable sentences."]},{"text":"**Healthy controls are more or less equally good at all stimulus types**, and they stay near the maximum score on each one. **Wernicke's patients are poor overall**, with low scores even on the simple control sentences.","children":["The poor scores of Wernicke's patients are probably a result of their **general comprehension deficits**, which affect understanding of words as well as grammar."]},{"text":"**Broca's patients** have a **particular difficulty keeping “who did what to whom” straight** when **syntax conflicts with common sense** or with the probable semantic roles for the nouns. Their scores are high on control sentences and on semantically constrained sentences, because meaning supplies the answer there. Their scores drop on reversible and improbable sentences, because only grammar can reveal the roles there.","children":[]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca's aphasia impacts syntax specifically**. The deficit appeared in understanding as well as in speaking, so it lies in the grammar itself.","children":["The title of the paper is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes,” which states the idea. Broca's patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis but keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"A more **specific theory of the “parts of language”** opens up **new ways of exploring structure-function relationships**. A **structure-function relationship** is a link between a brain structure and the function it supports. Before such theories, aphasias could only be described in broad terms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Rather than just breaking up aphasics into those with good and poor comprehension, or those who are more or less fluent**, researchers can **isolate problems with particular aspects of language**. One patient might know word meanings well but assign the wrong roles in complex sentences. Another might produce a fluent grammatical frame filled with words that fail to carry the intended message.","children":["A single fluency rating would hide the difference between these two patients."]},{"text":"The key division is **the grammar versus the lexicon**. **Grammar** concerns the rules that relate words to one another in a sentence, and the **lexicon** concerns stored words and the ability to retrieve the right ones. This division gives specific predictions, so controlled tasks can test whether a particular operation is impaired and relate that impairment to a particular brain region.","children":["A grammar deficit predicts trouble with reversible sentences but not with word meanings.","A lexical deficit predicts trouble with word meanings and naming but not with grammatical structure."]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**. **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and **indicate which animal was doing the action**. Pictures of a dog and a cat appeared on a computer screen, and participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","children":["The event is always a dog biting a cat, but the sentence structure changes."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the doer first. The **active** type is “The dog is biting the cat,” and the **subject cleft** type is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat,” so for both types a shortcut of choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer.","children":["A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s” and “that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft, the focused noun is the subject, which is the doer."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the receiver of the action first. The **object cleft** type is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting,” where the focused noun is the object, the one being bitten. The **passive** type is “The cat is bitten by the dog,” where the receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","children":["For these two types the first-animal shortcut gives the wrong answer, so choosing the dog correctly requires grammatical analysis.","A listener who always picks the first animal succeeds on the first two types and fails on the last two, while a listener who parses the grammar succeeds on all four."]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** do this task, **a pattern emerges much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by type of aphasia with the Western Aphasia Battery, and the percentage of correct responses was measured for active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences.","children":["**Elderly controls** score close to 100% on all four sentence types.","**Wernicke's** patients score low on every sentence type, around 43% to 50%, which is near chance."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia patients have particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, which are the object clefts and passives. They score about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts, and then they drop to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives. These are **noncanonical** structures, meaning they depart from the usual doer-first order.","children":["**Their performance is overall not so great**, because even on simple active sentences they are far below the elderly controls. The pattern combines a general drop in performance with an extra cost for complex structures."]},{"text":"The other groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop for complex sentences. **Anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, score about 89% on actives and subject clefts, about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives. **Conduction** aphasia patients score about 75% on actives and subject clefts and fall to about 42% on object clefts.","children":["Each group's profile provides a clue to the nature of its deficit."]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system** in different ways. The stress conditions were things like **speeding up the speech**, adding noise, or **asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task**.","children":["**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal. A **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies from speech, so it sounds muffled.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** are number lists that participants had to remember while listening, which uses up memory resources."]},{"text":"With a single stressor, accuracy stays near 99% in the normal condition and stays high for actives and subject clefts in every condition. Object clefts suffer most, and with noise accuracy falls from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts. Passives also drop under noise, to about 91%.","children":["When two stressors are combined, the effects grow larger. With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy is about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts, but it drops to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives.","Compression plus visual digits lowers object clefts to about 90% while leaving actives near 98%."]},{"text":"**Object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all the manipulations**, which is the same pattern seen in Broca's aphasia. These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge, so stressing their processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile. This suggests that **the comprehension deficits seen in Broca's aphasia patients may be a “performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction in resources hurts them first. The patients may still have the grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"**Are Broca's aphasics' comprehension deficits language-specific?** **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match an action to an object**. In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing has a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking, and participants choose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster. In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead of by pantomime.","children":[]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, and aphasic patients made more errors in both. In the non-linguistic version, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients scored **93.4%**. In the linguistic version, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients scored **87.5%**. So **Broca's aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Accuracy also depended on the type of wrong choice offered, which is called the **distractor**. With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and aphasic patients scored 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors, which are related in meaning to the correct object, controls scored 99.2% and aphasic patients dropped to **86.3%**.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and aphasic patients scored 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, so an affordance-based distractor is a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The nonverbal version removes language from the input but keeps meaning in the task. A pantomimed action has to be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb has to be linked to its object in a sentence.","children":["A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the nonverbal version. The patients' difficulty on that version points to a broader problem with action meanings, which is shared with language."]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca's area**. Lesion maps link brain areas to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**. The strongest associations fall in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, and that region includes Broca's area.","children":["The maps use three horizontal slices of the brain, and colored regions mark where damage was associated with poorer performance. The hottest colors, red and yellow, mark the strongest relationships."]},{"text":"The task only asked participants to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object. They did not have to produce a sentence, and they did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction.","children":["Patients whose damage included Broca's area had the most trouble interpreting pantomimed actions."]},{"text":"Damage to Broca's area therefore affects **more than syntax**. Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved. Broca's area supports **action understanding** as well as grammatical processing.","children":["Any account of Broca's area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"**Are Wernicke's aphasics' comprehension deficits language-specific?** **Saygin and colleagues** asked patients to **match environmental sounds to pictures**. In the **nonverbal** version, a speaker plays an environmental sound, such as a cow mooing, and participants choose between pictures such as a cow and a sheep. In the **verbal** version, the speaker plays a spoken phrase, such as “cow mooing,” and participants choose between pictures such as a cow and a violin.","children":[]},{"text":"The groups differed in overall accuracy. **Controls** scored 99.2% verbal and 98.1% nonverbal, patients with **right hemisphere damage** scored 99.1% and 98.2%, and **anomic** patients scored 98.6% and 96.7%. **Broca's** patients scored 96.3% verbal and 95.2% nonverbal. **Wernicke's patients were particularly bad at this task**, scoring **90.0% verbal** and **91.3% nonverbal**.","children":["Controls, right hemisphere patients and anomic patients performed equally well, Broca's patients performed worse, and Wernicke's patients performed worst."]},{"text":"**No significant difference was observed between the verbal and nonverbal versions of the task**. Wernicke's patients struggled as much with the sound of a cow mooing as with the spoken phrase. So the deficit is not specific to words.","children":["A spoken phrase tests verbal comprehension, and the environmental sound tests the same comprehension without words.","Their difficulty concerns linking sounds of any kind to their meanings, whether the meaning arrives through language or through ordinary sounds."]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"**Deficits in both verbal and nonverbal sound-picture matching are associated with Wernicke's area**. Lesion overlays mark where damage occurred in patients who were impaired on each version. There were **8 patients impaired in the nonverbal version** and **10 patients impaired in the verbal version**.","children":["The warmest colors, red and yellow, mark areas damaged in the largest number of patients."]},{"text":"The two sets of overlays look very similar. In both, the region of greatest overlap falls in the **posterior temporal area of the left hemisphere**, around Wernicke's area. So the verbal and nonverbal impairments come from damage to the same general region.","children":[]},{"text":"The two versions differ in their input, but they share a common demand. A spoken phrase conveys the meaning through words, while an animal call conveys it without language. Both tasks require connecting meaningful sounds to what they represent, and damage around Wernicke's area disrupts that connection in both cases.","children":["Wernicke's area appears to support **meaningful sound processing in general**, with language as one important case."]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"**So, how language-specific are the deficits in aphasia?** This is **still a controversy**. Some patients are especially vulnerable to complex grammatical structures, which suggests that some aspects of language are handled in specialized ways. The same patients can also struggle with action meanings or environmental sounds in tasks that involve no language, which suggests that language shares brain systems with other kinds of meaning.","children":["An account of aphasia has to cover both kinds of finding.","If aphasia deficits were purely linguistic, language would appear to have its own dedicated brain systems."]},{"text":"What should be clear is **how the terms of the debate have evolved**. The field now uses **relatively sophisticated tests to tap specific aspects of language**, such as reversible and improbable sentences, cleft sentences and nonverbal matching tasks. It uses **quantitative analyses of behavior**, which measure accuracy across sentence types, patient groups and stress conditions. It also uses **quantitative analyses of structure-function relationships**, which relate patterns of brain damage to performance across many patients.","children":[]},{"text":"The field has come **quite a long way from the 19th century**. Wernicke and Lichtheim drew boxes and arrows from a small number of individual cases.","children":["Modern research can test which specific operations are impaired and map those impairments onto damaged tissue across large groups of patients."]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"**Structure-function relationships** in aphasia data can be **formalized** with MRI data. **Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping**, from **Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers**, relates the location of brain damage to the severity of symptoms across many patients.","children":["A **voxel** is a small three-dimensional unit of an MRI brain image. For each voxel, patients with damage there are compared with patients without damage there.","If the damaged patients score much worse, that voxel is associated with the symptom."]},{"text":"The **“symptoms”** here are **defined very broadly**. One set of maps marks regions that are likely to be damaged in individuals with **fluency deficits**, and the strongest associations fall in the front part of the left hemisphere. The other set marks regions associated with **comprehension deficits**, and the strongest associations fall farther back, in the posterior temporal region.","children":["Colors from blue for weaker to red for stronger mark how strong each association is."]},{"text":"So data from a large number of patients is consistent with a **rough double dissociation**. **Posterior regions** are associated with **perception**, or comprehension, whereas **anterior regions** are associated with **production**, or fluency.","children":["Each patient contributes a damage pattern and a continuous score, so the method turns many individual cases into a single statistical map. That map gives a quantitative link between brain location and symptoms."]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"The question of **nature and nurture in child language** asks how much of language comes from built-in capacities and how much comes from experience. **Nativist positions** grew out of **Chomsky's critique of Skinner**. Skinner was a behaviorist who explained behavior, language included, with general learning mechanisms such as reward and punishment, and Chomsky argued that those mechanisms could not explain how children acquire grammar.","children":["For Chomsky, the important feature of language is its **generativity**, which is the ability to produce and understand an unlimited number of new sentences. Acquiring a language therefore means **identifying the abstract grammar** that produces the sentences of the language."]},{"text":"The **general learning mechanisms** studied by behaviorists were **not up to this task**, for two reasons. First, grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are **not \"similar\"** in any straightforward way, because one small change can make a sentence ungrammatical while two grammatical sentences can look nothing alike. Second, the context for language acquisition does not look like **operant conditioning**, which shapes behavior with rewards and punishments that follow it.","children":["Children are not reliably rewarded for grammatical sentences or punished for ungrammatical ones, so the **reward and punishment contingencies do not hold**."]},{"text":"Nativist approaches assert two things instead. Children have **innate knowledge of what language is like**, called **Universal Grammar**, which is a built-in set of principles that limits the grammars a child will consider. Children also use **language-specific acquisition processes**, which are learning mechanisms built specifically for language.","children":["Chomsky put the argument this way, \"The fact that all normal children acquire essentially comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity suggests that human beings are somehow specially designed to do this, with data-handling or 'hypothesis-formulating' ability of unknown character and complexity.\"","Every typical child reaches a complex grammar quickly even though experiences differ widely, so the speed and uniformity suggest something built in."]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** is the pattern in which listeners hear differences between speech sounds in terms of categories. Early theories treated it as a **language-specific** and **species-specific** phenomenon. Language-specific means it was thought to occur only for language, and species-specific means it was thought to occur only in humans.","children":["At the time, **only humans** were known to show categorical perception, and **only for speech sounds**, so it looked like a unique feature of human language."]},{"text":"The classic evidence comes from a synthetic consonant continuum of fourteen stimuli. The upper formant transition changes in equal steps from one stimulus to the next while the lower formant stays the same. In **labeling**, listeners switch sharply from **/b/** to **/d/** around stimuli 4 and 5, and from /d/ to **/g/** around stimuli 9 and 10.","children":["In **discrimination**, accuracy **peaks near those boundaries** and drops inside each category, whether the pairs are one, two or three steps apart."]},{"text":"Categorical perception is a compelling example of how **similarity between stimuli is defined in linguistic, rather than physical, terms**. Two sounds that differ by the same acoustic amount can sound almost identical if they belong to the same category. Two sounds with that same acoustic difference can sound clearly different if they fall on opposite sides of a category boundary.","children":["What counts as similar therefore depends on the categories of the language, and these patterns were taken as signs of a specialized speech system."]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be measured through behaviors that change when an infant notices something new. Head turns and sucking reveal what an infant hears without the infant saying anything. Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","children":["The experimenter decides exactly which acoustic difference is presented, so a head turn or renewed sucking tells whether the infant detected that difference."]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab, an infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes. The infant sits on a parent's lap while an experimenter holds the infant's attention forward with toys. A correct head turn after a change earns an interesting visual reward, such as a lit-up moving toy.","children":["Once the infant has learned this link, a head turn after a sound change means **the infant heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** relies on the infant's natural response to something new. The infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a pressure recorder, and strong sucks make a sound play. As the same sound repeats, the infant grows used to it, which is called **habituation**, and sucking slows down.","children":["If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change.","The head turn depends on a learned link between a sound change and a reward, while sucking depends on a natural response to novelty, so the two methods give complementary evidence."]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** using **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, which is the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration. In a syllable with a long VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than in a syllable with a short VOT of +10 milliseconds.","children":["Infants heard a syllable over and over until their sucking slowed down, and then the sound either changed or stayed the same."]},{"text":"The experiment compared **three conditions**. In **20D**, the two sounds differed by **20 milliseconds** of VOT, +20 and +40 milliseconds, and came from **different adult phonemic categories**. In **20S**, the sounds also differed by 20 milliseconds, either −20 and 0 or +60 and +80 milliseconds, but came from the **same category**. In **0**, the **control** condition, the same sound continued throughout.","children":[]},{"text":"In every condition, sucking rose above baseline and then declined as the sound became familiar. In 20D, sucking **jumped back up** after the change, to around 50 responses per minute. In 20S, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling, and in the control condition it kept declining to about 15 responses per minute.","children":["Infants responded to a change that **crossed an adult phoneme boundary** but not to an equally large change within a category.","The two changed conditions were matched in acoustic size, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary, and the control condition establishes what happens with repetition alone."]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"**Why was Eimas's result a big deal?** Categorical perception was considered to be the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** that later linguistic processes could use. A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/.","children":["Finding this ability in infants suggested that the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds are really little**, and their other abilities are only beginning. Some **gross motor milestones** for that age, meaning large movements of the body, are manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while prone, which means lying face down. Their **color vision and general visual acuity**, the sharpness of vision, are still \"in progress.\" They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines.","children":["Basic perception in other areas is still developing, and four-month-olds cannot yet make speech sounds well."]},{"text":"This is **very, very early in development** for an apparently language-specific ability to emerge, so it **seemed like evidence for innateness**. Infants sort speech sounds into categories before they can produce them and while other skills are immature.","children":["Because speech categorization ran so far ahead of other abilities, it looked built in instead of learned through long experience."]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** for speech categories is the decline in sensitivity to sound distinctions that are not used in the language a child hears. At around six months, **English-learning infants** show evidence of discriminating speech contrasts they have never heard before. Over the course of development, the ability to perceive these contrasts **declines**.","children":["**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts. The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two \"ta\"-like sounds, and the **Salish contrast** is a distinction between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language."]},{"text":"They measured the share of infants at each age who reached a **discrimination criterion**. At **6 to 8 months**, about 95 percent of English-learning infants discriminated the Hindi contrast and about 80 percent discriminated the Salish contrast. At **8 to 10 months** these fell to about 67 percent and 52 percent, and at **10 to 12 months** they fell to about 20 percent and 10 percent. Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants discriminated their own native contrast close to 100 percent of the time.","children":["This is **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**. Infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"**How is this related to categorical perception?** As infants form the categories of their native language, sounds that their language groups together become harder to tell apart.","children":["A nonnative distinction that falls within one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"The infant findings support **a very compelling narrative**. In it, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system**.","children":["These abilities are **present very early in development**, as the infant studies suggest."]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world,\"** able to hear **all contrasts**. That means they have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech, which is knowledge that applies to the speech sounds of every language and not one particular language. Children then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems** to the specific language in their environment.","children":["Calling infants \"citizens of the world\" captures the idea that a newborn is ready to learn any human language."]},{"text":"The **role of learning**, then, is to **select** among the many possible **innately specified phonetic contrasts**. Learning narrows down the **inventory**, which is the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive, to just those that are meaningful in the child's native language.","children":["Learning works by elimination, because it drops the contrasts the native language does not use and keeps the innate categories the language needs.","Infants start with a full set of possible contrasts, so the native language decides which innate categories survive."]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"The citizens-of-the-world view faces **some problems**. Three of them concern **categorical perception**. Categorical perception is **not limited to language**, because it also occurs for nonspeech sounds. It is also **not limited to humans**, because animals without language perceive speech sounds categorically.","children":["Categorical perception is also **task-dependent**. How categorical the results look depends on what the listener is asked to do, such as naming a sound or judging whether two sounds differ."]},{"text":"The fourth problem concerns **infant speech perception**, which is **not actually \"universal.\"** Infants do not begin able to hear every contrast equally well.","children":["Some contrasts are hard for young infants to detect, even contrasts that occur in their own language."]},{"text":"Each problem targets a different part of the narrative. The nonspeech and animal findings question whether categorical perception is special to language, and the task effects question how categorical perception really is. Uneven infant sensitivity questions whether all contrasts are innately available.","children":["A full account of development has to explain contrasts that **improve with experience** as well as contrasts that are lost, so learning must **build categories** as well as remove them."]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds**. The sounds varied in **rise time**, which is how quickly a sound grows to full loudness after it starts. A **short rise time** produces a sound like a **plucked** string, and a **long rise time** produces a sound like a **bowed** string.","children":["The stimuli included **sawtooth waves**, speech syllables and sine waves, each made with a 10-millisecond rise time and a 70-millisecond rise time.","With a 10-millisecond rise the wave reaches full size almost at once, and with a 70-millisecond rise it grows gradually."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, listeners identified each sound as a pluck or a bow and also discriminated pairs that were 10 milliseconds apart. **Pluck responses** started near 100 percent at short rise times and dropped steeply between about 30 and 50 milliseconds, while **bow responses** rose in the same region to near 100 percent. Discrimination stayed near 55 to 60 percent for most pairs. It **peaked near 75 percent** for pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, **where the labels switched**.","children":[]},{"text":"The link between a label boundary and better discrimination appears here for a sound distinction that has **nothing to do with phonemes**. Pluck and bow are labels for kinds of sound onset and are not speech categories.","children":["Categorical perception can therefore arise in **general auditory processing**, without a mechanism specific to language."]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested whether **infants** show categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**, using high-amplitude sucking. Infants heard a sound over and over until their sucking slowed down. The sound was then changed, and three conditions were compared.","children":["The conditions were **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**."]},{"text":"In all three conditions, sucking started near 20 per minute and declined to about 12 before the change, as the sound became familiar. After no change, sucking stayed near 12. After a within-category change, sucking also stayed near 12. After a **between-category change**, sucking **jumped back up** to about 18 and then declined again as the new sound became familiar.","children":["Infants responded more strongly to a non-speech change that **crossed a category boundary** than to a change within a category."]},{"text":"This is the **same pattern** that Eimas and colleagues found for speech. Adults show categorical labeling and discrimination for plucked and bowed sounds, and infants show categorical discrimination for nonspeech changes.","children":["Neither group needs language knowledge to show the pattern, so early categorical perception reflects a **general auditory ability**."]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"**Chinchillas** also show categorical perception of **speech sounds**, following **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**. The animals were trained using **operant conditioning**, a form of learning in which a behavior becomes more likely when it is followed by a reward. Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-millisecond lag** stimulus or the **80-millisecond lag** stimulus was an **SD** for food.","children":["SD stands for **discriminative stimulus**, which is a signal that a response will be rewarded.","The lag is the **voice onset time**, and humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, the animals were tested with intermediate VOTs along the continuum from /ga/ to /ka/, and human listeners heard the same sounds. The measure was the proportion of stimuli labeled /ga/. The /ga/ responses stayed near 1 at short VOTs and then fell steeply to near 0, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds. The chinchillas' labeling had the same **steep, categorical shape** as the human listeners' labeling.","children":[]},{"text":"**Increasing the intensity of the burst and aspiration noise** shifted the category boundary to a **shorter VOT**, made the switch steeper, or both, for one chinchilla and one human listener. The **burst** is the brief noise at the release of a stop, and **aspiration** is the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release. Both species responded to these acoustic properties in a similar way.","children":["Animals without language sort a speech continuum into categories, so categorical perception of speech is **shared with other species**."]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found that **some contrasts are less salient than others for infants**. A **salient** contrast is one that is easy to notice. The contrasts they tested involve **nasal consonants** in the **onset**, which is the start of a syllable.","children":["The nasals differ in **place of articulation**, which is where in the mouth the sound is made. The sound **/m/** is made with the lips, **/n/** with the tongue just behind the teeth, and **/ŋ/**, the final sound in \"sing,\" with the back of the tongue against the soft palate."]},{"text":"Infants heard either the same syllable repeated or a syllable that switched, and the researchers compared looking time on same trials and change trials. Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change. For the native contrast between **[ma] and [na]**, English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials. For the nonnative contrast between **[na] and [ŋa]**, the same infants looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials, so there was **no increase** for the change.","children":[]},{"text":"A **place contrast for nasals in onset**, /na/ and /ŋa/, is **not readily perceived by four-month-olds, independent of language experience**. In contrast, /ma/ and /na/ is **relatively salient**.","children":["The gap comes from the sounds themselves and not from experience, because some contrasts are harder to hear from the start.","That undercuts the idea that every contrast begins equally available."]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** also found that **between 6 and 10 months**, exposure to the contrast between /na/ and /ŋa/ in a **Filipino language environment increases its salience**. Filipino treats [na] and [ŋa] as different syllables, so infants learning Filipino hear the contrast regularly.","children":["At **6 to 8 months**, Filipino-learning infants looked about 3.8 seconds on both same and change trials, so the change had no measurable effect.","At **10 to 12 months**, looking rose from about 3.3 seconds on same trials to about 4.8 seconds on change trials, so by then they noticed the switch."]},{"text":"English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months showed no response to this contrast, while they did respond to [ma] and [na]. So it **cannot be that all speech contrasts are available innately**. Sensitivity to [na] and [ŋa] starts out weak, even for infants whose language uses it, and hearing the contrast over the first year makes it easier to detect.","children":[]},{"text":"**Exposure to contrasts in the environment is necessary for acquisition in some cases**. Learning can strengthen a contrast as well as preserve it.","children":["Development therefore includes **building up sensitivity** to hard contrasts, not only losing sensitivity to unused ones, so experience plays an active role in shaping speech categories."]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"**What is the alternative to the nativist approach?** The alternative is the **emergentist approach**. Emergentist approaches assume that **associative learning mechanisms** are sensitive to **statistical regularities in the environment**.","children":["**Associative learning** is learning that two things go together because they repeatedly occur together. **Statistical regularities** are patterns in how often things occur and which things occur together.","On this view, language structure emerges from general learning mechanisms working on these patterns in rich input."]},{"text":"Classical conditioning is the simplest example of associative learning. It can be described with three neurons, a **bell** neuron, a **food detector** neuron and a **salivation** neuron. The food detector already connects strongly to salivation, so food produces salivation. When the bell repeatedly occurs together with food, the **synapse** between the bell neuron and the salivation neuron, which is a connection between neurons, is **strengthened**. After enough pairings, the bell alone can produce salivation.","children":[]},{"text":"**How can this extend beyond conditioning to support learning for pattern recognition?** **Pattern recognition** is the ability to identify a recurring structure, such as a speech sound, across many varied examples. The same strengthening of connections could link acoustic features that tend to occur together.","children":["Repeated co-occurrence in speech could then build up categories that were never specified in advance.","Applied to speech, infants would learn categories by tracking which sounds occur together and how often, instead of starting with a full set of built-in categories."]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"The **American vowels** give an example of statistical regularities. When **Kuhl and others** talk about **learning the statistics of distributions in the environment**, they mean patterns like these. A **distribution** describes how often each value occurs across many examples.","children":[]},{"text":"A **vowel space** is determined by the **first and second formants**. The first formant runs from 300 to 1,200 Hz and the second formant from 600 to 3,400 Hz. Each production of a vowel is one point in this space, so each vowel forms a cloud of points across many productions.","children":["The clouds **overlap** heavily, so a single pair of formant values can belong to more than one vowel.","That overlap is why speech categorization is difficult, because listeners must learn where each vowel is centered and how far it spreads."]},{"text":"Maybe we learn the vowels of our language by **extracting the statistics of these distributions**. Over many exposures, the listener tracks where productions of each vowel tend to cluster, so the categories reflect the shape of the input. A **\"prototypical\" /a/** would then be just the **mean**, or perhaps the **mode**, of the distribution of all the /a/ sounds we hear.","children":["The mean is the average of all the values, and the mode is the most frequent value.","Either way, the prototype comes from experience, so it can differ across languages and listeners."]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"**Does anything like that actually happen?** **Maye and colleagues (2002)** tested whether infants actually learn from distributions. They had infants **listen passively** to stimuli from a continuum from [da] to [ta] with eight steps that differed in **voice onset time**.","children":["Passive listening means the infants simply heard the sounds, with no task or reward."]},{"text":"Two groups heard different distributions during familiarization. In the **bimodal distribution**, stimuli 2 and 7 were presented most often, 16 times each, while the middle stimuli were rare. Two peaks give **evidence for two different categories**. In the **unimodal distribution**, stimuli 4 and 5 in the middle were presented most often, while the ends were rare. One peak gives **evidence for a single category**.","children":[]},{"text":"At test, infants listened to **stimuli 3 and 6** in either **alternating** or **non-alternating** trials. In alternating trials the two stimuli switched back and forth, and in non-alternating trials one stimulus repeated. If infants treat 3 and 6 as different sounds, the two kinds of trials should hold their attention differently.","children":["After unimodal exposure, six-month-olds looked 4.85 seconds on alternating trials and 4.53 seconds on non-alternating trials, and eight-month-olds looked 4.98 and 5.20 seconds.","After bimodal exposure, six-month-olds looked 5.66 and 6.41 seconds, and eight-month-olds looked 5.45 and 6.15 seconds."]},{"text":"**Only infants pre-exposed to the bimodal distribution** were sensitive to the difference between conditions. The unimodal infants looked about equally long on both kinds of trials, as if they had **learned to treat stimuli 3 and 6 as members of the same category**.","children":["A few minutes of exposure to a distribution changed how infants grouped the sounds, which supports statistical learning of speech categories."]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"There is experimental evidence that **social interaction is important for learning**. In one experiment, **nine-month-old American infants** were exposed to **Mandarin Chinese** in twelve 25-minute sessions. Some infants had **live exposure**, with a Mandarin speaker playing and reading with them in person. Others had auditory or audiovisual exposure through audio or television, and a control group had English exposure instead.","children":["After exposure, infants were tested on a **Mandarin phonetic contrast** using the head-turn technique. Infants with live Mandarin exposure scored about 65 percent correct, above the English control group at about 56 percent.","Infants with TV or audio exposure scored at about the same level as the controls, close to chance. The same exposure to a Mandarin speaker is therefore **more effective when it happens in person and is interactive** than when it is viewed as a video."]},{"text":"A second experiment manipulated **social responses**. **Eight-month-old infants** received feedback from their mothers in response to their vocalizations. In the **contingent condition**, mothers responded right after the infant vocalized, so the response depended on the infant's behavior. In the **yoked-control condition**, mothers responded on a schedule copied from another infant, so the responses were **noncontingent**.","children":["Each session had 30 minutes of familiarization, then a 10-minute baseline, a 10-minute social response phase and a 10-minute **extinction** phase, in which the responses stopped."]},{"text":"The measure was the proportion of syllables among the infants' vocalizations. At baseline both groups produced about 0.05 to 0.08. During the social response phase the contingent group rose to about **0.32** while the noncontingent group stayed near 0.05. During extinction the contingent group stayed higher, at about 0.2 compared with about 0.1.","children":["**Contingent social feedback increased the quantity and complexity of infants' vocalizations**, so the responses changed what the infants produced."]},{"text":"Social interactions contingent on the production of different sounds seem to function much like a **reinforcing stimulus**, which is something that makes a behavior more likely when it follows that behavior. One idea is that humans find social interaction with other humans **innately reinforcing**. Another is that we are innately good at using **social cues** to learn from other people.","children":[]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"**Statistical learning with constraints** means learning from patterns in the input in a way that is guided and limited. **What do we mean by \"constraints\"?** The word covers a lot of things, and three kinds stand out.","children":[]},{"text":"The first kind limits **which stimulus properties can be contrastive**. A **contrastive** property is one that distinguishes words, such as voicing, which separates /b/ from /p/. Speaker identity and speech rate change the sound a great deal but do not change which word is said, so they are **not contrastive**.","children":["A learner who tracked every statistical pattern would form categories for different voices and speeds, so learning has to focus on the properties that matter for meaning."]},{"text":"The second kind is **based on earlier learning**. What a learner has already acquired shapes what is easy to learn next. Once native categories are in place, new distinctions that cut across them become harder to notice and learn.","children":["Constraints of this kind are central to **sensitive periods**, in which earlier experience limits later learning."]},{"text":"The third kind comes from **social and interactive contexts for learning**. The same sounds are learned more effectively in live, responsive interaction than from a recording, and contingent responses from caregivers shape infants' own vocal behavior.","children":["Learning depends on the patterns in the input, on what the learner already knows and on the social situation in which the input arrives.","Together, these constraints help explain why learning is fast and why it focuses on the distinctions that matter."]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"Speech is **messy** because **there are no physical boundaries between words** in the speech signal. **Word segmentation** is the job of working out where one word stops and the next one starts in continuous speech. In an utterance such as “I hope it's in my desk,” the sound energy of each word flows straight into the next word, so no silent gap separates the words.","children":["The utterance lasts about 1,800 milliseconds, and the energy of one word runs into the next with no blank space between them."]},{"text":"Where there are **pauses** in the speech stream, they come from **closures** that are **as likely to happen within words as between words**. A **closure** is a moment when the vocal tract is fully blocked, as for the consonants /p/, /t/, /d/ and /k/. Very little sound escapes during a closure, so a brief silence appears.","children":["In “hope” and “it's,” these silences fall inside words or at their edges, so they do not match the word boundaries.","A listener who treated every silence as a boundary would split some words and merge others."]},{"text":"We are still **really good at finding words** in this “sea of sounds,” and adults hear separate words with no effort. **How do we learn to do this?** That is the harder question, because adults can lean on the words they already know.","children":["Infants have to discover the words in the stream before they can learn what those words mean."]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"Listeners have **several cues to word boundaries**, but no single cue works for every word. Some words **appear in isolation**, as in “Hello,” “No,” “Please,” “Go” and “Stop,” so their boundaries are obvious. **Not all words** are heard alone, so most words have to be found inside longer utterances.","children":["A second cue is **phonotactics**, which describes the sound sequences a language allows. **Some sequences of phonemes are very unlikely to occur within words**, so they suggest a break.","In “wordboundary” the sequence /db/ almost never occurs inside an English word, and in “speechcode” the sequence /tʃk/ suggests a break in the same way. The word “movement” calls for caution, because its /vm/ sequence might suggest a break although movement is one word."]},{"text":"**Stress patterns** give another cue, because **in English most words are stressed on the first syllable**. A stressed syllable is louder, longer or higher in pitch. **Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** familiarized **7.5-month-old infants** with one word and then played them passages. The kingdom passage kept using “kingdom,” as in “Your kingdom is in a faraway place,” and the device passage kept using “device,” as in “My new red device makes ice cream.”","children":["Infants familiarized with “kingdom,” which has first-syllable stress, **showed a preference for the kingdom passage**.","Infants familiarized with “device,” which has second-syllable stress, **did not show the same kind of preference**."]},{"text":"In connected speech, **stress may help signal the beginnings of words**. An infant who starts a new word at every stressed syllable finds kingdom easily but cuts device in the wrong place.","children":["This cue **will not work in every language, or for every word in English**, because languages put stress in different places."]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"**Transitional probabilities** offer another cue to word boundaries, and infants can use them through **statistical learning**. Statistical learning means picking up patterns in how often things occur together. Consider the phrase “pretty baby,” which has a word boundary between “ty” and “ba.”","children":["A learner who counts how often each syllable follows each other syllable can find the joints in the speech stream."]},{"text":"In English words, the syllable **/ti/** is **very strongly predicted by the syllable /prɪ/**. After “pre,” the next syllable is very often “ty,” because pretty is a common word. This is **less likely to be true for syllable pairs across words**, because many different words can follow “ty,” so “ba” is poorly predicted.","children":["The syllables “ty” and “ba” go together only when pretty happens to be followed by baby."]},{"text":"The comparison can be written with **frequencies** of occurrence. Dividing the frequency of “pretty” by the frequency of “pre” gives the chance that the syllable “pre” is followed by the syllable “ty” in English. Dividing the frequency of “tyba” by the frequency of “ty” gives the chance that the syllable “ty” is followed by the syllable “ba” in English. The first value is **far greater** than the second.","children":["Syllables inside pretty predict each other strongly, and syllables that straddle pretty and baby predict each other weakly.","Syllable pairs inside a word recur together again and again, while pairs that cross a word boundary recur much less reliably."]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"Stated more abstractly, **the likelihood of syllable B following syllable A is greater within words than between words**. That likelihood is the **transitional probability** from A to B. Inside a word the same syllables always come in the same order, so the probability is high. At a word boundary almost any word can come next, so the probability is lower.","children":["A drop in transitional probability is a good guess for where a word boundary falls."]},{"text":"This cue is **probabilistic**, so it works most of the time but not every time. The sentence “What's that in the road a/head?” can be heard as “a head” or as “ahead.” The sounds are identical, so the meaning of the whole sentence decides the boundary.","children":["“I scream you scream we all scream for ice cream” plays on “I scream” and “ice cream” having almost the same sounds. One sequence of sounds can be split into words in two ways."]},{"text":"Transitional probabilities tip the odds toward one way of splitting the stream, and they work alongside stress, phonotactics and words heard alone. When the cues agree, segmentation is easy.","children":["When cues clash or are missing, as with “a head” and “ahead,” listeners fall back on context."]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996)** separated **statistical information from other cues**. They wanted to find out whether **transitional probabilities** alone can be used to identify words. The stimuli were **tightly controlled** so that every other cue to a word boundary was removed.","children":["If infants still find the words, they must have used transitional probabilities, because nothing else was left to use."]},{"text":"Three controls did this work. The speech was **monotone**, with **no changes in pitch, duration or amplitude**, so stress could not mark where words begin. Every syllable was a **simple CV syllable**, one consonant followed by one vowel, so there were **no phonotactic cues** to word boundaries. The syllables were **presented continuously** with **no pauses**, so silence could not signal a boundary.","children":["Amplitude means loudness, and it is one of the things that makes a syllable sound stressed.","With only CV syllables no unusual consonant clusters ever occurred."]},{"text":"The researchers built an **artificial language** from a set of **three-syllable “words,”** such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila. These words were joined in random order into one long stream.","children":["The only clue to where each word began and ended was the transitional probability between syllables."]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"The stream in the artificial language has **no breaks at all**, as in “tokibugikobagopilatipolutokibu,” “gopilatipolutokibugikobagopila” and “gikobatokibugopilatipolugikoba.” This is exactly what the infant hears, with no spaces and no pauses. Four words, tokibu, gikoba, gopila and tipolu, are **hidden in the stream**, and each recurs many times in random order.","children":["To an adult reader the stream looks like nonsense, because nothing marks where words begin and end except the order of the syllables."]},{"text":"Even with no marked boundaries, the syllables follow **regular patterns**. Inside tokibu, “to” is always followed by “ki,” and “ki” is always followed by “bu.” After “bu,” though, the stream may go on with “gi,” “go” or “ti,” depending on which word comes next.","children":["Some syllable pairs are fully predictable and others are not."]},{"text":"Everything needed to find the words is in the stream. A learner who **keeps track of which syllables follow which** can discover the predictable pairs, so the words come out of the statistics of the sequence.","children":["The infant must discover the words only by tracking the order of syllables."]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"Marking each of the four words in its own color makes their positions in the same stream visible to a reader. **Tokibu** is red, **gikoba** is green, **gopila** is pink and **tipolu** is purple, and each appears many times. The infant gets no such marking, because the infant hears only one unbroken run of syllables.","children":["The colors make the statistical structure visible to us, but the signal itself contains no spaces."]},{"text":"Each word keeps the **same inner order** every time, while its **neighbors keep changing**. The syllables “to,” “ki” and “bu” always come in that order, but tokibu may be followed by gikoba, gopila or tipolu. Transitional probabilities are high inside each word and lower across word edges for that reason.","children":["At the end of each word, the next syllable could start any of the other words."]},{"text":"The word edges are exactly the places where **transitional probability drops**. A learner who notices where predictability drops recovers where one word ends and the next begins.","children":["Learning which syllables reliably belong together is how the learner finds the boundaries that the colors mark."]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"**Testing for an effect of transitional probabilities** takes some care. After listening to a long stream of syllables, it is trivial that infants are more familiar with sequences they have heard than with ones they have not. Infants could prefer heard sequences only because they are familiar, without using transitional probabilities at all.","children":["The test added two details that remove this possibility."]},{"text":"First, **half of the words were repeated more frequently** than the other half. Second, the test for familiarity compared words with **“part-words”** that were **matched for frequency of occurrence** with real words. A **part-word** is a three-syllable sequence that crosses a word boundary, so it joins the end of one word to the start of the next.","children":["Because frequency is equal, familiarity cannot explain any difference between a word and a part-word."]},{"text":"Take the stream “tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu.” The word **GOPILA** and the part-word **BU-GIKO** both appear **twice**, so they are equally frequent. The probability of **GI given BU** is **2 out of 4**, while the transitional probabilities inside GOPILA are **both 2 out of 2**.","children":["BU occurs four times, once at the end of every tokibu, but only two of those are followed by GI, which gives 0.5.","GO is followed by PI both times and PI is followed by LA both times, which gives 1.","If infants treat gopila differently from bu-giko, the difference must come from how predictable the syllables are."]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"The test produced a **novelty effect**, because **infants looked longer in the direction of “part-word” stimuli**. Mean looking time was about **6.8 seconds** for words and about **7.6 seconds** for part-words. The difference was statistically significant.","children":["Part-words held the infants' attention longer than words did."]},{"text":"Longer looking toward part-words is a **novelty preference**. Infants tend to look longer at whatever seems new. The words had grown familiar during listening, so they were less interesting.","children":["The part-words seemed less familiar, although infants had heard them just as often as the words.","The infants must have noticed that the syllables in part-words do not reliably go together."]},{"text":"This result suggests that infants **had learned the statistical regularities of the speech stream** and **were not merely picking up on simple frequency of occurrence**. Words and part-words were matched for frequency, so only their transitional probabilities could separate them.","children":["After a short exposure, infants pulled units out of a continuous stream using only how predictable the syllables were."]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"**Identifying word referents** is the problem that comes after segmentation. A **referent** is whatever a word refers to. **Now that children can “find” the words, how do they learn what they mean?** They need more evidence, because segmentation gives a recurring chunk of sound and says nothing about its meaning.","children":["A child may hear “cat” while looking at a cat.","Recognizing the sound pattern “cat” is one achievement, and knowing that it names the furry animal is a separate one."]},{"text":"Any scene holds many objects, parts, properties and actions. The learner cannot tell from the sound alone which of these the speaker has in mind, so the learner needs further evidence about reference.","children":["Segmentation solves the problem of grouping sounds, and a separate process links that group of sounds to what it names."]},{"text":"Learning a word means **linking its form to the speaker's intended meaning**. The child gathers that evidence from the situation in which the word is used.","children":["An infant can recognize a word form such as cat before knowing what it names."]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"**The inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings. **Induction** means reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and the rule here is the meaning of a word. A child hears a new word while watching a cat, and the word could mean many things.","children":["It might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, which are categories at different levels, or it might be **FELIX**, the name of this particular cat.","It might name a property such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**, an action such as **WALKING**, or a part such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**. Each candidate fits what the child sees, so nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends.","children":["Every one of these meanings fits the situation equally well."]},{"text":"This is a version of **Quine's “gavagai” problem**. In Quine's example, a linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by. “Gavagai” might mean rabbit, but it might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","children":["Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast, so something must narrow the possibilities.","**Joint attention**, **intentional cues** from the speaker and **default word learning strategies** do this narrowing."]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"**How do children acquire word meanings?** One way is through **joint attention**, which is **sharing an attentional focus with another person**. Two people look at the same object, and each knows that the other is attending to it too.","children":["When a child and an adult attend to the same object, a word spoken by the adult is likely to be about that object."]},{"text":"**Gaze following emerges around 9 months of age**. **Gaze following** means looking where someone else is looking. When an adult looks over at an object, the infant follows the adult's eyes to that object.","children":["Gaze following lets infants join the adult's focus."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**. The infant grasps that the adult's attention is aimed at something specific. If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent.","children":["Joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space** for that reason, because it ties the word to what the speaker is attending to.","Understanding that others attend to particular things is a foundation for learning that words refer to what speakers are attending to."]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"**Joint attention optimizes word learning**, according to **Baldwin and colleagues**. Two groups of infants heard a novel word while they played with an object. In the **joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby** labeled the object the baby was playing with. In the **no joint attention group**, a **voice from behind a screen** labeled the object the baby was playing with.","children":["In both groups the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","The groups differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"The result was that **18-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group**. Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough for learning.","children":["The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, so the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker."]},{"text":"Infants needed evidence that a **speaker meant the word to refer to that object**. The social exchange around the word supported learning, so joint attention does more than place a word next to an object.","children":["A word and an object occurring together did not produce learning without that evidence."]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"**How do children narrow the hypothesis space for new words?** One way is through **intentional cues from the speaker**, such as **eye gaze direction**. An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to.","children":["When a speaker looks toward one toy, the direction of the eyes marks which object the speaker is attending to."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to **16 to 17-month-olds** and **18 to 19-month-olds** in two conditions. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the **toy that the infant was focused on** while saying the new word. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a **toy in a bucket**, a different toy from the infant's, while saying the new word.","children":["In follow-in labeling the infant and the adult look at the same toy, so either cue points to the right object.","In discrepant labeling the infant's focus and the speaker's focus point to different toys."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition separates the infant's focus from the speaker's focus. Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy. Infants who follow the speaker's gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket.","children":["Comparing the two conditions tests whether infants treat the **speaker's gaze as evidence** of what the word means."]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**Gaze direction** helps a child work out which object a speaker is naming when several objects are present. A speaker who looks at one object while saying a new word ties the word to that object. The child can follow the speaker's eyes to the **intended referent**.","children":["A child who notices where the speaker looks can work out which of several objects a new word names."]},{"text":"A speaker's focus can be **different from the child's own focus**. Following it means using information about someone else's attention. The child can then attach a new word to an object the child was not looking at when the word was spoken.","children":["Gaze direction is a cue that works even when the child is focused elsewhere."]},{"text":"Gaze **narrows the possible meanings** although the sound of the word gives no clue. The child combines the new word with evidence of what the speaker intends.","children":["Following gaze rests on understanding that a speaker's eyes point to what the speaker means.","The result is a far more specific mapping than linking the word to everything in view."]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"Intentional cues from the speaker also include **affective and behavioral cues**. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions, and **behavioral cues** are the speaker's actions. An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was looking for.","children":["An action, such as reaching for something, can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that **infants used adults' facial expression to identify the intended referent of a novel word**. The adult's emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one the adult meant. Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult's expression picked out.","children":["These cues add to gaze as evidence of what the speaker means."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken, and the speaker's reaction can **single out one of them**. A satisfied expression signals that the speaker found the wanted object. A disappointed expression signals that an object is not the one the speaker meant.","children":["Word learning involves understanding the goal behind the interaction for that reason."]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"A speaker's **emotional reaction** works as an **intentional cue** that can pick out the intended object from several candidates. A reaction of satisfaction or excitement can tie one object to what the speaker was trying to find or do. The learner can use that tie to interpret a new label.","children":["A word may be said near several objects that are not all equally likely referents."]},{"text":"**Intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction**. Facial expressions and actions also reveal what a speaker means. The helpful information lies in how the adult responds to each object.","children":["Connecting the label to that response shrinks the **hypothesis space** of the word, which is the set of meanings it might have."]},{"text":"Infants who use emotional reactions to learn words are **following the speaker's goals**. They understand that the speaker is searching for something and that the word names the thing the speaker wants.","children":["The word attaches to the object that meets the goal.","Objects that disappoint the speaker are ruled out."]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"Children also bring **word learning strategies** to the task. These are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space for new words**, so children have a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** is one example. It leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**, not one of its parts, its color, its texture or an action it is doing.","children":["A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"This assumption **rules out many unlikely meanings at once**, so it speeds learning. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is another word learning strategy. **As a default, objects can have only one name**. The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","children":["Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, which is roughly between 18 and 24 months of age."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view. When the child hears a new word, the child takes it to mean the parrot, because the dog already has a name.","children":["The child does not use any gaze or emotional cue to make this choice.","The strategy uses the child's existing vocabulary to narrow the possibilities."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works together with the **whole object assumption**. The whole object assumption says that a new word names an object, and mutual exclusivity says that it names an object with no name yet.","children":["Mutual exclusivity is a default, so later learning can override it when an object turns out to have more than one name."]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"Under **mutual exclusivity**, a child who hears an unfamiliar word gives it to an object that **has no known name yet**. An object the child can already name is an unlikely target for a new word. The choice comes from comparing the objects that are in view.","children":["The child works out the meaning from the objects present and the words already known."]},{"text":"**Prior vocabulary** decides which objects count as already named. Two children in the same scene can guess differently if they know different words.","children":["The strategy builds directly on what the child has already learned."]},{"text":"Vocabulary growth **builds on itself** for that reason. A child who knows many names can rule out many objects. The unnamed objects that remain become the likeliest referents for new words.","children":["Every word the child knows helps narrow the meaning of the next new word."]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"**How do children start putting words together?** In production, it can be hard to tell what is going on when children produce only **one, two or three words at a time**. Very different processes can produce the same short utterance, so the question has no quick answer.","children":["A child might say “Up!” or “Pick you up!” or “Mommy up!”"]},{"text":"**Menn** distinguishes among four kinds of early utterances. The first is **“just a bunch of relevant words,”** where the child strings together words that fit the situation with no grammar linking them. The second is **formulas**, which are phrases memorized as whole units without the child knowing they have separate parts. The third is **word combinations**, in which the child joins separate words productively to make new combinations. The fourth is **stable constructions**, which are consistent patterns the child uses to build utterances and are **not necessarily adult-like**.","children":["“Pick you up!” sounds more advanced, but it may be a formula copied whole from adult speech.","“Mommy up!” is shorter, but it may be a true combination of two separate words."]},{"text":"Utterance length alone cannot tell which process produced an utterance. In practice, **a full description of the context** in which an utterance is produced is **necessary but often not sufficient** to distinguish among these.","children":["Context reveals what the child intends and how the utterance is used across situations.","Even with full context, the source of a given utterance can stay uncertain."]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"**Comprehension** and **production** develop at different rates. **Even children at the one-word stage in production are able to use basic transitive word order in comprehension**. In the **one-word stage** a child speaks only in single words.","children":["A **transitive** sentence has a verb with a subject and an object. English transitive order puts the actor first and the one acted on after the verb."]},{"text":"The **structure-mapping account** of **early syntactic bootstrapping**, from **Gertner and Fisher**, describes how children do this. **Syntactic bootstrapping** means using sentence structure to help learn meaning. From the input sentence “The boy is tickling the girl!” the child builds a **partial sentence representation** that records the verb tickle with boy as the first noun and girl as the second noun.","children":["From the observed scene of a boy tickling a girl, the child builds **conceptual representations** of possible events. These are giggling with the girl as agent, tickling with the boy as agent and the girl as patient, and playing with the boy or the girl as agent.","An **agent** performs an action, and a **patient** is affected by it."]},{"text":"A **one-to-one mapping between nouns and participant roles** lines up the two nouns with the two participants in the tickling event. The sentence has two nouns, so it fits the event with two participants, and the order of the nouns suggests who does what.","children":["A child who says only single words can still use noun order to tell who did what, so comprehension runs ahead of production.","Matching nouns to the participants in a scene helps the child work out what the verb means."]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"A **generalization test** checks whether children have truly learned the **transitive structure**. If children have really learned the transitive structure, they will **generalize it to new verbs**. A child who has only memorized particular sentences will be stuck with an unfamiliar verb, but a child who knows the structure can use word order with any verb.","children":["Choosing the scene that fits “The duck is gorping the bunny” from two novel actions requires knowing that the first noun is the doer and the second is the one acted on.","The verb “gorp” is made up, so only the order of the nouns can help."]},{"text":"**Gertner and colleagues (2006)** found that **two-year-olds can understand this**. Two scenes appeared side by side in each of two 8-second test trials. In one scene a person dressed as a duck pushed a person dressed as a bunny in a wagon, and in the other scene the bunny acted on the duck. Children **looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the rabbit** than at the reverse.","children":["Before the first trial, during a 5-second blank screen, children heard “Hey look! The duck’s gonna gorp the bunny!”","While the two scenes were in view, children heard “The duck is gorping the bunny! See?” as they watched.","Before the second trial, during a 4-second blank screen, children heard “The duck gorped the bunny! Find gorping!”"]},{"text":"Children at this age typically have a **mean length of utterance (MLU)** of **around 2**. MLU is the average number of **morphemes**, which are meaningful words or word parts, in each utterance. Their three-morpheme utterances might be like “Duck gorp bunny.”","children":["Their own speech is short and incomplete, yet they understand the full transitive structure, so comprehension of grammar runs ahead of production."]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"**Critical periods** and **sensitive or “optimal” periods** are two ways to describe how the timing of experience affects learning, and they differ in how strict the time limits are. A **critical period** has a **discrete, “programmed” onset and offset**, so the window opens and closes at fixed points in development. Change in response to the environment, which is learning, is **strictly limited to this period**, so there is **no sensitivity to the environment before or after** it.","children":["Experience outside the window has no effect at all, so a critical period predicts that late exposure produces no learning."]},{"text":"A **sensitive or “optimal” period** is a looser link between timing and learning. **Learning is possible throughout the life span**, but it is **optimal during particular windows**, when it is easiest or works best. These windows might have a **gradual onset and offset**, and their timing **can itself be affected by experience**.","children":["A sensitive period predicts that late learning is harder but still possible, with a lasting advantage for early learners."]},{"text":"The two ideas differ as impossibility differs from reduced effectiveness. Any late improvement counts against a strict critical period, while a lasting early advantage supports a sensitive period.","children":["Even the classic critical period in vision fits the term sensitive period better, and the same question comes up for the evidence on language learning."]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is **poor vision in one eye despite normal peripheral function**. **Peripheral function** means the working of the eye itself, so the eye can be healthy while vision through it stays poor. The trouble lies in how the brain handles the input from that eye.","children":[]},{"text":"Three conditions can lead to amblyopia. **Strabismus** is a misalignment of the eyes, so the two eyes point in different directions and the brain receives two conflicting images. A **cataract** is a clouding of the lens of the eye, so the light reaching the back of the eye is blocked or blurred. **Anisometropia** is a large difference in focusing power between the two eyes, so one eye's image is much blurrier than the other's.","children":["Each condition keeps the brain from getting normal visual input for some period of time."]},{"text":"**Periods of deprivation from normal visual input** caused by these conditions can result in **poor vision long after the conditions themselves have been remediated**, which means corrected. Straightening the eyes, removing the cataract or correcting the focus restores the input from the eye, but the brain may still fail to use that input well.","children":["A temporary problem early in life can leave a lasting effect, so the timing of the problem matters as much as the problem itself.","A cataract removed early may leave little lasting damage, while a cataract removed late may leave poor vision even after the lens is clear."]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"**Amblyopia occurs because of competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**. Both eyes send input to **visual cortex**, and the two inputs **compete** for control of the neurons there. When both eyes work normally, they share the cortical neurons in balance. **Abnormal vision in one eye** can lead to **“colonization”** of the cortical representation of that eye by the other eye, so the stronger eye takes over neurons that would have served the weaker eye.","children":[]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation**, which means blocking or degrading the input to one eye, reveals how the takeover unfolds in mice. The changes come in three steps over a little more than a week.","children":["Within **2 to 3 days** there is **increased spine motility**. **Dendritic spines** are the small projections where neurons receive connections, and increased motility means they become unstable and move around.","Within **3 to 4 days** there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration**. Connections from the deprived eye are lost, and **Wallerian degeneration** is the breakdown of nerve fibers that have been cut off from their source.","After **more than a week** there is **axon sprouting and new spine birth**, because the open eye's fibers grow new branches and form connections in the space the deprived eye lost."]},{"text":"When normal function is restored to the deprived eye, it **remains at a competitive disadvantage**, because the other eye now holds the connections. **Normal cortical responses cannot be restored by normal experience**, because ordinary viewing with both eyes open keeps favoring the stronger eye.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"**Treatments for amblyopia are based on competition**. One of the most effective treatments involves **intermittently patching the “good” eye**. A patch over the stronger eye blocks its input for part of the day, so the brain has to rely on the weaker eye.","children":["**Why patch the stronger eye?** The stronger eye is winning the competition in visual cortex, so covering it removes its advantage for part of the day."]},{"text":"Patching **restores the competitive balance between the eyes** and permits the brain's own **plasticity** to **restore normal function**. Plasticity is the brain's ability to change its connections in response to experience. With the strong eye covered, the weaker eye's input can win back cortical connections.","children":["The patch does not repair the eye itself. It changes the experience the brain receives, and the brain's own plasticity does the rest."]},{"text":"This treatment is **most effective early in development**, and it **may be ineffective by late childhood, around 10 years**. Young brains are highly plastic, so rebalancing the input leads to rapid reorganization. As the visual system matures, the same treatment has less and less effect.","children":["The timing of treatment therefore matters as much as the treatment itself."]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"**Levi and Polat (1996)** studied **human adult plasticity after the critical period**. Experiments with adults have begun to find that **plasticity is not abolished entirely, even well into adulthood**. Some of these patients were **in their 50s**, an age normally considered past the point where treatment for amblyopia works.","children":[]},{"text":"The adults did **long-term, intensive training on specific psychophysical tasks** with the affected eye. A **psychophysical task** measures the relationship between a physical stimulus and what a person perceives, such as the smallest contrast a person can detect. The training led to **improvements in both task performance and general visual function**, so the benefits spread beyond the trained task to vision more broadly.","children":["Training was counted in **kilotrials**, and one kilotrial is a thousand trials. The training ran from 0 to about 50 kilotrials."]},{"text":"Improvement was measured as a falling **threshold**, which is the smallest stimulus a person can reliably detect, so a lower threshold means better vision. The threshold changed in three stages as training went on.","children":["Over the first **19 kilotrials** the threshold **dropped steadily**, and this change was highly significant.","From about **20 to 35 kilotrials** the threshold **leveled off** with little further change.","Around **36 kilotrials** the threshold **dropped again** to a new, lower level and stayed there."]},{"text":"The adult visual system therefore keeps some **experience-dependent plasticity**. The right kind and amount of training can still produce change after ordinary late treatment stops working.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"**Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** found that **playing video games enhances visual acuity and visual attention in adult amblyopia**. The adults played an action video game, a first-person shooting game set in a battle scene, using their amblyopic eye.","children":["**Visual acuity** is the sharpness of vision, the ability to see fine detail, so better acuity means clearer vision through the weak eye.","**Visual attention** is the ability to select and focus on the relevant parts of a visual scene, such as targets in a busy battle."]},{"text":"Action games demand fast visual judgments and constant attention, so they push the visual system to use the amblyopic eye's input. The participants were adults whose childhood visual development was long over, so their improvement came from the training itself. Intensive visual experience improved their vision well after the age when patching usually works best.","children":[]},{"text":"Video game training adds to the evidence for **plasticity** in adult amblyopia. A demanding, engaging visual task can drive change in a mature visual system.","children":["An early optimal window can close without removing every possibility of improvement."]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"**Amblyopia is a classic example of a sensitive period**, and three facts about it support that. A **peripheral disturbance in early experience** can lead to **life-long disruption in visual function**, so an early problem with the eye itself permanently affects vision. The long-term loss of function is the **sequela** of a **temporary deficit during experience-dependent organization of the brain**. Treatment is **most effective during a limited developmental window**.","children":["A **sequela** is a lasting result of an earlier condition. The brain organized itself around the abnormal input, and that organization stays after the eye is corrected.","Patching works best early and loses its effectiveness by late childhood."]},{"text":"However, **new evidence suggests that the right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood**. Intensive perceptual training and video game play improved vision in adults. Later learning is harder and requires special conditions, but it still happens.","children":[]},{"text":"This pattern fits a **sensitive period** better than a strict critical period. A strict critical period would rule out any later improvement, and adults with amblyopia did improve.","children":["Amblyopia combines a strong early window with some capacity for change later in life, which is what defines a sensitive period."]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"**How can the vision evidence be related to language?** Two lines of work connect them. The first is **Lenneberg's** early attempts to synthesize **developmental neurology** with the notion of a **“language acquisition device”**. The second is **studies of early deprivation**, with data from **neglected children** and from **Deaf children with limited input to sign**.","children":["**Developmental neurology** is the study of how the nervous system develops. A **language acquisition device** is a proposed innate capacity for learning language."]},{"text":"Lenneberg asked whether changes in the developing brain could open and close a window for acquiring language. He linked language milestones to stages of brain development. If such a window exists, language learning depends on the state of the brain as well as on the input.","children":[]},{"text":"Deprivation studies look at people who first met an accessible language unusually late. Neglected children were cut off from normal language input, and some Deaf children have no access to sign language in early childhood.","children":["Comparing these cases with typical learners tests whether the age of first exposure affects the final outcome."]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"In **Biological Foundations of Language**, **Lenneberg (1967)** was attempting to synthesize **contemporary linguistics** with what was known about **brain development**. Studies of language development were starting to find a fairly robust and **stage-like pattern** of language development. Data from Austrian, British and American children between 8 and 36 months of age follow the same order.","children":["**Single words** appear first, mostly between about 10 and 18 months.","**Two-word phrases** come next, mostly between about 18 and 26 months.","**Sentences of five words or more** come last, between about 28 and 36 months."]},{"text":"Lenneberg paired these language milestones with motor milestones at each age. At each age, typical children reach a motor achievement and a language achievement on a similar schedule.","children":["At **8 months** infants stand holding on and pick up small objects, and **reduplicated** babbling, such as repeated syllables, becomes frequent.","At **10 months** infants creep efficiently and pull to standing, and their vocalizations mix with sound play such as gurgling. They try to imitate sounds and begin to tell words apart.","At **12 months** infants walk with one hand held, and words such as **mama** or **dada** emerge. They show definite signs of understanding some words and simple commands.","At **18 months** children have a definite vocabulary of more than three but fewer than fifty words, and comprehension progresses rapidly."]},{"text":"The idea that language might have **maturational milestones** was seen as consistent with the **unfolding of a biological program**. Maturational milestones are developmental achievements tied to physical growth. Language milestones line up with motor milestones, so the two seem to share a biological timetable.","children":["Lenneberg proposed that brain maturation sets the pace for both, so the timing of language learning depends on the developing brain."]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"In Lenneberg's time, **data on typical brain development were sparse**, because they were almost entirely based on **post-mortem analyses of tissue**. **Post-mortem** means that the tissue was studied after death. Researchers could not follow the same children's brains as they grew, so they had to piece development together from the brains of children who had died at different ages.","children":[]},{"text":"Measurements of **neuron size** give the cell body volume of neurons in layers III, IV and V of the cerebral cortex at different ages. Neuron size grows rapidly in the first two years and then levels off.","children":["**No data were available between ages 2 and 18**, so the measurements have a large gap. That gap overlaps much of the period of **language acquisition**."]},{"text":"Nonetheless, Lenneberg observed that **abrupt changes in neuron size and packing density before age two** might be **permissive for the beginning of language acquisition**. He made this argument from the measurements for very young children and for adults.","children":["**Packing density** is how closely neurons are crowded together in the tissue.","**Permissive** means these changes make language acquisition possible without directly causing it."]},{"text":"On this view, **the critical period seems to open after this initial period of rapid development**. The brain first goes through a burst of physical growth, and once that growth settles, the window for learning language opens.","children":["Lenneberg filled the gap in the data with a reasonable guess about how the brain matures, so later research would be needed to test it directly."]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"More extensive data were available from **studies of recovery from stroke**. **Aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions**, where a **lesion** is an area of damaged brain tissue. Damage that leaves an adult with lasting aphasia often has much milder effects on language in a child.","children":[]},{"text":"In extreme cases a **hemispherectomy** was performed, which is surgery that removes or disconnects one entire **cerebral hemisphere**, typically to relieve seizures in people with epilepsy. **Children in whom the left hemisphere is removed nonetheless seem to develop typical language abilities**. **Adults do not recover language after this procedure**.","children":[]},{"text":"Lenneberg's summary of hemispherectomy outcomes, based on **Basser (1962)**, sorts the cases by the age when the lesion was acquired. The outcome depends strongly on that age.","children":["Before the teens, **49** children with the **left** hemisphere removed had speech that was **not affected or improved**, and only **3** had permanent aphasia, all of whom had aphasia before the operation.","Before the teens, **38** children with the **right** hemisphere removed had speech not affected or improved, and 5 had permanent aphasia, all of whom had aphasia before the operation.","During puberty, a single case of left removal had **slow improvement**, with some residual aphasia to the end of life, 27 months after surgery.","Among **adults** with the **left** hemisphere removed, **none** had speech unaffected or improved, and **6** had **permanent aphasia**. Among adults with the right hemisphere removed, 25 had speech unaffected or improved, and none had permanent aphasia."]},{"text":"These outcomes, among other things, led Lenneberg to hypothesize that a key factor in **closing the critical period** was the development of adult-like **cerebral dominance**. Cerebral dominance is the specialization of one hemisphere, usually the left, for language.","children":["In young children language is not yet fixed in the left hemisphere, so the right hemisphere can take over. Once dominance is established in adulthood, the right hemisphere can no longer take over, so removing the left hemisphere leaves permanent aphasia."]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"**As always, things are more complicated.** Systematic studies of children with **perinatal strokes** add important detail to the findings on recovery. A perinatal stroke happens between the **prenatal period and 28 days after birth**. Outcomes for these children are **clearly much better than for adults with similar injuries**, but the children still have some effects of the injury.","children":[]},{"text":"The first complication is that **early language deficits appear in both right-hemisphere and left-hemisphere patients**. In adults, language problems mainly follow damage to the left hemisphere. In young children, damage to either side causes early language delays.","children":["**Some deficits are worse for children with right-hemisphere damage**, which is the opposite of the adult pattern."]},{"text":"The second complication is that **language outcomes are generally in the low-normal range**. That suggests a **persistent delay rather than a catching up**. Children recover far better than adults, but they often do not reach the level of children without injury.","children":["Early plasticity is therefore real but limited. A young brain can reorganize to support language, but the reorganized system may not work as well as the original one would have."]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied the **effects of early deprivation** through the case of **Genie**. Genie was born in **April 1957**. When researchers first met her she was **13 years and 7 months old**, a painfully thin child who looked six or seven.","children":[]},{"text":"Her early life involved **extreme isolation and abuse**. **From the age of 20 months** she was confined to a small room under conditions of increasing physical restraint. Most of the time she was harnessed to an infant's potty chair, and otherwise she was confined in a homemade sleeping bag in an infant's crib covered with wire mesh.","children":["She received minimal care from a mother who was rapidly losing her sight, and she was fed only infant food.","She was **physically punished by her father if she made any sounds**. Her father and older brother did not speak to her, but at times they barked at her like dogs.","There was no television or radio in the home, and her contact with other people was extremely limited."]},{"text":"When she was hospitalized for malnutrition, Genie **could not stand up straight or chew food** and was **not toilet trained**. She **did not speak, cry or produce any vocal sounds**.","children":["Her case is a test of what happens when first-language learning starts in adolescence.","Her lack of language came together with severe physical, social and emotional deprivation, so the effect of missing language input cannot be separated from her other hardships."]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"**What did Genie learn** during the **four years** that her language was observed? Genie **learned quite a bit** in that time. She came to **comprehend hundreds of words**. She came to comprehend **some aspects of morphology**, and she was **particularly good with plurals**. She also came to comprehend **some prepositions**.","children":["**Morphology** is the system of meaningful word parts, such as the plural ending, so she learned to use word endings to tell one from many.","Prepositions are words for spatial relationships between objects, so her learning went beyond names for objects."]},{"text":"A test of prepositions used **buttons and plastic boxes** of different colors and sizes, and Genie had to move them according to spoken instructions. A sample item was “Put the red box behind the blue box.” The test covered **under, over, in, on, behind** and **in front of**, along with **in back of** and **next to**.","children":[]},{"text":"Between October 1972 and August 1973, Genie gave **more correct than incorrect responses for every preposition**. Her scores were strongest for in and on.","children":["**In** had 37 correct and 4 incorrect, and **on** had 36 correct and 9 incorrect.","**Over** had 11 correct and 2 incorrect, and **under** had 17 correct and 7 incorrect.","**In front of** had 20 correct and 6 incorrect, **in back of** had 7 correct and 3 incorrect, **next to** had 7 correct and 1 incorrect, and **behind** had 12 correct and 5 incorrect."]},{"text":"Some language can therefore be **acquired late**. This learning took place after puberty, long after the window Lenneberg proposed, but it was **uneven** across different parts of language.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"**What didn't Genie learn** in the years she was observed? Genie's **production lagged severely behind her comprehension**. **Some lag is typical of development**, because all children understand more than they can say. Genie had also been **punished for making sounds** as a child, which could limit how much she spoke.","children":["Her speech therefore gave away less than she actually knew."]},{"text":"Her **grammatical abilities developed much more slowly than her vocabulary**. In a test of **conjunctions**, **five familiar objects in a row** were placed in front of her, and she was asked to point to one or more of them. The items included “Show me the fork and pencil,” “Show me the crayon or the knife” and “Show me either the spoon or the crayon.”","children":["**And** requires pointing to every named object. **Or** requires pointing to just one of them, which depends on understanding a logical relationship."]},{"text":"In her **early responses** Genie treated **or** sentences the same as **and** sentences, so she pointed to both objects. Her **later responses** distinguished and from or, but she still did not understand the meaning of **either/or**.","children":["Between October 1971 and December 1973, **and** sentences produced **100 correct**, 3 incorrect and 0 unanalyzable responses.","**Or** sentences produced only **7 correct**, **51 incorrect** and 6 unanalyzable responses."]},{"text":"Genie grasped and almost perfectly but failed or almost entirely, despite a vocabulary of hundreds of words. Her strong vocabulary and weak grammar fit the idea that **grammar depends more on early exposure than vocabulary does**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"Genie's case leaves **lots of questions**. One question is **whether Genie was learning disabled**. She might have had difficulties from birth that would have affected her language even without deprivation. Another question is **what the residual impacts of neglect and abuse were on her social development**. Severe trauma could affect learning in many ways beyond the lack of language input.","children":["Disability, abuse and late exposure to language could each affect grammar learning, and no single case can separate them."]},{"text":"A further limit is that **after four years, funding to study her was pulled**. Nobody can know **what she might have learned after a longer time**.","children":["The evidence covers only what she learned within those four years."]},{"text":"Nonetheless, the **disparity between her vocabulary development and her grammatical development** is widely interpreted as suggestive that **grammar learning in a first language is specifically impaired after a sensitive period**. She learned hundreds of words but made little progress with grammar. On this interpretation, vocabulary can be learned at any age, while grammar depends on early exposure.","children":["Her pattern of strong vocabulary and weak grammar became an influential piece of evidence for a sensitive period in first-language learning."]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** studied deaf people who acquired a first language late. **Many deaf children do not get sign language input** but have an **otherwise typical upbringing**. Unlike Genie, these children grow up in ordinary, caring homes, so their only unusual experience is a lack of accessible language early in life.","children":["That makes them a cleaner test of first-language timing."]},{"text":"**Children aged 9 to 15 have difficulty learning American Sign Language (ASL)** if this is their **first time learning a language**. A **comparison group** of children who went deaf after initially learning a spoken language **do much better**. Both groups learned ASL late, but only one group had an earlier language. **These differences persist into adulthood**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two tasks measured the outcome in adults. One task tested ASL and the other tested English.","children":["ASL performance was measured by **recall of complex ASL sentences**. Deaf adults with **no early language** scored about **36%**, while deaf adults with an **early spoken language** scored about **78%**.","English performance was measured by **judgments of whether complex English sentences were grammatically correct**, where chance is 50%. Deaf adults with **no early language** scored about **52%**, which is close to chance. Deaf adults with an **early signed language**, ASL in infancy, scored about 80%, and hearing adults with an early spoken language other than English also scored about 80%."]},{"text":"The advantage comes from **having any language early**, whatever its type. Early ASL helped later English learning as much as an early spoken language did, and people with no early language struggled with both ASL and English.","children":["Early accessible language, whether signed or spoken, provides the foundation for learning languages later."]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"**So, is there a “critical period” or a “sensitive period” for L1?** The evidence supports age effects in learning a first language, or **L1**, but it leaves the exact nature of the window uncertain.","children":["The window does not wait for language to arrive, and the unusual cases make a firm answer hard to reach."]},{"text":"**Recovery from stroke, and certainly hemispherectomy, looks very, very different in childhood and adulthood**. Children can develop language after damage that would leave adults with permanent aphasia. Age therefore has a strong effect on the brain's ability to reorganize for language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Deprivation of input does not appear to extend the critical period**. It might seem that a child without language input would keep the window open until input arrives. Instead, Genie and the deaf adults who learned sign late both have lasting difficulty, especially with grammar.","children":["The window seems to follow a maturational schedule, whether or not language has been experienced."]},{"text":"**All of these data are from highly unusual cases with lots of other things going on**, so it is **difficult** to reach firm conclusions. Stroke, brain surgery, abuse and neglect each bring complications beyond the timing of language input.","children":["There is also a **lack of animal models**, because animals do not learn human language.","Experiments such as **gene knockouts**, which disable specific genes to test their role, cannot be done with people."]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"**Babies are overrated** as language learners. When people talk about **sensitive periods** for a second language, or **L2**, they often say that infants, toddlers and children are “better” at learning language than adults, and it is important not to get carried away with that idea. A typical child by 18 months uses at least 20 words, understands “in and out” and “off and on,” and answers simple questions such as “What’s that?” with words or gestures.","children":["Imagine an adult who only knew about 20 words after 18 months of language instruction. That adult would be considered a very poor learner."]},{"text":"**Adults initially learn more quickly than children**. This is due largely to **transfer from L1**, the first language, and to **better general cognitive skills**.","children":["Transfer lets adults build on what they already know about how language works. General cognitive skills include memory, attention and reasoning."]},{"text":"Where adults do worse is **ultimate attainment**, which is the final level of skill a learner eventually reaches. Adults **rarely become native-like** in a second language, and this may also be due in part to transfer.","children":["The first language that helps adults start quickly may also hold them back from native-like skill. Speed of early learning and final level of success are different measures, so children and adults compare differently on each."]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"Some of the **language milestones** of a toddler are actually **general conceptual abilities**. Examples are the ability to deal with **spatial relations between objects** and the ability to form **event representations**, which are mental representations of who does what in an event. A toddler who reaches these milestones is building new ideas about the world as well as new words.","children":["Understanding “in and out” and “off and on” depends on understanding spatial relations between objects. Pretend play, such as giving teddy a drink, depends on event representations."]},{"text":"**Later learners of a second language** have a lot more of this conceptual knowledge in place. Because the concepts are already there, later learners can focus on learning how those concepts are represented in the **target language**, which is the language being learned.","children":["An adult learning Spanish already understands what “in” and “on” mean as concepts. The job of the adult is mainly to learn how the new language expresses them, which is a smaller job than building concepts and words together."]},{"text":"Later learners also **already know another language**, and that has an impact on how the second language is learned. The first language provides ready-made ways of expressing concepts.","children":["Some of those ways fit the second language and help, while others clash with it and cause errors."]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer** is the use of native language (L1) processes and representations for a second language (L2). A learner comes to the L2 with a fully working language system already in place. Because of that, the learner brings L1 habits for hearing sounds, building sentences and finding words into the L2.","children":["Every habit built for the first language comes along into the second, whether it helps or hurts."]},{"text":"Transfer can have both **positive and negative impacts** on second-language learning and performance. **Positive transfer** happens when an L1 pattern matches what the L2 requires, so shared sounds, words and structures give the learner a head start. **Negative transfer** happens when an L1 pattern conflicts with the L2.","children":["With negative transfer the learner makes errors or finds the L2 pattern hard to learn. The errors appear where the two languages differ, because the habits of the learner point the wrong way there."]},{"text":"A cartoon of two men at a bar has one of them asking “Do you not be happy with me as the translator of the books of you?” The sentence uses English words, but its structure follows the grammar of another language.","children":["Word-by-word translation carries over the word order and phrasing of the first language, so the sentence is an example of negative transfer in grammar."]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"Transfer affects **phonetic and phonological learning**, which is the learning of speech sounds. **Categorical perception** leads to **acquired similarity** for sounds in the same category. Acquired similarity means that sounds a language groups into one category come to sound more alike through experience with that language.","children":["Sounds from many, many languages can be heard at the UCLA phonetics archive."]},{"text":"Hindi has **minimal pairs** that make the problem clear, where a minimal pair is two words that differ in only one sound. In each pair one word has a **dental** consonant, made with the tongue against the teeth, and the other has a **retroflex** consonant, made with the tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth.","children":["Two words both sound like “tap” to English ears, and one means hot while the other means a kind of beat. Two words both sound like “dab,” and one means pressure while the other means a kind of fruit. Two words both sound like “ari,” and one means saw, the tool, while the other means crooked."]},{"text":"**Native English speakers** tend to use the English **alveolar** category to interpret both dental and retroflex consonants. An alveolar consonant, such as English /t/ or /d/, is made with the tongue on the ridge just behind the teeth. English has only the alveolar category, so both Hindi sounds get mapped onto it and seem the same.","children":["In this case **transfer makes learning the L2 contrast more difficult**. The English category was learned successfully, but it now hides a difference that Hindi uses to tell words apart."]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"Transfer also affects **grammar learning**. **Classifiers** are a somewhat unusual grammatical feature across languages. A classifier is a word that must appear between a number or demonstrative and a noun, and it is chosen according to the kind of thing the noun names.","children":["The structure is a demonstrative pronoun, then a nominal classifier, then a noun, as in **yí gè rén**, “one person,” where **gè** is the classifier."]},{"text":"Chinese has many classifiers, and each one is used with a particular kind of noun. **Gè** is the general classifier, **bǎ** is used with objects that have a handle, **běn** with books and similar objects, and **kuài** with objects that are divided into pieces. **Liàng** is used with wheeled vehicles, **tái** with devices and gadgets, **zhāng** with flat objects, and **zhī** with long, thin objects.","children":["**Xiē** is used to indicate an indeterminate amount, or with the plural of pronouns and demonstratives."]},{"text":"**Native English speakers learning Chinese have difficulty learning classifiers**. In English, when we want to say “a chair,” “that dog,” “the car” or “one book,” we do not worry much about noun semantics when picking a determiner or pronoun. In Chinese, different classifiers are required by different nouns, so the learner must notice properties such as shape and function.","children":["One way to think about this is that English speakers have **learned to ignore the different cues for classifiers** as part of learning English grammar. Learning Chinese requires paying attention again to cues that English taught them to ignore."]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender** is another example where transfer causes problems. Grammatical gender is **different from social or cultural gender**, because it is a system that sorts nouns into classes, often called masculine and feminine. There is nothing “male” about a book or “female” about liberty, yet in French **le livre**, the book, is masculine and **la liberté**, liberty, is feminine.","children":["Other masculine examples are le cheval (the horse), le chien (the dog) and le bruit (the noise). Other feminine examples are la colombe (the dove), la chemise (the shirt) and la maison (the house)."]},{"text":"Grammatical gender has implications for **agreement**, which means that related words must match the gender of the noun. Pronouns, adjectives and some forms of verbs all need to be coordinated with noun gender. English has no grammatical gender on nouns, so English speakers have no habit of tracking it.","children":["A learner has to know the gender of each noun in order to use the right forms of all the related words."]},{"text":"French is famous for having **synonyms with different gender**. **Auto** and **voiture** both mean car, **rivière** and **fleuve** both mean river, and **vélo** and **bicyclette** both mean bicycle, and in each pair one word is masculine and the other is feminine. Words with the same meaning can have different genders, so gender has to be **learned word by word**.","children":["Common word endings give clues to gender. Masculine nouns often end in -age, -r, -t or -isme, as in le fromage (the cheese), le professeur (the teacher), le chat (the cat) and le capitalisme. Feminine nouns often end in -ie, -ion, -ité or -tié, -ance, or -nne, -mme and -lle, as in la boulangerie (the bakery), la nation, la fraternité (brotherhood), la balance (the scales) and la fille (the girl)."]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"**Transfer is not all bad**. Vocabulary learning in some L1 and L2 situations is aided by the **similarity of words across languages**. French **auto** resembles English “auto” and refers to a car, French **rivière** resembles English “river,” and French **bicyclette** resembles English “bicycle.”","children":["Japanese **コロッケ (korokke)** refers to a croquette, and Japanese **モノレール (monoreru)** refers to a monorail."]},{"text":"Sometimes the similarity is due to **shared etymology**, where etymology is the history of where a word comes from. Words with a shared origin are called **cognates**. French rivière and English river are cognates because both come from the same older word.","children":["Sometimes the similarity is due to more recent **“borrowing.”** A **loan word** is a word that one language takes from another, and Japanese korokke and monoreru were borrowed from European languages with their sounds adapted to Japanese."]},{"text":"Cognates and loan words give **positive transfer** for vocabulary. The learner already knows a similar form with a similar meaning, so the new word comes with a familiar anchor.","children":["A learner who knows river can quickly link it to rivière, because the two words share sounds and meaning. That link makes the new word easier to store and to recall."]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"**Not all similar-sounding words are cognates**. The **bias** to interpret L2 words as related to similar-sounding L1 words can be very strong, and sometimes the result is embarrassing. A word that looks or sounds like a word in another language but means something different is called a **false friend**.","children":["Spanish “Arnold está embarazado” does not mean that Arnold is embarrassed. Spanish **embarazado** means **pregnant**, so an English speaker relying on the similar sound of embarrassed would get the meaning badly wrong."]},{"text":"False friends reveal how automatically learners link L2 words to similar L1 words. The link happens before the learner checks the meaning, so a familiar-sounding word is assumed to mean the familiar thing.","children":["Embarazado sounds like embarrassed, so English speakers assume that it means embarrassed."]},{"text":"The same tendency drives both **helpful and harmful transfer**. When the meanings match, as with cognates, the link speeds up learning. When the meanings differ, the same link leads to errors.","children":["A helpful cognate and a misleading false friend differ only in whether the meanings actually match."]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** tested in the lab whether people **use cognates** in “second language” learning. They used **paired-associate learning** of made-up words. In paired-associate learning, participants study pairs of items and later have to recall one item when they are given the other.","children":["The participants were Dutch speakers. The Dutch words stood in for their first language, and the made-up words stood in for a new second language."]},{"text":"The made-up words varied in their **similarity to their Dutch associates**, so some were cognate-like and some were not. They also varied in **concreteness**. A concrete word names something that can be seen or touched, and an abstract word names an idea.","children":["Crossing the two factors gave four kinds of pairs, so the effect of each factor can be measured separately. The pairs were **gitaar** and **gettar** (“guitar,” concrete, cognate), **tijger** and **duunze** (“tiger,” concrete, non-cognate), **domein** and **domaan** (“domain,” abstract, cognate), and **herstel** and **groop** (“repair,” abstract, non-cognate)."]},{"text":"Participants were simply presented with pairs of Dutch words and their non-word paired associates and asked to learn these pairs. They were then **tested a week later on retention**.","children":["Retention means how well they remembered the pairs after a delay without further study."]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** found that **cognates are learned more rapidly and also remembered more easily**. Recall was tested over three training sessions, and session 4 came after one week with no training. Recall increased across the three training sessions for every kind of word, but the starting points were very different.","children":["**Concrete cognates** were recalled 69.7% of the time in session 1, and **concrete non-cognates** started at only 43.7%. **Abstract cognates** started at 42.7%, and **abstract non-cognates** started lowest at just 19.0%. By session 3 concrete cognates reached 93.4%, concrete non-cognates 92.0%, abstract cognates 86.0% and abstract non-cognates 72.7%."]},{"text":"The week without training separated the kinds of words again. Concrete cognates dropped only to 78.4% and concrete non-cognates to 69.0%. Abstract cognates dropped to 68.4%, while abstract non-cognates fell sharply to **36.0%**.","children":["Responses were also faster for cognates in every session. In session 1 concrete cognates took about 1,880 milliseconds and abstract non-cognates took about 3,641 milliseconds."]},{"text":"This is evidence that **cognate status can contribute to the very earliest stages of word learning** in a second language. The cognate advantage was largest in the first session, when learning had just begun, and it reappeared after a week, when non-cognates were forgotten more.","children":["Concreteness also had a large effect, and the two factors add together, so abstract non-cognates were the hardest of all. One explanation for concreteness effects is called **context availability**, the idea that concrete words are easier to link with a supporting context."]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"**Is this a good experiment?** It is, because **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** did not rely only on a test that cognates could pass without real learning. If they had only tested **receptive vocabulary**, meaning how easily participants could produce the Dutch word given its associate, the result would not be very impressive. In receptive testing the learner sees the new word and has to supply the familiar Dutch word.","children":["For cognate-like pairs the new word already looks like the Dutch word, so a participant could answer correctly by noticing the resemblance without having learned the pair."]},{"text":"The meaning of **gitaar**, **tijger** and **domein** might be guessed without any Dutch training, because they resemble English guitar, tiger and domain. The concrete cognates in the study were just as close to their Dutch partners.","children":["The pairs included broer and breur, brood and brodde, jongen and jange, lepel and lippel, lichaam and lechem, naald and nadel, paraplu and parredu, and rivier and riffer. They also included schaar and skare, stier and stire, trein and trinen, varken and vark, viool and vialo, and vrouw and vrauk."]},{"text":"The study also involved a test of **expressive vocabulary**, which the authors call **productive testing**. In productive testing the learner sees the Dutch word and has to produce the new made-up word. The learner has to remember the exact new form, such as gettar, out of many similar possibilities.","children":["Productive testing removes the shortcut, so a cognate advantage there reflects **real learning of new forms**."]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"**What did participants actually learn in the study?** They learned the new word forms themselves, and **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** could tell because they compared **productive testing** with **receptive testing**. Both were measured immediately after learning, in session 3, and at retest a week later, in session 4. The measure that matters is how much recall dropped over the week.","children":["Productive scores were lower than receptive scores, because generating a new form is harder than recognizing a familiar one."]},{"text":"In **productive testing**, learners had to produce the new word. Concrete words fell from 92.7% to 73.7%, a drop of 19.0 points, and abstract words fell from 79.3% to 52.2%, a drop of 27.1 points. Cognates fell from 89.7% to 73.3%, a drop of **16.4 points**, while non-cognates fell from 82.3% to 52.5%, a drop of **29.8 points**.","children":["In **receptive testing**, learners had to give the Dutch word. Concrete words dropped 8.8 points, from 97.7% to 88.9%, and abstract words dropped 26.6 points, from 91.5% to 64.9%. Cognates dropped only **7.8 points**, from 98.5% to 90.7%, while non-cognates dropped **27.7 points**, from 90.7% to 63.0%."]},{"text":"Cognates and concrete words were **forgotten less in both directions**, so their advantage extends from initial learning to long-term retention. The cognate advantage held up in productive testing, where the learner had to recall the new form.","children":["Similarity to a known word helped learners form a lasting memory of the new word."]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"**Are similar L1 words always activated when processing L2?** This is a central research question, and there have been many, many studies of it. When a bilingual person hears a word in the second language, words in the first language that sound similar might also become active. That activation could happen automatically, even when the person is trying to use only the second language.","children":["If L1 words become active even then, the two languages are never fully separate in the mind."]},{"text":"One nice example comes from a **visual world experiment** by **Marian and Spivey (2003)**. In a visual world experiment, participants hear spoken instructions while viewing a display of objects, and their eye movements are recorded.","children":["Looks toward an object whose L1 name sounds like the spoken L2 word would reveal that the L1 word was activated."]},{"text":"Eye movements are useful here because they reveal activation without asking the person to report it. The participant still goes on to choose the correct object, so the looks capture what was active along the way.","children":["The answer from this experiment is that Russian L1 words were activated while native Russian speakers listened to English, their L2."]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"This kind of experiment was used earlier for **categorical perception** by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)**. They studied gradient effects of within-category **voice onset time (VOT)** on **lexical access**. Participants saw a display with a lamp, a ship, a bear and a pear, and they heard a word from a continuum between “bear” and “pear.”","children":["Bear and pear differ only in their first consonant, so they compete during recognition. The lamp and the ship are unrelated fillers."]},{"text":"**Eye movements** can tell us about **competition** between the possible responses that participants are considering. A participant could click on the bear after having looked briefly at the pear. Those looks mean that “pear” was active as a competitor.","children":["Eye tracking therefore measures how strongly an alternative word is active, even when the final choice is correct."]},{"text":"The amount of competition between BEAR and PEAR seemed to **depend on distance from the category boundary**. Sounds closer to the boundary between /b/ and /p/ produced more looks to the competitor, and sounds farther from the boundary produced fewer.","children":["Applying the same method across languages lets researchers ask whether a word in one language competes with a similar word in another."]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds. Participants viewed a grid containing pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","children":["The display included a **beaker**, which was the target, a **beetle**, which shares its beginning with beaker, a **speaker**, which rhymes with beaker, and a **carriage**, which is unrelated."]},{"text":"The measure was the probability of fixating each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the target word began. Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, because both fit the first sounds of the word. Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 at around 500 milliseconds and then fell once the sounds that distinguish the words arrived. Looks to the beaker kept rising and approached 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","children":["Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, with a peak near 0.1, because its overlap comes at the end of the word. Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The cohort effect is this **early competition** from words that share a beginning with the target. Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds, and they drop out when the input stops matching them.","children":["Eye movements track this rise and fall moment by moment, even when listeners end up choosing correctly."]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** examined the cohort effect with **cross-language competitors**. A cross-language competitor is an object whose name in the other language shares its beginning with the spoken target word. They hypothesized that if **L1 words are always activated**, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is “speaker” in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches. If Russian is active while the participant listens to English, the matches should attract looks."]},{"text":"For English targets, each target was paired with an English competitor and a Russian competitor. **Speaker** was paired with spear and spichki (matches), boot with book and buben (tambourine), and shark with shovel and sharik (balloon). Chair was paired with chess set and cherepaha (turtle), marker with marbles and marka (stamp), and barbed wire with bark and barhat (velvet). Plug was paired with plum and platye (dress), gun with gum and gaika (nut), and lock with lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish).","children":["For Russian targets the pairing was reversed. Russian spichki was paired with English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles), marka with marker and marlya (cheese cloth), and platye with plum and plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The connection between a target and its cross-language competitor is **only in sound**, because speakers and matches share no meaning. Both competitors overlap with the target in sound, but only the overlap of the Russian competitor depends on knowing Russian.","children":["Looks to the matches during an English task are therefore a direct measure of the activation of spichki."]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** first had Russian-English bilinguals follow instructions in English, which tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**. The measure was the percent of trials on which participants looked at each competitor. When no competitor was present, participants looked at a control filler object on only **7%** of trials.","children":["When only the **between-language competitor** was present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials. When only the **within-language competitor** was present, participants also looked at it on 18% of trials."]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to **both within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent**. The Russian name of an object pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did.","children":["When both competitors were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more, on 19% of trials. They **still looked at the between-language competitor a lot**, on 13% of trials, which is about twice as often as in the control condition."]},{"text":"These results mean that **the first language stays active during second-language listening**. Bilinguals keep their L1 active while they process their L2.","children":["They still select the correct object, but L1 words compete along the way."]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"The same bilinguals in **Marian and Spivey (2003)** then did the experiment in Russian, which tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**. When only the between-language competitor was present, which was an object with a similar English name, participants looked at it on **8%** of trials. With no competitor, looks to the control filler happened on **5%** of trials.","children":["When only the within-language competitor was present, participants looked at it on 14% of trials. When both were present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 8% of trials and at the within-language competitor on 13%."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do this experiment in Russian. The small numerical difference between the between-language and control conditions, 8% compared with 5%, is **not statistically significant**. A difference that is not statistically significant is within the range of chance variation.","children":["The within-language competitor still produced a reliable cohort effect in Russian."]},{"text":"The pattern across the two experiments is **asymmetric**. Russian words became active during English tasks, but English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","children":["The asymmetry fits the idea that the first language is more strongly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation is the idea that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it. The model has an **L1 lexicon**, which is the first-language vocabulary, an **L2 lexicon**, which is the second-language vocabulary, and a store of **concepts**, which holds meanings.","children":["**Lexical links** connect the L1 and L2 words directly. **Conceptual links** connect each lexicon to the concepts."]},{"text":"The L1 lexicon is assumed to be **larger** and **more strongly connected to relevant concepts**. The conceptual link between L1 and the concepts is strong, while the conceptual link between L2 and the concepts is weak.","children":["First-language words therefore have strong links to their meanings, and second-language words have weaker links to their meanings."]},{"text":"The L2 lexicon is assumed to have **strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon**. The lexical link from L2 to L1 is strong and the lexical link from L1 to L2 is weak, so **activation can spread more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word** than the other way. This occurs because this direction is emphasized in learning.","children":["When people learn a second language, they usually learn each new word by connecting it to its L1 translation. The link from L2 words directly to concepts stays weaker, at least early in learning, and it can strengthen as proficiency grows."]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** can explain the results of **Marian and Spivey (2003)**. Consider what happens in the model when the task is performed in L2, with the target “speaker.” The concept for “matches” **partially activates the Russian word for “matches,”** spichki, because L1 words are strongly connected to their concepts.","children":["Spichki is also partly activated by the sounds of the English word speaker, since the two words begin alike. The link between speaker and spichki is only sound overlap, because speakers and matches share no meaning."]},{"text":"This activity **feeds back to the conceptual activation** for “matches.” The extra activation of spichki strengthens the concept of matches. Conceptual activation for “matches” then leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**, which is the matches.","children":["Strong L1 conceptual links are what let a Russian word drive the eyes toward its object."]},{"text":"When the task is done in L1, we might imagine that the **conceptual links with L2 are too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably. In the Russian version, an English competitor would need strong links from the English word to its concept. In the model, L2 words have only weak conceptual links, so they cannot drive looks to their objects.","children":["The unequal strength of the links explains why the effect appears in one direction and not in the other."]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**. A balanced bilingual is a person who is about equally skilled in both languages. One might expect a balanced bilingual to work like “two monolinguals in one,” with each language running separately, but the model predicts the opposite.","children":["The reason is that in a balanced bilingual the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**. Every concept strongly activates its word in both languages, so both vocabularies become active whenever either one is used."]},{"text":"Because both words are active, **competitive dynamics and inhibition will be engaged all the time**. Competitive dynamics means that the words of the two languages constantly compete for selection. **Inhibition** is the suppression of the word from the language that is not being used.","children":["The bilingual must constantly select the right language and suppress the other."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on the advantages of bilinguals in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks. Cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","children":["If bilinguals constantly practice suppressing one language, they might become better at controlling attention in general, including in tasks that have nothing to do with language."]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"So far we have considered one particular **sequence of second-language learning**. A person begins learning L1, later learns L2, and at the same time continues learning and using L1. Both languages are kept in use from then on.","children":["In this sequence the first language is learned early and is also kept in use."]},{"text":"**But what happens when you stop using L1 after L2 is introduced?** The answer from adopted adults and childhood overhearers is that L2 can appear to completely replace L1, although some residual knowledge may remain that later learning can unmask. In this second sequence the person begins learning L1 and later learns L2, but does not continue learning or using L1.","children":["L1 is no longer heard or used, while L2 takes over."]},{"text":"This situation separates the effect of **learning a language early** from the effect of **continuing to use it**, which usually go together. If early learning leaves a permanent foundation, traces of L1 should remain even without use. If continued use is needed, L1 might fade or disappear.","children":["Comparing the two sequences reveals whether early learning alone leaves a lasting mark."]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Pallier, Dehaene, Poline, LeBihan, Argenti, Dupoux and Mehler** studied brain imaging of **language plasticity in adopted adults**, and they asked whether a second language can replace the first. They tested adults aged 20 to 32 who had been adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between the ages of 3 and 8. These adults had learned Korean as young children and then moved into homes where only French was spoken.","children":["They stopped using Korean entirely after adoption. By adulthood they had spoken only French for many years, so they are a natural test of what happens when L1 use stops."]},{"text":"The researchers tested whether the adoptees had any **residual knowledge of Korean**. Residual knowledge is any remaining trace of the earlier language. Any Korean that survived would reveal what early learning leaves behind when the language is no longer used.","children":["Monolingual French speakers with no Korean experience served as the comparison group. If the adoptees did better than the French speakers on Korean-related tasks, some Korean knowledge would have survived."]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"Korean has a **three-way stop contrast**. A **stop** is a consonant made by fully blocking the airflow and then releasing it, such as /p/, /t/ or /k/. English and French distinguish two kinds of stops at each place in the mouth, such as /b/ and /p/, while Korean distinguishes three kinds at each place.","children":["The three kinds are **aspirated**, **plain** and **tense**. An aspirated stop is released with a strong puff of air, a plain stop has a lighter release, and a tense stop is produced with a stiffened, tightened throat."]},{"text":"The contrast appears in three series, in recordings from the UCLA Phonetics Archive. The **bilabial** series, made with the lips, has **pʰul** “grass,” **pul** “fire” and **p’ul** “horn.” The **dental** series, made with the tongue at the teeth, has **tʰal** “mask,” **tal** “moon” and **t’al** “daughter.” The **velar** series, made with the back of the tongue, has **kʰo** “nose,” **kok** “music” and **k’ok** “exactly.”","children":["The three words in each series are made at the same place in the mouth and differ only in the type of stop. A listener must hear the three-way difference to tell the words apart."]},{"text":"The contrast is a sensitive test of **retained Korean**. French speakers have no such contrast, so they tend to hear the three Korean sounds as two, while a Korean speaker hears all three as different.","children":["Adoptees who kept any Korean sound knowledge should hear the differences better than French speakers do."]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"**Ventureyra and colleagues** found that Korean adoptees in France were **no better than monolingual French speakers** at the Korean contrasts. Adoptees, French native speakers and Korean native speakers heard pairs of sounds, and the measure was how often each group responded “different.”","children":["For the **plain and tense** contrast for p and k, Korean speakers responded “different” on about 96% of trials, while adoptees did so on about 8% and French speakers on about 3%. For the **plain and aspirated** contrast for p and k, Korean speakers scored about 90%, adoptees about 19% and French speakers about 26%."]},{"text":"The other Korean contrasts gave the same pattern. For the **tense and aspirated** contrast for p and k, Korean speakers scored about 95%, adoptees about 23% and French speakers about 20%. For the plain and tense contrast for s, Korean speakers scored about 79%, adoptees about 26% and French speakers about 23%.","children":["Two control conditions checked general performance. On a **vowel** contrast all three groups said “different” on about 97 to 99% of trials, and when the two sounds were identical all groups rarely said “different,” at about 3 to 13%."]},{"text":"The control conditions confirm that the adoptees **could do the task**. The difficulty was specific to the Korean stop contrasts, where the adoptees heard the differences at the same level as people who had never heard Korean.","children":["Their early Korean experience left **no measurable advantage** for these sounds."]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"In a more extensive study, **Pallier and colleagues** found that adoptees were **no better than monolingual francophones** on three tasks. The first task was **distinguishing Korean from a collection of largely unrelated languages**, which taps familiarity with the overall sound of Korean. Participants rated on a scale of 1 to 7 how much each sentence sounded like Korean.","children":["Both groups rated Korean sentences around 4, but they also rated Japanese sentences around 4 to 5. Polish sentences got about 2, and Swedish and Wolof sentences got about 2.5 to 3, so adoptees did not pick out Korean any better than French speakers did."]},{"text":"The second task was **identifying the correct Korean translation of French words**, which taps memory for Korean vocabulary. Adoptees scored about 55% correct and French speakers about 50%, which is close to chance for both.","children":["The third task was **determining whether a short excerpt was repeated from a recorded sentence they had just heard**, which taps sensitivity to Korean sound sequences. Both groups detected the excerpts in French sentences about 95% of the time, and both scored about 80% for Korean, Japanese and Polish sentences, with no adoptee advantage for Korean."]},{"text":"These are **not standard psycholinguistic tasks**. Pallier and colleagues were looking for **any remaining trace of Korean**, so they used tasks designed to catch even weak knowledge. They did not find any.","children":["On every measure, the adoptees looked like people who had never learned Korean."]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"**Pallier and colleagues** also used brain imaging. They found that listening to French activated the **classical language network**, including **superior temporal** and **inferior frontal** regions, in both groups.","children":["The superior temporal region is the upper part of the temporal lobe, and it is involved in processing speech sounds. The inferior frontal region is the lower part of the frontal lobe, and it includes Broca's area."]},{"text":"The comparisons subtracted brain activity for other languages from activity for French. French minus Polish, French minus Japanese and French minus Korean were computed for the French group and for the Korean group, who were the adoptees. In every comparison, French produced more activity in the language network than the foreign language did.","children":["This held for both groups, so both processed French as their own language."]},{"text":"**Subtracting Korean from French** might have revealed some difference between the groups if there were any **residual processing ability for Korean** left. If adoptees still processed Korean as a language, Korean would activate their language network more than it did for French speakers, so the difference between French and Korean would be smaller in adoptees. The difference was not smaller in the adoptees.","children":["The brains of the adoptees responded to Korean as a foreign language, much as they responded to Polish and Japanese. The brain imaging results therefore agree with the behavioral results."]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"Interactions between L2 and L1 can also be thought of in terms of **competitive dynamics**. Imagine that **L1 is represented as a set of connections** within and between different brain regions. These connections are built and maintained through use.","children":["Connections that are used get stronger, while connections that are not used can be taken over by competitors."]},{"text":"The same process occurs in vision with **monocular deprivation** in mice. After 2 to 3 days, the connections of the deprived eye show **increased spine motility**. After 3 to 4 days, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration**, which is the breakdown of cut-off nerve fibers. After more than a week, the fibers of the other eye show **axon sprouting and new spine birth** and take over the lost space.","children":["The eye that keeps receiving input gains control of the connections that the deprived eye loses."]},{"text":"If the L1 connections are never activated again, they could be **“taken over” by L2**. L2 receives constant use, so its connections strengthen and spread. L1 receives no use, so its connections weaken and can be claimed by L2.","children":["On this account, L2 replaces L1 through competition for the same neural resources."]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"**Can a second language replace the first?** The answer from the adoptees is **“Apparently YES!”** The adoptees showed no behavioral or brain evidence of their childhood Korean. As far as these tests could measure, French had completely replaced Korean.","children":["No test, behavioral or brain-based, found any trace of Korean."]},{"text":"**Are traces of L1 left behind that can be “salvaged,” though?** Salvaging a trace would mean recovering or using some leftover effect of the earlier language. Work with childhood overhearers of Spanish suggests that such traces can remain, because their early listening gave them an advantage in pronunciation when they studied Spanish later.","children":["A trace might not appear on a test of current ability but could still make **relearning** easier."]},{"text":"Testing current recognition and testing later learning ask different questions. One asks what the person can do with the language now. The other asks whether new learning benefits from something left over from the past.","children":["Relearning is the measure that could reveal a trace that the adoptee tests could not detect."]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"**Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh**, from the group of Terry Au at UCLA, examined this question in a study called “Overhearing a language during childhood.” They studied undergraduate students who **overheard Spanish frequently as children** but never learned to speak the language. All participants started classroom instruction of Spanish at around age 14, and they were tested at the end of a Year 2 college Spanish course.","children":["The **childhood overhearers** were compared with native speakers and with typical late L2 learners."]},{"text":"On **pronunciation**, the overhearers were far ahead of the late learners. **Voice onset time** for word-initial voiceless stops was 19.3 milliseconds for native speakers, 19.6 for overhearers and 36.2 for late learners, so overhearers matched native speakers while late learners produced longer, English-like values. Word-medial voiceless stops gave 22.4, 23.4 and 31.2 milliseconds.","children":["**Lenition** is a softening of a consonant, which Spanish applies to voiced stops between vowels. Word-initial voiced stops were lenited 43% of the time by native speakers, 24% by overhearers and 1% by late learners, and word-medial voiced stops 72%, 63% and 23%.","Accent ratings on a 5-point scale for voiceless stops were 4.3 for native speakers, 3.5 for overhearers and 2.2 for late learners, and for voiced stops they were 4.4, 3.3 and 2.7. Overhearers also knew some childhood slang, with 17% correct in production and 22% in comprehension, compared with 3% for late learners and 79% and 88% for native speakers."]},{"text":"On **grammar**, the overhearers had little or no advantage. Gender agreement was 93% for native speakers, 70% for overhearers and 68% for late learners, and number agreement was 92%, 88% and 85%. Grammaticality judgment accuracy was 94%, 64% and 63%, with reaction times of 1,042, 2,475 and 2,209 milliseconds.","children":["Childhood overhearing gave a clear advantage in pronunciation but not in grammar. Early listening left traces in the sound system that were **unmasked** when the students later studied Spanish, while grammar seems to need more than overheard input."]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"Taking the data from the **Korean adoptees** together with the data from the **overhearers**, two things follow. If L1 is removed from the environment entirely, **L2 can appear to completely replace it**. The adoptees showed no trace of Korean on any test of current ability.","children":["Complete loss of exposure can make a first language seem to vanish."]},{"text":"But this replacement is **not necessarily complete**. There may be some **residual knowledge** that can be “unmasked” with later learning, as with the pronunciation of the Spanish overhearers.","children":["The overhearers showed a clear advantage once they began learning Spanish."]},{"text":"There are also **potentially important differences between these populations**. The adoptees were tested on their current knowledge without any new Korean instruction. The overhearers were tested after classroom Spanish that ran from around age 14 through a second-year college course, which gave hidden knowledge a chance to appear.","children":["These and other differences between the groups make the two sets of results hard to compare directly."]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"Several **potentially important differences between these populations** could explain why one group kept traces of an early language and the other did not. **Korean is rarely heard or encountered in France**, which is not so for Spanish in Los Angeles. The overhearers may have kept hearing Spanish throughout their lives, and that continued exposure could have kept their Spanish sound knowledge active.","children":["A **heritage language** spoken by other family members also differs from a language not spoken by anyone you know. The overhearers often heard Spanish from relatives, while the adoptees lost all contact with Korean speakers."]},{"text":"The languages themselves differ too. **Cognates** are common between Spanish and English but rare between Korean and French, so shared vocabulary could help overhearers relearn.","children":["Spanish and English also have a **shared writing system**, because they use the same alphabet, while Korean uses a different script from French."]},{"text":"This is a **general issue** with work on second language acquisition and bilingualism. Languages and learning situations differ in many, many ways that are **hard, perhaps impossible, to control experimentally**. Researchers cannot assign people to grow up hearing particular languages in particular settings.","children":["Untangling these factors would require comparing populations that differ in only one way, which is rarely possible with real language histories."]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"**Second-language learning**, also called **L2 learning**, works as a **model system** for sensitive period research. A model system is a common, easily studied situation that is used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly. **Lenneberg (1967)** developed an influential theory that the sensitive period had to do with the development of **cerebral dominance**, which is also described as developmental changes in **language laterality**.","children":["**Cerebral dominance** means that one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language. **Language laterality** is how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both.","On this theory, the window for learning language closes once language becomes fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory. The first was a **rough alignment** of the literature on **brain development** with new insights about **language milestones**. The second was a review of the literature on **resilience after brain injury or surgery**.","children":["Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off, and no measurements were available from age 2 to 18. Lenneberg tied the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","Children who lost the left hemisphere often went on to develop language, while adults did not regain it."]},{"text":"The theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, which means its stage of physical development. A young, less specialized brain can move language to other tissue, but a mature, specialized brain has fewer ways to do that, so language learning becomes harder.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"Much of what is known about **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** comes from research on **idiosyncratic cases** or relatively **rare phenomena**. Idiosyncratic means particular to one individual. The evidence includes children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere, and Genie, who was cut off from language until age 13.","children":["Every one of these cases carries other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse, so it is hard to tell which factor caused the outcome."]},{"text":"In contrast, **many people learn second languages at different times in their lives**, often with **somewhat limited success**. Second-language learners are common, so researchers can study **large groups** instead of single rare cases. They begin at every age from early childhood to late adulthood, and their final success can be measured.","children":["Comparing early starters with late starters tells researchers how the age of first exposure relates to final skill."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** have also been used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive or critical period**. If later starters reliably end up at lower levels, then the age where the decline appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","children":["The **first language complicates** the comparison, because every L2 learner already knows a language and that knowledge shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** studied **critical period effects in second-language learning**, and their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language. They started from **Lenneberg (1967)**, who hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** running from early infancy until puberty. In its basic form, that hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition, so it was important to find out whether it extends to second-language acquisition too.","children":["If it does extend, young children should be better second-language learners than adults and should reach higher final levels of proficiency."]},{"text":"They tested that prediction by comparing the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States **between the ages of 3 and 39**. By the time of testing, these people had lived in the United States for between 3 and 26 years. They were tested on a wide variety of structures of English grammar with a **grammaticality judgment task**.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals. Test performance was **linearly related to age of arrival up to puberty**, so each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score. After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable and unrelated to age of arrival**.","children":["The age effect was not an accidental result of differences in amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The effect appeared on **every grammatical structure tested**, although the structures varied a lot in how well later learners mastered them. The authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**."]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** devised a **grammaticality judgment test**, or **GJT**. A grammaticality judgment is a decision about whether a sentence follows the rules of the language. Participants **listened to recorded sentences** and only had to say whether each sentence was grammatical or not. The test was **untimed**, so participants could take as long as they needed to decide.","children":[]},{"text":"**Can you determine what is being tested in each of these example sets?** Each item came in a grammatical version and an ungrammatical version marked with an asterisk, and each pair tests one rule of English. (1a) “The farmer bought two pigs at the market” and (1b) “The farmer bought two pig at the market” test **plural marking** on nouns. (2a) “The little boy is speaking to a policeman” and (2b) “The little boy is speak to a policeman” test the **progressive -ing** form of the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"Several pairs on the test target articles and other **determiners**. (6a) “Tom is reading a book in the bathtub” and (6b) “Tom is reading book in the bathtub” test whether a needed article is present. (7a) “The boys are going to the zoo this Saturday” and (7b) “A boys are going to the zoo this Saturday” test whether the determiner agrees with the noun in number. (8a) “Larry went home after the party” and (8b) “Larry went the home after the party” test a spot where English does not allow an article.","children":[]},{"text":"Other pairs on the test target questions. (23a) “When will Sam fix his car?” and (23b) “When Sam will fix his car?” test **wh-question** word order, where the helping verb must come before the subject. (24a) “What do they sell at the corner store?” and (24b) “What they sell at the corner store?” test the need for the helping verb “do” in a question.","children":["Taken together, the pairs cover many different rules of English grammar."]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"The data relate each participant's score on the grammar test to their **age of arrival**, or **AOA**. The score is the number of items correct out of 276, and the participants are split at puberty, which is the end of the critical period in Lenneberg's proposal. Splitting the data this way tests whether the age effect changes after puberty.","children":["A **correlation**, written r, measures how closely two variables are related, on a scale from −1 to +1. A value near −1 means one variable falls steadily as the other rises, and a value near 0 means little relationship."]},{"text":"**What patterns do we see in these data?** Among **early arrivals**, aged **3 to 15**, the correlation was **−0.87**, so scores fall steadily as age of arrival goes up. Children who arrived between about 3 and 7 score near the maximum, around 270. Scores fall to around 250 for arrivals near age 10 and to around 210 to 230 for arrivals near age 15.","children":["The strong negative correlation means that age of arrival predicts most of the score in this group."]},{"text":"Among **late arrivals**, aged **17 to 39**, the correlation was only **−0.16**. Scores are **lower overall and widely scattered**, from about 160 to 250. Some late arrivals score around 250, while others of similar age score below 180.","children":["The weak correlation means that age of arrival predicts little about who does well among adults.","The whole pattern combines a **strong age effect in childhood** with **lower, highly variable performance in adulthood**."]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features. There is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**, where AOA is age of arrival and earlier arrival goes with higher scores. **Early learners, up to about age 8, are often near-perfect**, which means **native-like**, because their scores sit close to those of native English speakers. Late arrivals have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**, because among adults age of arrival barely predicts the score.","children":[]},{"text":"The first two features are about learners who arrived as children. The youngest arrivals reach native-like grammar, so near-perfect scores are the clearest sign of an early advantage. Arrivals between about 8 and 15 reach steadily lower levels, so the **decline begins well before puberty**.","children":["Adult arrivals scored lower still, with wide differences between individuals."]},{"text":"The phrase **“nearly random distribution”** has a specific meaning here. It means that among late arrivals, age of arrival and score are only weakly related.","children":["Late arrivals scored **well above chance**, but individual scores varied enormously. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** argue that these data are consistent with a **sensitive period** for L2 learning, although they use the term **“critical period.”** Their argument has two parts.","children":[]},{"text":"First, the **dependence of ultimate attainment on AOA** is consistent with a **loss of language-learning ability over development**. **Ultimate attainment** is the final level of skill a learner reaches. Scores fall steadily as arrival age rises through childhood, so the ability to learn language seems to decline as children mature.","children":["The timing is **not entirely consistent with Lenneberg's hypothesis**, because Lenneberg put the end of the critical period at puberty and the decline here starts around age 7 or 8.","Lenneberg was working with a **large gap** in the data between early childhood and adolescence, with no brain measurements between ages 2 and 18, so his timing was only a rough estimate."]},{"text":"Second, **attainment in late acquisition is all over the place**, because adults who arrived at the same age reach very different levels. Johnson and Newport argue that after the sensitive period closes, attainment depends on **idiosyncratic learning abilities** and not on the usual language acquisition processes. Idiosyncratic learning abilities are individual differences in skills such as memory, motivation or analytical skill.","children":["Children share one natural acquisition process, so they reach similar levels at similar ages.","Adults have lost that process and must lean on their own abilities, which differ a great deal between people."]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"There are **two versions of the “critical period” hypothesis**, and both start from the same claim that early in life humans have a **superior capacity for acquiring languages**. The **exercise hypothesis** says that if the capacity is not exercised during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation. If the capacity is exercised, however, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life.","children":["On the exercise hypothesis, learning a first language early keeps the ability alive, so an adult who learned an L1 normally should still learn an L2 as well as a child does."]},{"text":"The **maturational state hypothesis** says that the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used. On this version, even someone who learned an L1 normally loses the superior capacity while growing up, so that person will learn an L2 less well as an adult.","children":[]},{"text":"**Which of these do you think JN89 see as more consistent with their data?** **Johnson and Newport (1989)** see the **maturational state hypothesis** as the better fit, and it is the idea named in the title of their paper. Every one of their participants had a normal first language, yet the later starters still learned the L2 less well.","children":["The exercise hypothesis predicts that these adults would learn as well as children, because they had already exercised the capacity."]},{"text":"**Does this change our interpretation of the L1 critical period data?** The L1 data cannot choose between the two versions, because in cases like Genie the capacity was never exercised, and both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases. Only second-language data can tell the two versions apart, because only L2 learners have already exercised the capacity before learning another language.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"**Test design** raises a **methodological issue**, because the **grammaticality judgment task is very different from fluent language use**. In fluent use, people speak and understand quickly without stopping to think about rules. In a grammaticality judgment task, people consider each sentence and decide whether it is correct.","children":["One thing people worry about is the use of **“metalinguistic” or “explicit” knowledge**. **Metalinguistic knowledge** is conscious knowledge about language, such as knowing the rule that English plurals add “-s.”"]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** contrasts **implicit knowledge** and **explicit knowledge** on several characteristics. Implicit knowledge brings **intuitive awareness** of linguistic norms, and explicit knowledge brings **conscious awareness**. Implicit knowledge is **procedural**, which means knowing how to use rules and fragments, and explicit knowledge is **declarative**, which means knowing facts about grammatical rules and fragments that can be stated. Implicit knowledge is variable but systematic, and explicit knowledge is often anomalous and inconsistent.","children":["Implicit knowledge is reached through **automatic processing** and is used in **fluent performance**. Explicit knowledge is reached through effortful **controlled processing** and is used when a speaker is **planning** or having difficulty.","Implicit knowledge is **nonverbalizable**, so people cannot explain it, and explicit knowledge is **verbalizable**. Implicit knowledge is potentially **only learnable within the critical period**, and explicit knowledge is learnable at **any age**."]},{"text":"An untimed GJT lets people draw on explicit knowledge, so it may miss the implicit knowledge that the critical period is supposed to affect. A learner who consciously applies a taught rule can earn the same score as a learner who knows the rule implicitly, so the score is the same whichever kind of knowledge produced it.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"One suggestion is that the **task demands of the untimed GJT** make it a better measure of **explicit learning of “metalinguistic” knowledge** than of grammar acquisition. **Ellis (2005)** compared the **design features** of five tests.","children":["**Imitation** and **oral narrative** rely on feel, are done under time pressure and focus on meaning, so they require no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **timed GJT** relies on feel and is done under time pressure, but it focuses on form, and it requires no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **untimed GJT** relies on rules, has no time pressure and focuses on form, so it does allow metalinguistic knowledge. The **metalanguage** test, which asks people to state rules, also relies on rules, has no time pressure, focuses on form and requires metalinguistic knowledge."]},{"text":"Ellis then related each test to two background measures, which were the **age when people started learning** and their **years of formal instruction**. Only two relationships were significant. Starting age was related to the **timed GJT**, with a correlation of **−0.23**. Years of formal instruction were related to the **untimed GJT with ungrammatical items**, with a correlation of **0.27**.","children":["The other tests had only weak relationships with either measure."]},{"text":"Consistent with this suggestion, **untimed GJT performance is correlated with years of formal education in L2**. Formal classroom instruction teaches explicit rules, and those rules help on the untimed test. Starting age matters for the timed test, because time pressure limits the use of explicit rules.","children":["An age effect found with an untimed GJT may partly reflect explicit, taught knowledge and not natural grammar acquisition."]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"**Transfer** raises another issue that is both methodological and theoretical. Transfer means using first-language knowledge while learning a second language. Languages that are **typologically more similar** can benefit more from transfer, where typological similarity is similarity in structure, such as word order and word forms.","children":["Cognates and loan words, such as French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette**, and Japanese **korokke** and **monoreru**, give learners familiar words to build on."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested people with **English as their L2** and **Chinese or Korean as their L1**. Chinese and Korean are very different from English in grammar and vocabulary, so these learners could get little help from transfer.","children":[]},{"text":"**What do you suppose would happen if the languages were more similar?** Speakers of a language close to English could reuse much of what they already know, so they would get much more help from transfer. Their scores might then relate to age in a different way.","children":["If that happens, the age pattern in the Johnson and Newport data depends on the particular languages involved as well as on age."]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"**Not all aspects of the grammar are equally difficult**. **Johnson and Newport (1989)** compared the mean percentage of errors on **12 types of English rules**. The comparison covered native speakers and groups that arrived at ages 3 to 7, 8 to 10, 11 to 15, 17 to 24 and 25 to 39. The rule types are determiners, plurals, verb subcategorization, past tense, pronouns, particles, auxiliaries, third-person singular, wh-questions, yes/no questions, word order and progressive -ing.","children":["**Verb subcategorization** is the set of rules for which kinds of words or phrases a verb can take after it."]},{"text":"Native speakers and the youngest arrivals make very few errors on any rule. The latest arrivals, aged 17 to 24 and 25 to 39, make the most errors on **determiners**, around 65 to 68%, and on **plurals**, around 45 to 55%. Errors on **word order** and **progressive -ing** are low for every group, under about 10%.","children":[]},{"text":"Some of this looks like the **order in which children learn language**, because progressive -ing and word order are easiest for late learners and are also among the first structures children master. Some of it could be **transfer**. **Chinese has no determiners**, so Chinese speakers have no L1 category for “a” and “the,” and that makes English articles especially hard.","children":["Children learn **questions** very late, but questions are among the **easiest structures for late learners**. Adults may bring general cognitive skills or L1 knowledge to question forms.","How hard a structure is depends on the structure itself and on what the learner's first language provides."]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"**Birdsong and Molis (2001)** repeated the Johnson and Newport study with **Spanish speakers learning English**. For the **Chinese and Korean speakers** in the Johnson and Newport data, early arrivals aged 3 to 15 had a strong correlation with age of **−0.87**, and late arrivals aged 17 to 39 had a weak one of **−0.16**. For the **Spanish speakers**, early arrivals aged 3 to 16 had only a weak correlation of **−0.23**, and late arrivals aged 17 to 44 had a strong one of **−0.69**.","children":["The Spanish speakers had almost the reverse pattern.","Spanish-speaking **early arrivals** scored near the maximum regardless of age, with only a slight decline even for those arriving around 16.","Spanish-speaking **late arrivals** declined strongly with age. Their scores fell from around 260 for those arriving near 18 to around 160 for those arriving after 40."]},{"text":"**What are the consequences of this for the idea that AOA effects on GJT performance reflect maturational constraints?** **Maturational constraints** are limits on learning caused by the brain's development. If a fixed maturational schedule controlled learning, the pattern should look the same for every first language. The pattern changed with the learners' first language instead, which a purely maturational account does not predict.","children":["Spanish is much closer to English than Chinese or Korean, so **transfer** may help Spanish speakers reach native-like grammar even when they arrive late in childhood. The age effect depends on the **language pairing** as well as on age."]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"It is not clear that these findings can even be called “conclusions.” What does hold across studies is that, overall, **L2 attainment is lower for older learners**.","children":["The age effect in second-language learning is real, but it depends on many factors that are hard to separate."]},{"text":"How much lower depends on three things. It depends on **what constructions are tested**, because some structures, such as determiners, are far harder for late learners than others, such as word order. It depends on **how they are tested**, because timed and untimed tests can disagree when the untimed test leaves room for explicit knowledge. It depends on **language pairings**, because Spanish speakers and Chinese or Korean speakers have different age patterns in learning English.","children":[]},{"text":"It is also **hard to dissociate** several factors that usually go together. **Age of acquisition** is hard to separate from the **amount and quality of experience**, because younger arrivals usually get more years of exposure and more immersion, such as schooling in the language. **Transfer effects** are hard to separate from **L2 acquisition**, because L2 performance reflects what was learned and also what carries over from the L1. **Performance** is hard to separate from **competence**, generally speaking.","children":["**Competence** is underlying knowledge of a language, and **performance** is how well that knowledge is used on a given task. A test score measures performance, so it may not reveal competence directly."]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-54":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between a first language, L1, and a second language, L2, in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation means that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it. The model connects an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a shared store of concepts.","children":["**Lexical links** join words across the two languages, and **conceptual links** join each vocabulary to meanings. L1 words have strong links to concepts, while L2 words have weaker conceptual links."]},{"text":"Consider what happens in this model when the task of **Marian and Spivey** is performed in L2, when Russian-English bilinguals hear the English instruction to pick up the speaker. The concept for “speaker” **partially activates the Russian word for “matches,”** which is spichki. The link between speaker and spichki is sound overlap, because the two words begin with the same sounds and share no meaning.","children":["This activity **feeds back to the conceptual activation** for “matches,” because L1 words are strongly connected to their concepts. Conceptual activation for “matches” leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**, although the listener can still pick up the speaker correctly."]},{"text":"When the task is done in L1, we might imagine that the **conceptual links with L2 are too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably. An English competitor would need a strong link from its English name to its concept to attract the eyes during a Russian task.","children":["The unequal strength of the links explains why the other language interferes in one direction but not in the other."]}],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property. These stimuli are constructed by **keeping F1 constant** and **varying the F2 onset frequency linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13. The **F2 onset frequency** is the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition.","children":["Holding F1 constant removes one possible difference between the stimuli.","Varying the F2 onset **linearly** means that it changes by the same amount every time, so **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series. Early in the series, F2 starts well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises. As the onset climbs, the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat, and later F2 starts above the vowel frequency and falls.","children":[]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum is a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories. Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and others straddle a boundary.","children":["A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size of change.","If perception is categorical, steps within a category will sound alike and steps across a boundary will sound different, so any advantage for boundary pairs comes from the boundary itself."]}],"review-3":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond if perception worked according to a particular account. This simple version assumes that perception is **non-categorical**, which means that it tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries.","children":["The model works as a baseline, because real data can be compared against it to see whether perception is more categorical than a gradual account allows."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization would get “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category. The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","children":["Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**. The farther a stimulus is from the /b/ prototype, the less /b/-like and the more /d/-like it sounds.","**Stimulus 2** would be heard as **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. It sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype, so the responses split in that same proportion."]},{"text":"Under this account, **labeling** changes smoothly across stimuli 1 to 11. The /b/ responses fall in a straight slope to 0 at stimulus 6, where /d/ responses peak at 1, and /d/ then falls to 0 at stimulus 10, where /g/ reaches 1.","children":["Two neighboring labels are equally likely, at 0.5, halfway between their prototypes. Every change is gradual, with no sudden jump from one label to the next."]}],"review-4":[{"text":"The same simple ideal observer can assume that perception is **linear**, which means that equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large. The other important prediction is that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are **“same” or “different.”** These are separate tasks, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a sort of “peaked” function**. There are three categories and listeners are choosing between labels, so the responses for each category rise toward its prototype and fall away from it. These peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination works differently under the linear account. The pairs compared are two steps apart, from stimuli 1 and 3 through stimuli 9 and 11, and every pair has the same physical separation. If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, **discrimination accuracy would also be constant**, at about 0.8 for every pair.","children":["A flat pattern would mean that category boundaries play no role in hearing differences. A peaked pattern would mean that the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","A listener can be unsure which name to choose and still hear that two sounds differ."]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** measured actual labeling and discrimination along the continuum from /b/ through /d/ to /g/. Listeners labeled each stimulus, and they also judged pairs of stimuli that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart. The results departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions, and this pattern is **categorical perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling had **sharp changes** in identification. Stimuli 1 through about 3 were heard as /b/ close to 100% of the time. Around stimuli 4 and 5 the responses switched almost completely to /d/, and around stimuli 9 and 10 they switched just as quickly to /g/.","children":["These switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes that the ideal observer predicted.","The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**. Accuracy peaked at the boundary between /b/ and /d/ and again at the boundary between /d/ and /g/. Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, which is the level expected from guessing.","children":["Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data, on the assumption that listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels. The predictions captured much of the real pattern, and between the peaks the predicted accuracy falls to about 50%, which is chance.","children":["Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity to acoustic differences beyond the category labels alone."]}],"review-6":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing** in speech perception. A thresholded model would have a hard time explaining why **the number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**. A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in a single sound, such as bear and pear.","children":["Looks to the competitor climb steadily as VOT nears the boundary. This happens even when the final answer is the same, so it reflects **graded activation within one category**."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity **never reaches the phoneme level**. Feature evidence can swing between +voiced and -voiced, but the phoneme level stays silent until /b/ is chosen all at once. The phoneme level therefore cannot know how close a sound came to the boundary.","children":["Every token placed in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level. Word-level competition would then be identical for every /b/, so nothing could produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"A **cascaded account** explains the effect directly. More support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word, so sounds nearer the boundary draw more looks to the competitor.","children":[]}],"review-7":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. Activity runs up from phonemes to words and also back down from words to phonemes. **Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","children":["An uncertain sound can partly activate a word, and that word can then strengthen the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"Influence therefore travels **in both directions**. Acoustic evidence changes which words are active, and active words shift the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming. This helps listeners cope with noisy or ambiguous input.","children":[]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**. Cascading sends incomplete evidence **forward**, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words. Feedback sends influence from words **back down** to phonemes.","children":["An interactive model can have both cascading and feedback.","The reason to add feedback comes from experiments in which **word knowledge changes how sounds are heard**, such as the **Ganong effect**."]}],"review-8":[{"text":"One prediction for the experiment of **Ganong (1980)** comes from a **late “correction” process**. In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual, but there is a bias to report having heard a word**. The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge. At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a real word.","children":[]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias**. Three kinds of continua are compared, measured by the proportion of voiced responses from short VOT to long VOT. In **word-nonword** continua the voiced end forms a word, in **nonword-word** continua the voiceless end forms a word, and in **neutral** continua neither end has a lexical advantage.","children":["A bias added after perception should push responses toward the word by the **same amount everywhere**.","The word-nonword responses would be shifted up even at the long VOT end, staying near 0.25 and not falling to 0. The nonword-word responses would be shifted down at the short VOT end, starting near 0.75 and not at 1."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions at each step. Under late correction, the lexical effect is a **flat line near 0.25** across the whole continuum.","children":["The bias favors the word equally at clear steps and at ambiguous steps, because perception and word knowledge stay separate and word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"review-9":[{"text":"A second possibility is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**. In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other. Near an ambiguous onset, the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support.","children":["The effect changes **categorization itself**, and not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ **only in the middle** of the continuum. The word-nonword, neutral and nonword-word responses all start near 1 at short VOT and end near 0 at long VOT, because the sound is clear at both ends. Near the boundary, the word-nonword condition crosses later and the nonword-word condition crosses earlier.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** should therefore form a **peak**. It is near 0 at the endpoints, rises to about 0.35 for the items closest to the boundary, and falls again. The effect is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**, because those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve some ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","children":["At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant, so there is little room for word knowledge to help.","A **flat** lexical effect points to a reporting bias, while a **peaked** lexical effect points to a shift in the boundary itself."]}],"review-10":[{"text":"The results of **Ganong (1980)** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, which is the **boundary-shift** account. The proportion of voiced responses in the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions starts near 1 at short VOT and falls toward 0 at long VOT. The two conditions separate most in the middle, near the boundary.","children":["The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary and then falls back."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary **rules out a simple reporting bias**, because word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest. At the boundary, the word that fits the context tips the balance. At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence decides the answer alone.","children":[]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen, and he focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**. A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when they were unsure of the sound. Later models would emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","The whole-word representation can then shape how an ambiguous part of the word is interpreted during recognition."]}],"review-11":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme-level and word-level activations** at four moments as the input unfolds. The input begins with a sound that is **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**. It is followed by **/l/**, the vowel **/ʌ/** and **/g/**, which together make the word **plug**.","children":["In the **first frame**, only the ambiguous first sound has arrived. **/b/** and **/p/** are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2, and word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"In the **second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive. Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl, and partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl. Several candidates gain activation together, including **plug**, **blood** and **blush**, because all of them fit the beginning heard so far.","children":["Phoneme activity for /p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"In the **fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives. The /g/ fits plug but does not fit the endings of blood or blush. **Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, while the alternatives fall away.","children":["**/p/ ends up more active than /b/** at the phoneme level."]}],"review-12":[{"text":"As the input unfolds over time, **activity accumulates for “plug,”** especially when the **final /g/** arrives. The /g/ is highly inconsistent with **“blood”** and **“blush,”** which were also activated in the third frame. Blood and blush matched the early part of the input, so they were plausible candidates for a while.","children":["Once /g/ arrives, blood and blush lose support because their endings do not fit."]},{"text":"The TRACE model therefore explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**. Top-down processing means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis. **Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**, and the feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","children":["Plug is the winning word, so /p/ receives extra support from it."]},{"text":"The ambiguous beginning becomes clearer because /p/ belongs to the word that the whole sequence supports. Information that arrives at the **end of a word** can reach back and shape how an **earlier sound** is heard.","children":["This happens through interactions during recognition, and the ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/."]}],"review-13":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start with a **parser**, which is a mental process whose job is to build a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read. A syntactic tree records how the words group into phrases and how those phrases relate to each other.","children":["The trees for the horse sentence and the dinosaur comic both come from a Language Log piece about this sentence."]},{"text":"In the **correct tree**, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**, that holds “the horse” plus a reduced clause. The reduced clause means “that was raced past the barn,” and the words “that” and “was” are silent in the actual sentence. The clause also has an empty subject position. Inside the clause, raced takes the prepositional phrase, or **PP**, “past the barn,” so the main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**.","children":["In the **failed parse**, “the horse” is an ordinary NP and “raced past the barn” is the main VP, with raced as the main verb. **Fell** is then left with nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"Any parser has to work inside a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**. Words arrive quickly, one after another, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence before it starts building structure. It can also keep only a limited number of structures in play at once.","children":["An account of processing has to explain how the parser copes with these two limits when a sentence is ambiguous."]}],"review-14":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is **much more complex syntactically** than the failed parse, because it has **many more nodes**. A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","children":["The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was.”","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"One strategy that saves space is to **initially prefer smaller trees**, and this strategy is called **“minimal attachment.”** Under minimal attachment, the parser fits each new word into the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes. Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment.","children":[]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**, because that analysis needs the fewest nodes. The reading in which “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause, so the parser passes it over at first.","children":["The smaller structure lightens the immediate load, but it leads the reader down the garden path. When fell arrives, it requires the larger structure that was passed over, so the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework. It is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**, so new words get attached to the phrase that is being built right now.","children":["Keeping a clause open means that the parser keeps adding incoming words to it, and does not close it off and start a new structure."]},{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** the phrase **“with the dynamite”** wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** that starts with **“blew open.”** **Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for this sentence.","children":["In the **preferred** structure, the PP attaches to the **VP**. This **verb-phrase attachment** makes the dynamite the **instrument** that the burglar used to blow open the safe.","In the **unpreferred** structure, the PP attaches to the **NP** “the safe.” This **noun-phrase attachment** makes the phrase say which safe was blown open, namely the safe that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they say different things. Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the new phrase joins it. Minimal attachment prefers the same attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than building an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite.”","children":["For this sentence the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning without any difficulty."]}],"review-16":[{"text":"Late closure can be seen at work in a sentence that **requires the “unpreferred” parse to be sensible**, such as **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels.”** Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe. The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**, so “with the jewels” has to attach to the noun phrase “the safe,” which is the unpreferred NP attachment.","children":[]},{"text":"In **careful tests of reading time and eye movements**, people are **slower to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse**. **Reading time** measures how long readers spend on each part of the sentence. **Eye movement** records track where readers look and when they jump back to reread earlier words.","children":["The extra time appears right at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea that the jewels were used to blow open the safe**, and then they have to **retrace their steps**. The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, because that is its usual preference and it is what it did for the dynamite. The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading, so the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase.","children":["The final meaning is easy to understand, but getting there carries a measurable **processing cost**, because the repair takes time."]}],"review-17":[{"text":"This syntactic explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence. It parallels the **“lumber mill”** model of thresholded processing from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without checking meaning or context, and meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**. It wants to make **the simplest tree possible**, which is **minimal attachment**. It does this partly by **leaving clauses open as long as possible**, which is **late closure**.","children":["Both preferences limit how much structure has to be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"This is one way of dealing with ambiguity **very efficiently**, because the parser can **ignore ambiguity until it becomes a problem**. It commits to one likely structure and builds only that one, and most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","children":["When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be revised, and that revision is the source of garden path difficulty. Garden paths are the price of an efficient strategy that works well on average."]}],"review-18":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**, because it can come from the visual scene right in front of us as well as from general knowledge. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a **visual world** task. They gave people a display of real objects, which were **a pencil, a box, an empty towel, and an apple sitting on a towel**. People then heard the instruction **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","children":[]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like it should be the end of the clause **“put the apple on the towel.”** In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**, the place where the apple should go.","children":["In the intended reading, “on the towel” says **which apple** to move, the one already on a towel, and **“in the box”** is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do seem to look at the empty towel while planning their movements**, as though they are considering it as a target, before they plan a movement to the box. With one apple in view and the ambiguous instruction, a typical sequence of looks goes to the apple at about 1,000 milliseconds, then to the empty towel shortly after, and then to the box at about 2,000 milliseconds.","children":["The comparison was the unambiguous instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box.”** With that instruction, the eyes go from the apple straight to the box, with no look to the empty towel.","The listener recognizes every word correctly. The error comes from taking the location phrase as the destination, which briefly changes the planned action."]}],"review-19":[{"text":"In a **two-referent context**, the display holds **two apples**. One apple sits on a towel and the other sits somewhere else, and the empty towel and the box are still there. “The apple” alone no longer says which apple to move, so the phrase **“on the towel”** now does useful work, because it picks out one apple from the two.","children":[]},{"text":"In this context, participants usually look first at the apple on the towel and then at the box. Looks to the empty towel become much less common.","children":["**The sequence and the timing of eye movements**, measured relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and the unambiguous instructions.","So the ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit instruction with “that’s on the towel.”"]},{"text":"The two-apple display gives “on the towel” a clear job before the listener even reaches “in the box.” With two apples in view, the listener needs to know which apple is meant, so a phrase that singles one out is expected. Listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start, so the destination reading never takes hold.","children":["The same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context is different."]}],"review-20":[{"text":"Now consider what happens when there are **two apples** in the display. It suddenly becomes **felicitous**, which means natural and appropriate in the situation, to give more information about **which apple** you are talking about.","children":["With only one apple, there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination.","With two apples, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"So **people do not look at the empty towel as much**. The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the **incorrect destination**, which is the empty towel. In the **one-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials, and unambiguous instructions led to almost none. In the **two-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials, which is close to the roughly **12%** for unambiguous instructions.","children":["The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts, so the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled."]},{"text":"This is an effect of **referential context**, which is the set of objects that a phrase could refer to. Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while they are still hearing the sentence.","children":["The large cost of ambiguity appears only when the display contains one apple."]}],"review-21":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, meaning a two-way division, sets the two aphasias against each other. **Wernicke's aphasia** is described as **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax is roughly intact**. “Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll's poem of grammatical sentences filled with nonsense words.","children":["One Wernicke's patient, asked about his health, replied “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves,” which is an example from Kriendler and colleagues (1971). The sentence flows smoothly but never forms a clear message.","Another patient said “My wires don’t hire right” when he was asked about his poor vision.","An aphasic physician who was asked if he was a doctor said “I’m a male demaploze on my own” and “I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go,” and **demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient described a fork as “a need for a schedule” and a spoon as “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** is described as **“telegraphic” speech**, with **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles** and **relative sparing of important content words**. One Broca's patient from **Badecker and Caramazza (1985)** was asked what brought him to the hospital. His answer included “Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital” and “Two doctors … and ah … teeth,” with long pauses throughout.","children":["Content words such as Dad, hospital, doctors and teeth carry pieces of the story. The grammatical words and endings that would connect them into sentences are largely missing, and the speech is full of pauses."]},{"text":"The two patterns look like mirror images. Wernicke's patients have fluent grammar with empty or wrong words, while Broca's patients have meaningful words with little grammar.","children":[]}],"review-22":[{"text":"The dichotomy can also be stated in terms of what is damaged. On this account, **Wernicke's aphasia** reflects **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** reflects **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words. **Activation** makes a candidate word available, and **inhibition** suppresses competing words that should not be chosen, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"This description is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Paul Broca's presentation of **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan,”** says that Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, which was “tan.” He **seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses**, and he in fact **seemed to understand what was being said**.","children":["**Why is it critical that Leborgne had his other senses intact?** Intact senses mean that the loss was specific to speech, so it counts as a dissociation.","A “capacity for articulate speech” also seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness,” which is a phrenology term for love of one's children.","If Tan could be examined today, his deficits would be specified at the **levels of description used in modern linguistics**, which are phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic."]},{"text":"The result is confusing, because **Broca's actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca's aphasics** today. They were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control issues**. They would be classified as suffering from **apraxia of speech**, which is a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak.","children":["Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"review-23":[{"text":"The dichotomy treats Wernicke's aphasia as a problem in the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a problem with grammar. It is interesting partly because it **aligns well with a sharp distinction that late 20th-century formal linguistics drew between the grammar and the lexicon**.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences. The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky's formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. A person recognizes a new item as a sentence **not because it matches some familiar item in any simple way**, but because **it is generated by the grammar** that each person has internalized.","children":["A sentence never heard before can still be understood, because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"Chomsky also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**. In **Chomsky (1955)**, neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker's past experience, and yet **the first is grammatical while the second is not**.","children":["The first sentence has no sensible meaning, but its word order follows English grammar. The second uses the same words in an order the grammar does not allow.","The two sentences are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference between them must come from grammar as its own system.","The aphasia dichotomy maps onto this split, with Wernicke's aphasia as a disorder of the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a disorder of the grammar."]}],"review-24":[{"text":"**“Man bites dog”** is the newspaper example of an unexpected event. **Why do we need psycholinguistic tests to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension?** The reason is that **in conversation, there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**.","children":["**Redundant** cues are several different sources of information that all point to the same meaning."]},{"text":"In “The dog bit the man,” there are **many ways to get the right meaning**. A listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN into an event representation**, **with or without syntax**, because dogs bite people much more often than people bite dogs. The sentence also follows **canonical word order**, which is **agent, then verb, then undergoer**, or **subject, then verb, then object**.","children":["The **agent** is the doer of the action, and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English, the first noun is usually the agent.","A lot of comprehension also takes place in a **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, which clarifies a great deal."]},{"text":"Broca's patients' comprehension might depend on these **compensatory heuristics**. A **heuristic** is a mental shortcut, and a compensatory one makes up for a weakness elsewhere. A patient might assume the most plausible event, or assume that the first noun is the doer, and either shortcut gives the right answer for “The dog bit the man” without any grammatical analysis.","children":["To test grammar directly, the sentences have to defeat the shortcuts, as “The dog that the man is biting is angry” does. Here the **man** is doing the biting, even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters, so only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** devised **a list of just this kind of sentence, along with a set of control sentences**. The materials come in four groups of eight sentences each. **Control sentences** are simple sentences in canonical order, such as “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle,” where the doer comes first and the event is ordinary.","children":[]},{"text":"**Center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints** contain one clause inside another, but meaning makes the roles obvious. A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause placed in the middle of the main clause. Examples are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round,” and apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone reveals who does what.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reversible center-embedded sentences** use two participants who could each plausibly do the action. Examples are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy,” where meaning gives no help and only grammar reveals the roles. **Improbable center-embedded sentences** describe events that go against expectations. Examples are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny,” where common sense points to the wrong answer.","children":["Worms do not usually eat birds, but in the improbable sentence the worm is the eater."]},{"text":"In every embedded sentence, the first noun is the one acted upon, and the doer is inside the relative clause. The grammatical structure stays the same across the three embedded groups, while the help or interference from meaning changes.","children":["A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on the control and semantically constrained sentences and fail on the reversible and improbable ones. A listener who parses normally should succeed on all four."]}],"review-26":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**, in which participants heard a sentence and chose which of the pictures matched its meaning. Accuracy reveals whether the listener understood who did what to whom, and no spoken answer is required. They compared healthy listeners with patients who had Broca's, conduction and Wernicke's aphasia on the four sentence types.","children":["The four types were control sentences, semantically constrained sentences, reversible sentences and improbable sentences."]},{"text":"**Healthy controls are more or less equally good at all stimulus types**, and they stay near the maximum score on each one. **Wernicke's patients are poor overall**, with low scores even on the simple control sentences.","children":["The poor scores of Wernicke's patients are probably a result of their **general comprehension deficits**, which affect understanding of words as well as grammar."]},{"text":"**Broca's patients** have a **particular difficulty keeping “who did what to whom” straight** when **syntax conflicts with common sense** or with the probable semantic roles for the nouns. Their scores are high on control sentences and on semantically constrained sentences, because meaning supplies the answer there. Their scores drop on reversible and improbable sentences, because only grammar can reveal the roles there.","children":[]},{"text":"This **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca's aphasia impacts syntax specifically**. The deficit appeared in understanding as well as in speaking, so it lies in the grammar itself.","children":["The title of the paper is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes,” which states the idea. Broca's patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis but keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"review-27":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**. **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and **indicate which animal was doing the action**. Pictures of a dog and a cat appeared on a computer screen, and participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","children":["The event is always a dog biting a cat, but the sentence structure changes."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the doer first. The **active** type is “The dog is biting the cat,” and the **subject cleft** type is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat,” so for both types a shortcut of choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer.","children":["A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s” and “that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft, the focused noun is the subject, which is the doer."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the receiver of the action first. The **object cleft** type is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting,” where the focused noun is the object, the one being bitten. The **passive** type is “The cat is bitten by the dog,” where the receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","children":["For these two types the first-animal shortcut gives the wrong answer, so choosing the dog correctly requires grammatical analysis.","A listener who always picks the first animal succeeds on the first two types and fails on the last two, while a listener who parses the grammar succeeds on all four."]}],"review-28":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** do this task, **a pattern emerges much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by type of aphasia with the Western Aphasia Battery, and the percentage of correct responses was measured for active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences.","children":["**Elderly controls** score close to 100% on all four sentence types.","**Wernicke's** patients score low on every sentence type, around 43% to 50%, which is near chance."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia patients have particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, which are the object clefts and passives. They score about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts, and then they drop to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives. These are **noncanonical** structures, meaning they depart from the usual doer-first order.","children":["**Their performance is overall not so great**, because even on simple active sentences they are far below the elderly controls. The pattern combines a general drop in performance with an extra cost for complex structures."]},{"text":"The other groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop for complex sentences. **Anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, score about 89% on actives and subject clefts, about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives. **Conduction** aphasia patients score about 75% on actives and subject clefts and fall to about 42% on object clefts.","children":["Each group's profile provides a clue to the nature of its deficit."]}],"review-29":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system** in different ways. The stress conditions were things like **speeding up the speech**, adding noise, or **asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task**.","children":["**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal. A **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies from speech, so it sounds muffled.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** are number lists that participants had to remember while listening, which uses up memory resources."]},{"text":"With a single stressor, accuracy stays near 99% in the normal condition and stays high for actives and subject clefts in every condition. Object clefts suffer most, and with noise accuracy falls from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts. Passives also drop under noise, to about 91%.","children":["When two stressors are combined, the effects grow larger. With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy is about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts, but it drops to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives.","Compression plus visual digits lowers object clefts to about 90% while leaving actives near 98%."]},{"text":"**Object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all the manipulations**, which is the same pattern seen in Broca's aphasia. These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge, so stressing their processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile. This suggests that **the comprehension deficits seen in Broca's aphasia patients may be a “performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction in resources hurts them first. The patients may still have the grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"review-30":[{"text":"**Are Broca's aphasics' comprehension deficits language-specific?** **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match an action to an object**. In the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing has a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking, and participants choose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster. In the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead of by pantomime.","children":[]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, and aphasic patients made more errors in both. In the non-linguistic version, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients scored **93.4%**. In the linguistic version, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients scored **87.5%**. So **Broca's aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Accuracy also depended on the type of wrong choice offered, which is called the **distractor**. With **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and aphasic patients scored 93.3%.","With **semantic** distractors, which are related in meaning to the correct object, controls scored 99.2% and aphasic patients dropped to **86.3%**.","With **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and aphasic patients scored 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, so an affordance-based distractor is a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The nonverbal version removes language from the input but keeps meaning in the task. A pantomimed action has to be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb has to be linked to its object in a sentence.","children":["A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the nonverbal version. The patients' difficulty on that version points to a broader problem with action meanings, which is shared with language."]}],"review-31":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca's area**. Lesion maps link brain areas to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**. The strongest associations fall in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, and that region includes Broca's area.","children":["The maps use three horizontal slices of the brain, and colored regions mark where damage was associated with poorer performance. The hottest colors, red and yellow, mark the strongest relationships."]},{"text":"The task only asked participants to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object. They did not have to produce a sentence, and they did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction.","children":["Patients whose damage included Broca's area had the most trouble interpreting pantomimed actions."]},{"text":"Damage to Broca's area therefore affects **more than syntax**. Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved. Broca's area supports **action understanding** as well as grammatical processing.","children":["Any account of Broca's area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"review-32":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be measured through behaviors that change when an infant notices something new. Head turns and sucking reveal what an infant hears without the infant saying anything. Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","children":["The experimenter decides exactly which acoustic difference is presented, so a head turn or renewed sucking tells whether the infant detected that difference."]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab, an infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes. The infant sits on a parent's lap while an experimenter holds the infant's attention forward with toys. A correct head turn after a change earns an interesting visual reward, such as a lit-up moving toy.","children":["Once the infant has learned this link, a head turn after a sound change means **the infant heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** relies on the infant's natural response to something new. The infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a pressure recorder, and strong sucks make a sound play. As the same sound repeats, the infant grows used to it, which is called **habituation**, and sucking slows down.","children":["If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change.","The head turn depends on a learned link between a sound change and a reward, while sucking depends on a natural response to novelty, so the two methods give complementary evidence."]}],"review-33":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** using **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, which is the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration. In a syllable with a long VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than in a syllable with a short VOT of +10 milliseconds.","children":["Infants heard a syllable over and over until their sucking slowed down, and then the sound either changed or stayed the same."]},{"text":"The experiment compared **three conditions**. In **20D**, the two sounds differed by **20 milliseconds** of VOT, +20 and +40 milliseconds, and came from **different adult phonemic categories**. In **20S**, the sounds also differed by 20 milliseconds, either −20 and 0 or +60 and +80 milliseconds, but came from the **same category**. In **0**, the **control** condition, the same sound continued throughout.","children":[]},{"text":"In every condition, sucking rose above baseline and then declined as the sound became familiar. In 20D, sucking **jumped back up** after the change, to around 50 responses per minute. In 20S, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling, and in the control condition it kept declining to about 15 responses per minute.","children":["Infants responded to a change that **crossed an adult phoneme boundary** but not to an equally large change within a category.","The two changed conditions were matched in acoustic size, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary, and the control condition establishes what happens with repetition alone."]}],"review-34":[{"text":"**Why was Eimas's result a big deal?** Categorical perception was considered to be the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** that later linguistic processes could use. A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/.","children":["Finding this ability in infants suggested that the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds are really little**, and their other abilities are only beginning. Some **gross motor milestones** for that age, meaning large movements of the body, are manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while prone, which means lying face down. Their **color vision and general visual acuity**, the sharpness of vision, are still \"in progress.\" They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines.","children":["Basic perception in other areas is still developing, and four-month-olds cannot yet make speech sounds well."]},{"text":"This is **very, very early in development** for an apparently language-specific ability to emerge, so it **seemed like evidence for innateness**. Infants sort speech sounds into categories before they can produce them and while other skills are immature.","children":["Because speech categorization ran so far ahead of other abilities, it looked built in instead of learned through long experience."]}],"review-35":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** for speech categories is the decline in sensitivity to sound distinctions that are not used in the language a child hears. At around six months, **English-learning infants** show evidence of discriminating speech contrasts they have never heard before. Over the course of development, the ability to perceive these contrasts **declines**.","children":["**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts. The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two \"ta\"-like sounds, and the **Salish contrast** is a distinction between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language."]},{"text":"They measured the share of infants at each age who reached a **discrimination criterion**. At **6 to 8 months**, about 95 percent of English-learning infants discriminated the Hindi contrast and about 80 percent discriminated the Salish contrast. At **8 to 10 months** these fell to about 67 percent and 52 percent, and at **10 to 12 months** they fell to about 20 percent and 10 percent. Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants discriminated their own native contrast close to 100 percent of the time.","children":["This is **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**. Infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"**How is this related to categorical perception?** As infants form the categories of their native language, sounds that their language groups together become harder to tell apart.","children":["A nonnative distinction that falls within one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"review-36":[{"text":"The infant findings support **a very compelling narrative**. In it, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system**.","children":["These abilities are **present very early in development**, as the infant studies suggest."]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world,\"** able to hear **all contrasts**. That means they have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech, which is knowledge that applies to the speech sounds of every language and not one particular language. Children then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems** to the specific language in their environment.","children":["Calling infants \"citizens of the world\" captures the idea that a newborn is ready to learn any human language."]},{"text":"The **role of learning**, then, is to **select** among the many possible **innately specified phonetic contrasts**. Learning narrows down the **inventory**, which is the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive, to just those that are meaningful in the child's native language.","children":["Learning works by elimination, because it drops the contrasts the native language does not use and keeps the innate categories the language needs.","Infants start with a full set of possible contrasts, so the native language decides which innate categories survive."]}],"review-37":[{"text":"**The inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings. **Induction** means reasoning from particular examples to a general rule, and the rule here is the meaning of a word. A child hears a new word while watching a cat, and the word could mean many things.","children":["It might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, which are categories at different levels, or it might be **FELIX**, the name of this particular cat.","It might name a property such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**, an action such as **WALKING**, or a part such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**. Each candidate fits what the child sees, so nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends.","children":["Every one of these meanings fits the situation equally well."]},{"text":"This is a version of **Quine's “gavagai” problem**. In Quine's example, a linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by. “Gavagai” might mean rabbit, but it might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","children":["Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast, so something must narrow the possibilities.","**Joint attention**, **intentional cues** from the speaker and **default word learning strategies** do this narrowing."]}],"review-38":[{"text":"**How do children acquire word meanings?** One way is through **joint attention**, which is **sharing an attentional focus with another person**. Two people look at the same object, and each knows that the other is attending to it too.","children":["When a child and an adult attend to the same object, a word spoken by the adult is likely to be about that object."]},{"text":"**Gaze following emerges around 9 months of age**. **Gaze following** means looking where someone else is looking. When an adult looks over at an object, the infant follows the adult's eyes to that object.","children":["Gaze following lets infants join the adult's focus."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**. The infant grasps that the adult's attention is aimed at something specific. If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent.","children":["Joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space** for that reason, because it ties the word to what the speaker is attending to.","Understanding that others attend to particular things is a foundation for learning that words refer to what speakers are attending to."]}],"review-39":[{"text":"**Joint attention optimizes word learning**, according to **Baldwin and colleagues**. Two groups of infants heard a novel word while they played with an object. In the **joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby** labeled the object the baby was playing with. In the **no joint attention group**, a **voice from behind a screen** labeled the object the baby was playing with.","children":["In both groups the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","The groups differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"The result was that **18-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group**. Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough for learning.","children":["The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment, so the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker."]},{"text":"Infants needed evidence that a **speaker meant the word to refer to that object**. The social exchange around the word supported learning, so joint attention does more than place a word next to an object.","children":["A word and an object occurring together did not produce learning without that evidence."]}],"review-40":[{"text":"**How do children narrow the hypothesis space for new words?** One way is through **intentional cues from the speaker**, such as **eye gaze direction**. An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to.","children":["When a speaker looks toward one toy, the direction of the eyes marks which object the speaker is attending to."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to **16 to 17-month-olds** and **18 to 19-month-olds** in two conditions. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the **toy that the infant was focused on** while saying the new word. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a **toy in a bucket**, a different toy from the infant's, while saying the new word.","children":["In follow-in labeling the infant and the adult look at the same toy, so either cue points to the right object.","In discrepant labeling the infant's focus and the speaker's focus point to different toys."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition separates the infant's focus from the speaker's focus. Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy. Infants who follow the speaker's gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket.","children":["Comparing the two conditions tests whether infants treat the **speaker's gaze as evidence** of what the word means."]}],"review-41":[{"text":"Intentional cues from the speaker also include **affective and behavioral cues**. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions, and **behavioral cues** are the speaker's actions. An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was looking for.","children":["An action, such as reaching for something, can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that **infants used adults' facial expression to identify the intended referent of a novel word**. The adult's emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one the adult meant. Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult's expression picked out.","children":["These cues add to gaze as evidence of what the speaker means."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken, and the speaker's reaction can **single out one of them**. A satisfied expression signals that the speaker found the wanted object. A disappointed expression signals that an object is not the one the speaker meant.","children":["Word learning involves understanding the goal behind the interaction for that reason."]}],"review-42":[{"text":"Children also bring **word learning strategies** to the task. These are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space for new words**, so children have a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** is one example. It leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**, not one of its parts, its color, its texture or an action it is doing.","children":["A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"This assumption **rules out many unlikely meanings at once**, so it speeds learning. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]}],"review-43":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is another word learning strategy. **As a default, objects can have only one name**. The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","children":["Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, which is roughly between 18 and 24 months of age."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view. When the child hears a new word, the child takes it to mean the parrot, because the dog already has a name.","children":["The child does not use any gaze or emotional cue to make this choice.","The strategy uses the child's existing vocabulary to narrow the possibilities."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works together with the **whole object assumption**. The whole object assumption says that a new word names an object, and mutual exclusivity says that it names an object with no name yet.","children":["Mutual exclusivity is a default, so later learning can override it when an object turns out to have more than one name."]}],"review-44":[{"text":"**Second-language learning**, also called **L2 learning**, works as a **model system** for sensitive period research. A model system is a common, easily studied situation that is used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly. **Lenneberg (1967)** developed an influential theory that the sensitive period had to do with the development of **cerebral dominance**, which is also described as developmental changes in **language laterality**.","children":["**Cerebral dominance** means that one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language. **Language laterality** is how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both.","On this theory, the window for learning language closes once language becomes fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory. The first was a **rough alignment** of the literature on **brain development** with new insights about **language milestones**. The second was a review of the literature on **resilience after brain injury or surgery**.","children":["Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off, and no measurements were available from age 2 to 18. Lenneberg tied the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","Children who lost the left hemisphere often went on to develop language, while adults did not regain it."]},{"text":"The theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, which means its stage of physical development. A young, less specialized brain can move language to other tissue, but a mature, specialized brain has fewer ways to do that, so language learning becomes harder.","children":[]}],"review-45":[{"text":"Much of what is known about **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** comes from research on **idiosyncratic cases** or relatively **rare phenomena**. Idiosyncratic means particular to one individual. The evidence includes children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere, and Genie, who was cut off from language until age 13.","children":["Every one of these cases carries other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse, so it is hard to tell which factor caused the outcome."]},{"text":"In contrast, **many people learn second languages at different times in their lives**, often with **somewhat limited success**. Second-language learners are common, so researchers can study **large groups** instead of single rare cases. They begin at every age from early childhood to late adulthood, and their final success can be measured.","children":["Comparing early starters with late starters tells researchers how the age of first exposure relates to final skill."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** have also been used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive or critical period**. If later starters reliably end up at lower levels, then the age where the decline appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","children":["The **first language complicates** the comparison, because every L2 learner already knows a language and that knowledge shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"review-46":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** studied **critical period effects in second-language learning**, and their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language. They started from **Lenneberg (1967)**, who hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period** running from early infancy until puberty. In its basic form, that hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition, so it was important to find out whether it extends to second-language acquisition too.","children":["If it does extend, young children should be better second-language learners than adults and should reach higher final levels of proficiency."]},{"text":"They tested that prediction by comparing the English proficiency of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States **between the ages of 3 and 39**. By the time of testing, these people had lived in the United States for between 3 and 26 years. They were tested on a wide variety of structures of English grammar with a **grammaticality judgment task**.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals. Test performance was **linearly related to age of arrival up to puberty**, so each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score. After puberty, performance was **low but highly variable and unrelated to age of arrival**.","children":["The age effect was not an accidental result of differences in amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The effect appeared on **every grammatical structure tested**, although the structures varied a lot in how well later learners mastered them. The authors concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**."]}],"review-47":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features. There is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**, where AOA is age of arrival and earlier arrival goes with higher scores. **Early learners, up to about age 8, are often near-perfect**, which means **native-like**, because their scores sit close to those of native English speakers. Late arrivals have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**, because among adults age of arrival barely predicts the score.","children":[]},{"text":"The first two features are about learners who arrived as children. The youngest arrivals reach native-like grammar, so near-perfect scores are the clearest sign of an early advantage. Arrivals between about 8 and 15 reach steadily lower levels, so the **decline begins well before puberty**.","children":["Adult arrivals scored lower still, with wide differences between individuals."]},{"text":"The phrase **“nearly random distribution”** has a specific meaning here. It means that among late arrivals, age of arrival and score are only weakly related.","children":["Late arrivals scored **well above chance**, but individual scores varied enormously. Two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]}],"review-48":[{"text":"There are **two versions of the “critical period” hypothesis**, and both start from the same claim that early in life humans have a **superior capacity for acquiring languages**. The **exercise hypothesis** says that if the capacity is not exercised during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation. If the capacity is exercised, however, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life.","children":["On the exercise hypothesis, learning a first language early keeps the ability alive, so an adult who learned an L1 normally should still learn an L2 as well as a child does."]},{"text":"The **maturational state hypothesis** says that the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used. On this version, even someone who learned an L1 normally loses the superior capacity while growing up, so that person will learn an L2 less well as an adult.","children":[]},{"text":"**Which of these do you think JN89 see as more consistent with their data?** **Johnson and Newport (1989)** see the **maturational state hypothesis** as the better fit, and it is the idea named in the title of their paper. Every one of their participants had a normal first language, yet the later starters still learned the L2 less well.","children":["The exercise hypothesis predicts that these adults would learn as well as children, because they had already exercised the capacity."]},{"text":"**Does this change our interpretation of the L1 critical period data?** The L1 data cannot choose between the two versions, because in cases like Genie the capacity was never exercised, and both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases. Only second-language data can tell the two versions apart, because only L2 learners have already exercised the capacity before learning another language.","children":[]}],"review-49":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds. Participants viewed a grid containing pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","children":["The display included a **beaker**, which was the target, a **beetle**, which shares its beginning with beaker, a **speaker**, which rhymes with beaker, and a **carriage**, which is unrelated."]},{"text":"The measure was the probability of fixating each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the target word began. Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, because both fit the first sounds of the word. Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 at around 500 milliseconds and then fell once the sounds that distinguish the words arrived. Looks to the beaker kept rising and approached 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","children":["Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, with a peak near 0.1, because its overlap comes at the end of the word. Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The cohort effect is this **early competition** from words that share a beginning with the target. Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds, and they drop out when the input stops matching them.","children":["Eye movements track this rise and fall moment by moment, even when listeners end up choosing correctly."]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** examined the cohort effect with **cross-language competitors**. A cross-language competitor is an object whose name in the other language shares its beginning with the spoken target word. They hypothesized that if **L1 words are always activated**, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is “speaker” in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches. If Russian is active while the participant listens to English, the matches should attract looks."]},{"text":"For English targets, each target was paired with an English competitor and a Russian competitor. **Speaker** was paired with spear and spichki (matches), boot with book and buben (tambourine), and shark with shovel and sharik (balloon). Chair was paired with chess set and cherepaha (turtle), marker with marbles and marka (stamp), and barbed wire with bark and barhat (velvet). Plug was paired with plum and platye (dress), gun with gum and gaika (nut), and lock with lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish).","children":["For Russian targets the pairing was reversed. Russian spichki was paired with English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles), marka with marker and marlya (cheese cloth), and platye with plum and plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The connection between a target and its cross-language competitor is **only in sound**, because speakers and matches share no meaning. Both competitors overlap with the target in sound, but only the overlap of the Russian competitor depends on knowing Russian.","children":["Looks to the matches during an English task are therefore a direct measure of the activation of spichki."]}],"review-51":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** first had Russian-English bilinguals follow instructions in English, which tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**. The measure was the percent of trials on which participants looked at each competitor. When no competitor was present, participants looked at a control filler object on only **7%** of trials.","children":["When only the **between-language competitor** was present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials. When only the **within-language competitor** was present, participants also looked at it on 18% of trials."]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to **both within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent**. The Russian name of an object pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did.","children":["When both competitors were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more, on 19% of trials. They **still looked at the between-language competitor a lot**, on 13% of trials, which is about twice as often as in the control condition."]},{"text":"These results mean that **the first language stays active during second-language listening**. Bilinguals keep their L1 active while they process their L2.","children":["They still select the correct object, but L1 words compete along the way."]}],"review-52":[{"text":"The same bilinguals in **Marian and Spivey (2003)** then did the experiment in Russian, which tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**. When only the between-language competitor was present, which was an object with a similar English name, participants looked at it on **8%** of trials. With no competitor, looks to the control filler happened on **5%** of trials.","children":["When only the within-language competitor was present, participants looked at it on 14% of trials. When both were present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 8% of trials and at the within-language competitor on 13%."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do this experiment in Russian. The small numerical difference between the between-language and control conditions, 8% compared with 5%, is **not statistically significant**. A difference that is not statistically significant is within the range of chance variation.","children":["The within-language competitor still produced a reliable cohort effect in Russian."]},{"text":"The pattern across the two experiments is **asymmetric**. Russian words became active during English tasks, but English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","children":["The asymmetry fits the idea that the first language is more strongly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"review-53":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation is the idea that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it. The model has an **L1 lexicon**, which is the first-language vocabulary, an **L2 lexicon**, which is the second-language vocabulary, and a store of **concepts**, which holds meanings.","children":["**Lexical links** connect the L1 and L2 words directly. **Conceptual links** connect each lexicon to the concepts."]},{"text":"The L1 lexicon is assumed to be **larger** and **more strongly connected to relevant concepts**. The conceptual link between L1 and the concepts is strong, while the conceptual link between L2 and the concepts is weak.","children":["First-language words therefore have strong links to their meanings, and second-language words have weaker links to their meanings."]},{"text":"The L2 lexicon is assumed to have **strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon**. The lexical link from L2 to L1 is strong and the lexical link from L1 to L2 is weak, so **activation can spread more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word** than the other way. This occurs because this direction is emphasized in learning.","children":["When people learn a second language, they usually learn each new word by connecting it to its L1 translation. The link from L2 words directly to concepts stays weaker, at least early in learning, and it can strengthen as proficiency grows."]}],"review-55":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**. A balanced bilingual is a person who is about equally skilled in both languages. One might expect a balanced bilingual to work like “two monolinguals in one,” with each language running separately, but the model predicts the opposite.","children":["The reason is that in a balanced bilingual the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**. Every concept strongly activates its word in both languages, so both vocabularies become active whenever either one is used."]},{"text":"Because both words are active, **competitive dynamics and inhibition will be engaged all the time**. Competitive dynamics means that the words of the two languages constantly compete for selection. **Inhibition** is the suppression of the word from the language that is not being used.","children":["The bilingual must constantly select the right language and suppress the other."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on the advantages of bilinguals in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks. Cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","children":["If bilinguals constantly practice suppressing one language, they might become better at controlling attention in general, including in tasks that have nothing to do with language."]}]}};
